  {"a":"sylas","b":"aatrox","win":["Aatrox","Aatrox","Aatrox","Aatrox","Sylas","Aatrox","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"The moment E comes online you can hook onto Aatrox and W him mid-rotation — the first real all-in threat he has to respect."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Aatrox's Q knockups extend trades he should be losing, and Deathbringer Stance heals off your max health — buy Grievous Wounds early and leave before his third swing."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Steal World Ender and pop it immediately — you get his R's combat-stat boost plus your own W heal, which is the one window you genuinely out-sustain him."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Aatrox's Black Cleaver or Eclipse spike consolidates his Q damage and passive drain, making extended brawls lethal — trade short or disengage when he activates R."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook E2 after Umbral Dash — it is down 5-9s and he cannot dash out","Use W at low HP in trades to maximize the missing-health heal and negate his passive drain","Steal World Ender at 6 and fight immediately — the window is the whole game"],"foe":["Land Q sweetspot knockups to extend trades past your W healing","Activate World Ender when you are low and force a drain-vs-drain brawl on his terms","Stall past your 9s W cooldown while Deathbringer Stance heals off you"]},"early":"At level 1 neither of you has a reliable engage — Aatrox's Q without E is a telegraphed ground-slam, and you only have Q or W. Farm safely and chip with Q's delayed explosion on his feet; do not walk up for extended autos because his passive healing on hit races your level-1 W output. The moment you hit level 2 and E is available, you have the first real window: dash in with E1 to absorb his Q burst on the shield, W him, then hook the E2 chain if he tries to walk away — one clean rotation at this level genuinely threatens his HP bar before his kit fully assembles.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 belong to Aatrox and you need to accept that plainly. His full Q kit means every extended trade ends with a knockup that refreshes his passive healing and gives him a free W chain-pull to hold you in the danger zone. Do not brawl past two spell rotations — trade your Q into him, weave a passive auto, W if he landed damage, then disengage before the third Q sweetspot catches you. Save E2 to escape the W pull, not to engage. The moment you hit 6 everything changes: steal World Ender before he can use it himself, activate it on top of him, and burst with Q-passive-W while your drained health scales the heal to maximum. That is your kill window; do not wait.","late":"After first items Aatrox reclaims the matchup because his Black Cleaver shred stacks through your W healing and World Ender on a short cooldown means he can force a drain fight on demand. Respect World Ender's indicator every time it is up — disengage with E1 dash the moment the red wings appear, let it expire, then re-engage. In teamfights your stolen R is still valuable but Aatrox's second item fully outpaces your AP bruiser scaling; play for objectives off the back of your level 6 lead rather than expecting to win a 1v1 at 30 minutes.","whys":["Level 1 is neutral for both — Aatrox's Q without the sweetspot follow-up barely threatens and your E is not yet online; farm with Q range and do not force a trade.","E coming online lets you E1-W-E2 chain him before he stabilizes; his passive hasn't stacked enough healing to race your W output in a short burst.","Full Q access means Aatrox's third-hit sweetspot knockup extends every fight past the point your W CD can save you — trade short and disengage after one rotation.","Levels 4-5 cement Aatrox's extended-trade dominance: Deathbringer Stance heals off your max health and Umbral Dash returns every 6s, so he out-drains your 9s W — fight one rotation, then leave.","Stealing World Ender gives you his R's combat stats on top of your own W's missing-health heal; this is the only window your sustain mathematically beats his in a prolonged fight.","Black Cleaver shred compounds through your W healing and Aatrox's R cooldown drops — he can force repeated drain fights, and you cannot sustain more than one exchange safely.","At 2+ items you are the teamfight flex — a stolen ultimate plus Riftmaker sustain beats what Aatrox brings to a 5v5, so take fights near objectives, never in his sidelane."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"akali","win":["Skill","Sylas","Akali","Skill","Skill","Akali","Akali"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online and you can hook her into a W all-in — the burst-heal means a straight trade at this level already favours you if Twilight Shroud is down."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit: E2 chain into Q slow, W lunge for the missing-HP burst, passive Petricite autos weaved in — one full rotation takes a significant chunk and heals you through her counter-trade."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Akali's R gives you two dashes and an execute — she no longer has an escape tool and you can all-in without fear of being outrun."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Akali's Shadowflame and Lich Bane let her one-rotation squishy targets; she outscales your sustained brawling and the 1v1 flips hard in her favour."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook her out of Twilight Shroud with E2 the instant she exits, then W for the missing-HP burst while she has no escape","Force extended fights where your W heal and passive Petricite autos grind down her energy-gated burst","Steal her R at 6 so she loses both her gap-close and her execute — then run her down"],"foe":["Pop Twilight Shroud before you land E2 so your chain passes through air and you waste both E charges","Burn your E to get on her, then re-enter Shroud and kite until your W is on cooldown before committing back","Stack items and outscale you — her two-item burst rotation deletes you faster than your W can heal"]},"early":"Level 1 you have Q and nothing else, so do not try to hook her — she has Five Point Strike poke and Twilight Shroud is already available to dodge your follow-up if she reads the aggression. Farm with Q range and count her Shroud's 20s cooldown. Level 2 the math shifts: the moment E is up and her Shroud is down, E1 dash in to absorb her counter-poke with the shield, then fire E2 to chain her, Q slow to confirm, and W lunge for the missing-HP burst. If she burned Shroud entering the wave, that is your window — take it every time. Level 3 consolidates the pattern: full kit with Petricite Burst autos woven between Q and W means one full rotation costs her a real chunk while your W heals back most of her counter-damage. Never commit E2 while Shroud is available; she becomes untargetable mid-flight and you hand her a free trade.","mid":"Levels 4-5 Akali starts to spike as she approaches 6 — she will play more aggressively and look to use Shroud as an engage tool rather than just an escape. Respect the Shroud-into-E dash pattern: if she enters Shroud toward you, hold your E and wait for her to materialise before firing the chain. Do not auto-attack into Shroud trying to proc passive; you give free damage. Bank the small HP leads from early trades and call for jungle pressure when she overextends on the wave. At 6 you hit the decisive window: steal her R with Hijack and she loses both halves of her mobility — no dash in, no execute out. This is the moment to force the all-in, ideally under your tower where she cannot disengage. Her R on your cooldown also makes you a roam threat with two dashes.","late":"Two items Akali flips this lane: Shadowflame into Lich Bane lets her one-shot you in the time it takes your W to land, and her reformed Shroud means she picks the fight's terms again. Your goal is to have banked enough kill and objective leads before this point that her scaling is irrelevant to your team's position. In teamfights, steal her R on cooldown and use it as a pick tool — you become the assassin. Never 1v1 her when both players are full items; your W heal cannot keep pace with her burst-then-reset pattern at that stage.","whys":["You only have Q at level 1 and she already has Twilight Shroud to dodge any hook follow-up — trade at Q range only and do not burn E into a Shroud she can pop reactively.","E is online and her Shroud has a cooldown — hook her the moment she exits, W for the missing-HP burst, and your heal beats her energy-gated counter-trade.","Both kits are live but the lane is genuinely contested: she can Shroud your E2 chain and force skill-checks, while you can full-combo her if she missteps — neither player freely wins the trade.","Akali approaches her level-6 spike; her burst opens with the Assassin's Mark empowered auto, so step back on that wind-up and hold E until she materialises out of Shroud.","Hijack her R and she loses both dash-in and execute — the 1v1 all-in now belongs to you, and her Shroud is the only tool left between her and a kill.","You hold a Hijacked R and Riftmaker sustain, but the lane is still contested skill-wise — pick fights where her Shroud is on cooldown and your W heal tips the extended trade your way.","Shadowflame and Lich Bane let Akali one-rotation you before your W lands — she outscales your brawling hard; convert mid-game leads into objectives rather than 1v1 duels."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"akshan","win":["Skill","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Akshan's Comeuppance gives you a ranged execute to use against him — but his R cooldown is long, so the value window is narrow; all-in the moment he fires it."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Omnivamp stacks up through the extended trades your W sustain enables, letting you survive the all-in threshold where Akshan's W burst previously ended the fight."},{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Akshan's E grapple swing lets him kite your E hook indefinitely — if he keeps terrain between you, his W poke bleeds you out before you touch him."},{"when":"Late","text":"Once you complete Riftmaker and Zhonya's you can dive Akshan under tower and survive the follow-up burst with W healing, converting sustained fights he cannot kite forever."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him mid-swing with E2 when his grapple anchor commits him to a fixed arc and he cannot dodge","Force W trades at low HP where Kingslayer's missing-health scaling and self-heal are maximised","Steal Comeuppance at 6 and spend it immediately while he has no R to disengage"],"foe":["Maintain E grapple swing mobility around pillars and walls so your E chain never connects","Poke with Avengerang and his Dirty Fighting passive to chip you below your W-heal breakeven point","Swing away the instant your E fires — Heroic Swing is back every 12-18s and your pull grabs air"]},"early":"You have no ranged answer to Akshan's Avengerang at level 1, and your Q Chain Lash requires walking into his auto range to get the crossing explosion — he just steps back. Sit at max Q tip range, last-hit safely, and do not blow your E trying to close before you have W to back up the all-in. His level 1 is also weak in a sustained brawl, so the lane is genuinely even here. At level 2 he gains Heroic Swing and the dynamic shifts hard against you: he can now swing around terrain to make your E hook whiff, and Going Rogue's camouflage baits you into pushing past safety. Respect the swing arc — if there is a pillar he is orbiting, do not commit your Abduct chain, because he redirects and the pull drags you into nothing. Take W at 2 and bank the heal for his three-hit passive shield.","mid":"Akshan controls the level 4-5 window almost completely because Heroic Swing keeps him just outside E range while Avengerang and his passive autos tick your HP down. Your W heal helps but it requires getting to him, and he denies that. The plan is to fish for an E hook while his swing is on its 12-18s cooldown, or the moment a swing ends inside your range. There is no stun on your chain — the pull is the lockdown, so W immediately after it lands. Level 6 is your inflection point: Hijack gives you Comeuppance, a ranged execute he cannot outrun. The moment he burns his own R, he has no burst safety net — E hook, pull him in, W for the heal spike, and spend the stolen R on the kill. Do not waste his R as poke; you need it for the kill window.","late":"With Riftmaker completed you can absorb enough poke to force sustained fights, and Akshan's W burst damage does not outpace your W Kingslayer healing once the Omnivamp is stacking through extended trades. His camouflage passive and revive make him slippery in skirmishes — prioritise killing him before he picks up a Revive proc by taking out his marked teammate first in teamfights, or isolating him top where no one is marked. In full late game your stolen R options improve dramatically if the enemy team has better ultimates than Akshan's Comeuppance — you will often ignore hijacking him entirely and prey on a backline ultimate. But in a pure 1v1, your W sustain and AP scaling win any prolonged melee fight he cannot fully disengage from.","whys":["Neither of you has a reliable trade tool at level 1 — Akshan's W passive is not yet online and your Q tip poke matches his auto range. Farm even and avoid burning flash.","Akshan's E grapple comes online and lets him permanently orbit terrain to dodge your E hook. His W camouflage reset makes aggressive trades on your part cost more than the damage is worth.","Full kit Akshan with Heroic Swing mobility and Avengerang poke bleeds you faster than your W heal replenishes. Your E hook is telegraphed; he swings away every time.","His Heroic Swing mastery peaks here — he controls spacing completely, keeping your chain range irrelevant while Avengerang whittles your HP. You need an error from him to punish.","Akshan still owns the spacing war, but his own R Comeuppance is now at risk. He must be cautious about firing it or you steal it immediately and turn his execute into yours.","Riftmaker's Omnivamp is online but not yet enough to overcome his burst-poke cycle at range. He kites between W procs and keeps the edge.","Your Riftmaker plus Zhonya's makes any sustained fight winnable — he cannot burst you down before W heals you back, and your stolen R (or a superior ally R) closes out fights he cannot fully kite."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"ambessa","win":["Sylas","Skill","Ambessa","Ambessa","Skill","Ambessa","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your E hook comes online — if you land it on an unshielded Ambessa you can chain W for a quick burst before she has her full dash suite."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Repudiation's shield and Lacerate's dash let her weave your Q and punish your E hook approach, making extended trades increasingly dangerous."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Ambessa's R gives you her engage tool and burst pattern — use it aggressively while she is without it to force a kill or burn Ignite."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ambessa completes her first damage item and Repudiation's empowered slam becomes lethal on a half-health Sylas — never poke into her brace after 12 minutes."}],"wants":{"you":["Land E hook when Ambessa has her dashes on cooldown so she cannot weave out of your W lunge","Steal her R at 6 and immediately all-in while she sits without her engage tool","Abuse W heal in short trades to stay healthy enough that her missing-health W burst is weakened"],"foe":["Dash through your Q chains to dodge the slow and reset your E hook angle","Bait your poke into a braced Repudiation so her slam lands empowered","Chain her CC into your W burst to kill you before Hijack can swing the fight"]},"early":"At level 1 you only have Q or W and Ambessa can walk through your Q chains with her dashes — hold your position, last-hit safely, and do not throw the hook. Level 2 is your first real window: land E and immediately W before she can dash out, then back off. This trade only works if both her dashes are down; if she has charges ready she will weave your W lunge and punish the whiff. Use Q to slow her approach and discourage extended trades rather than to set up all-ins you cannot follow through.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 belong to Ambessa. Repudiation shields her and slams back harder for every point of damage you put into the brace, and Drakehound's Step weaves a dash into each cast, so she slips your Q slow and repositions around your E hook. Respect her all-in range, keep the W heal to answer her burst rather than to open one, and step up only while Lacerate sits on its 9-13s cooldown. Level 6 flips the read: stealing Public Execution hands you a 130s suppress she must then fight without — that is your all-in window, use it before she reclaims tempo.","late":"At first item Repudiation's slam peaks and your hook is her invitation to delete you below half HP — play around your 9s W cooldown and never poke her brace while you are low. At two-plus items Sylas's AP and Riftmaker sustain start to pay off: Ambessa does not hyper-scale into an untouchable late game, and you can win the side-lane duel if you open with a stolen R. The matchup stays close; your edge is Hijack recycling her 130s ultimate on an 80/55/30s clock, hers is punishing every missed hook before your W undoes it.","whys":["You only have Q or W and Ambessa can already weave your single ability — farm safely and do not burn E into her with no follow-up.","Your E hook comes online but so does her full dash chain; a clean hook into W is available if you catch her with dashes down, making the level a genuine coin-flip.","Ambessa has full kit access and Repudiation punishes every extended trade — damage into her brace empowers the slam, so wait the shield out first.","Her damage and mobility compound across levels 4 and 5; Repudiation's shield absorbs your hook burst, Lacerate dashes through your W lunge, and she wins the exchange.","Stealing Ambessa's R gives you her engage pattern while she sits without it — all-in immediately and force a kill or summoner before she gets it back.","Ambessa's first item makes Repudiation's slam lethal; she dictates the fight with Lacerate's 9-13s dash, and your hook timing must be perfect to avoid handing her the kill.","Your AP and Riftmaker sustain consolidate and Ambessa does not out-scale into an unreachable late-game fighter — you can win the duel if you land E and open with a stolen R."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"aurora","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Aurora","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — Abscond's dash into the Abduct pull lets you all-in before Aurora can blink away, and Across the Veil is a 22s cooldown at rank 1."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full kit with W sustain — chain Q slow into E hook, land W, and passive-auto cleave to out-trade her poke pattern in extended melee range."},{"when":"Lvl 6 (Aurora)","text":"Between Worlds is Aurora's defining spike: the shockwave slows your approach and the zone she leaves lets her teleport across it, undoing the all-in you just committed."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker spike — your W heal plus Riftmaker omnivamp lets you absorb Aurora's poke budget and force all-ins she cannot sustain past two rotations."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook her with E Abscond-recast when her W blink is on cooldown — that is your only window to stick","Trade in melee range where your W Kingslayer heal and passive Petricite Burst autos out-sustain her Q poke","Steal Between Worlds at 6 and hold it defensively — the slow zone cuts her escape line, not just yours"],"foe":["Blink with Across the Veil as your E fires, so the pull grabs air and you burn both charges","Weave Q poke and passive drain from range while keeping her W hop off cooldown so she always has an escape","Zone you away from her W exit point and punish your E cooldown with a full Q-passive-E burst combo"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q or W but not both — Aurora is content to Q-E poke you from range and you cannot safely dash in yet. Take Q, last-hit safely, and deny her passive drain procs by stepping off the minion line when she raises her Q. The lane is hers to poke in this window but her damage cannot force you out before level 2 unless you bleed mana chasing her.","mid":"Level 2 changes everything: the moment E unlocks, you have a commit tool she cannot simply walk away from. The key read is Across the Veil — 22s at rank 1 and still 18s maxed, so bait it with a fake step-up, then E-dash and recast to pull yourself onto her. If the hook lands with her blink down, walk W into her face and cleave passive autos; she has no escape and your Kingslayer heal absorbs her retaliation. Levels 3-5 you run this loop on repeat: Q to slow her, E to close, W to burst and heal, passive autos in the extended trade. She will drift to max Q range between your rotations — keep the wave even or slightly pushed so she cannot freeze safely.","late":"At level 6 Between Worlds becomes a genuine threat — do NOT E into her while she holds it, because the shockwave slows you and the zone she leaves lets her teleport clean across the fight and reset it. Bluff the engage to bait the cast, let the zone expire, then E hook and kill her during its 100-140s cooldown. Stealing it with Hijack hands you the same slow zone to cut her exit line. Post-Riftmaker you win any all-in that starts in melee range — the W heal plus omnivamp outheals her poke budget, and Grievous Wounds shuts off Spirit Abjuration's drain. At 2-plus items you remain the stronger side-laner; she is a teamfight carry, but your stolen R comes back on 30s and hers does not, so take every fight the moment she spends it.","whys":["Aurora has Q poke but no W blink at level 1 — she can stack passive drain but cannot escape a level-2 gank or all-in, so you farm safely and set up the level-2 spike without wasting mana.","E Abscond-recast online: bait her 22s Across the Veil with a fake step-up, then dash and recast to pull yourself onto her before she can blink — one clean hook at level 2 sets the tone for the lane.","Full kit means every E hook leads into a Q slow and W Kingslayer burst-heal; she can no longer walk out of your trades, and passive Petricite autos in the extended fight compound the lead.","Her 18-22s blink is your clock — re-engage the instant it expires, and buy Grievous Wounds so Spirit Abjuration stops refunding the poke damage you just traded through.","Between Worlds is her defining spike: the leap shockwave slows you and the zone it leaves lets her teleport across it, so she resets the fight you thought you had won — bait the cast, then all-in during its 100-140s cooldown.","Riftmaker spike: your W Kingslayer plus Riftmaker omnivamp means every trade refills more HP than her Q poke removes — force the all-in on her W cooldown and she has no attrition plan left.","Two items in you are the stronger skirmisher and side-laner; she scales into a teamfight zone-threat but a Hijacked Between Worlds on 30s beats hers on 100s, so keep rotating and convert your lane advantage into map pressure and objectives."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"camille","win":["Skill","Camille","Camille","Skill","Sylas","Camille","Camille"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Precision Protocol's true damage and the Tactical Sweep heal come online — she forces the extended trade before your W heal and chain pull can answer cleanly."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals The Hextech Ultimatum on an 80s clock against her 140s: cage her inside her own R and she has no isolation tool left."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Camille's Trinity Force spike hits before your Riftmaker is complete — she true-damages through your W heal and shreds you in the short window between items."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your stolen R and AP burst let you carry teamfights, but Camille's own item scaling and true damage mean the 1v1 is hers to win if she catches you alone."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E2 only when Hookshot is on cooldown — committing into her wall range hands her a free stun","Dash E1 through the outer half of Tactical Sweep so she gets no heal, then counter with Q-W","Bank the stolen R until you can cage her away from her team for a decisive solo kill"],"foe":["Hookshot off a wall to knock you up and lock in the extended trade her Q true damage wins","Force your E charges out early with Q poke so you have no stun or shield when she dives","Out-scale into Trinity Force and activate The Hextech Ultimatum to eliminate you in a clean 1v1"]},"early":"Level 1 is a wash — you have Q to poke CS steps but she matches it and your kit has no real threat yet; farm quietly and do not burn E chasing. At level 2 things get genuinely difficult: Precision Protocol's true damage and the Tactical Sweep heal mean short trades consistently favour her, and her second Q hits hardest when she delays it, so step out on the wind-up. By level 3 you have the full hook, but she can Hookshot off a wall to cancel your approach before E2 lands — respect terrain and trade only inside its 12-16s gap. Your Q slow and Petricite autos do real damage in extended exchanges, but buy Grievous Wounds or her W cone simply refunds them.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are a genuine skill contest: you can short-trade E1 into Q-W and walk out, but a Hookshot knockup turns it into the extended fight her Q true damage and W heal win. Do not trade with both E charges spent — no dash means the outer cone lands and heals her, and you have no lockdown to answer the auto-reset. Level 6 is your real window: her R is 140s and yours is 80s, so steal The Hextech Ultimatum and cage HER — she loses her isolation tool while you chain Q slow, Petricite autos and W burst before she can disengage. Do not R a Camille under her own tower or at full HP; the cage is your prison, not a gift.","late":"Two items in, you are a genuine teamfight threat — stolen R from the most dangerous enemy, Riftmaker sustain, and E2 engage on an out-of-position carry are how you win games from this position. But do not 1v1 a Trinity Force Camille late: Precision Protocol's true damage ignores every resistance you bought, and her passive shields against whichever type you deal. Convert the Hijack advantage into objectives and pressure on her team, not a top-lane rematch she is favoured to win again.","whys":["Neither kit has a meaningful level-1 threat — farm to the wave tip with Q and do not waste E on a champion who has no cooldowns to punish yet.","Precision Protocol's true damage comes online and Tactical Sweep heals through your return trade — the empowered-attack wind-up is your cue to step out, not the cast.","Hookshot into a wall cancels your E2 approach angle and knocks you up into the extended trade her Q true damage wins — your chain pulls, it does not lock her down.","Neither side has a dominant window — trade inside Hookshot's 12-16s gap, buy Grievous Wounds so Tactical Sweep stops refunding her, and respect her passive shield.","Hijack gives you The Hextech Ultimatum: cage her away from her team and she has no isolation tool, no escape, and your Q-W-passive burst takes the kill.","Trinity Force completes before your Riftmaker and the true-damage math tilts against your healing — play for objectives and teamfights rather than forcing the 1v1 again.","At full build your stolen R and AP burst make you a teamfight carrier, but the 1v1 still favours Camille — pick fights on your terms with her team, not hers."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"cassiopeia","win":["Skill","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Skill","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your E comes online at 13s — the E2 hook into W is your first real threat, but Miasma grounds the dash and negates it."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack grants you Petrifying Gaze — facing the right direction you face-stun her and chain the full W-Q combo for a kill."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's burn stacks inside every W lunge and passive chain — your first clean extended trade window, but only when Miasma is on cooldown."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-build Twin Fang loops outdamage your W healing and heal her while they do it; never solo dive without Grievous Wounds and her R down."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Miasma (W) then immediately E2 hook onto her while she cannot recast it to re-Ground you","Get into melee range and chain Q-W-passive autos — your W heal erases the poke deficit in one extended trade","Steal Petrifying Gaze at 6 and use it facing her to face-stun and chain the kill"],"foe":["Land Miasma to Ground you before every E2 so your hook approach is completely disabled","Cycle Twin Fang on your poison stacks — each cast bleeds you and heals her back at the same time","Survive lane with farm advantage and outscale with Twin Fang DPS once she has Seraph's and Shadowflame"]},"early":"Level 1 you have no safe engage — Q range barely reaches her and she can immediately answer every step-up with Noxious Blast into Twin Fang chains. Walk up only to last-hit and accept losing some CS to her Q poke rather than burning E1 for a chase she simply kites away from. The real danger is Miasma: she will drop it in your path the moment you look to step up at level 2, Grounding your E dash entirely and leaving you stranded in melee range with no escape. Learn her Miasma cooldown — roughly 24 seconds base — and only commit to the E2 hook in the window immediately after it fades.","mid":"The 54% win rate comes from your level 6 spike, not lane dominance — Cassiopeia beats you in levels 3-5 with poke that chips you below the W heal threshold, and Twin Fang refunds it to her while she does it. Play patient, stack Petricite charges on safe farm, and step up only in Miasma's 16-24s gap. At 6, Hijack gives you Petrifying Gaze on an 80s clock: E2 hook her, cast it into her face so the stun lands rather than the slow, and the Q-W-passive burst finishes it. That is your lane-winning window — but a ready Miasma still grounds the E2 and sends you back, so check it every time.","late":"Once Cassiopeia completes Seraph's Embrace into Shadowflame her Twin Fang cycles outrun your W healing and top her up at the same time; without Grievous Wounds the sustained fight is unwinnable. Your path is converting the level 6 kill lead into dragon or tower and ending before 30 minutes. If the game goes long, play teamfight angles — E2 onto her backline, steal Petrifying Gaze to peel, and never 1v1 her at full build with her 80s R up.","whys":["She starts Noxious Blast and Twin Fang at level 1 and you have no dash yet — last-hit at Q range and bank the HP lead for when E comes online.","Her Miasma Grounds your E dash the instant you step forward — she erases your only engage tool and pokes freely all level 2.","Full kit does not help you if Miasma covers your hook angle — she wins every level 3 skirmish you initiate without Miasma on cooldown.","Twin Fang hits poisoned targets harder and heals her off the damage — buy Grievous Wounds or she simply wins the levels 4-5 attrition war.","Hijack grants Petrifying Gaze — land the face-stun into Q-W-passive burst and the kill is yours, but she can still Miasma the approach to nullify it.","Riftmaker burn stacks in your W and passive autos, but only when you reach her; Miasma still counters your first-item all-in pattern if it is up.","Full-build Cassiopeia Twin Fang loops out-DPS your W healing and Seraph's keeps her sustained — avoid the extended 1v1 unless her R is confirmed on cooldown."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"chogath","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Cho'Gath","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"E comes online at level 2 — hook-into-W all-in can chunk Cho'Gath before his Rupture zones you out, and by level 3 your full chain outpaces his poke in any extended trade."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Feast is a double win: you gain a true-damage execute no resistance stops, and Cho'Gath cannot grow while his own 80s copy sits on cooldown behind your 80s one."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Cho'Gath's Rupture knockup window is your early ticking clock — first Riftmaker cements your brawl before his tank itemization makes extended trades irrelevant."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ tank items Cho'Gath's raw HP, Rupture lockdown, and W silence create windows where your W heal cannot keep up with his stat wall and true-damage R."}],"wants":{"you":["Sidestep Rupture, then commit E inside its 6s gap — the E2 pull lands you on a champion with no dash to answer it","Hijack Feast at level 6 and immediately look for a kill or objective fight where true-damage execute swings the math","Keep trades short until level 2-3 full kit, then chain Q slow into E2 hook into W for the full burst rotation"],"foe":["Land Rupture (Q) to knock you airborne and cancel your E2 approach before you can hook in","Stack Feast kills for permanent health and let Carnivore refill him off the wave so he never has to leave lane","Use Feral Scream (W silence) to interrupt your chain rotations mid-trade and prevent W cast"]},"early":"Level 1 is genuinely even — you have Q slow or W available but no hook, and Cho'Gath pokes with Vorpal Spikes on his autos while Carnivore refills him off every minion. Don't step into melee range to trade; last-hit from Q range and read his Rupture animation. The moment Rupture misses you have about six seconds — without it he has zero engage. At level 2, E comes online and the dynamic shifts: dash in with E1, recast to pull yourself onto him, Q slow, W heal. Feral Scream is his only interrupt on 11s — bait it or get your W off before he does. By level 3 your full rotation beats his pre-tank stats outright.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are skill-dependent because Rupture is now genuinely punishing — land it on your E approach and you lose the window entirely, standing in the open as a melee mage. Play the clock: it is a flat 6s, so commit the instant it lands and misses, not before. The W heal and Petricite autos win the extended trade when you get in cleanly, but do not overstay into Feral Scream's 9-11s silence. At 6 the lane tightens rather than swings: you get Feast on 80s and so does he, and his version grows him permanently — take the all-in while his is spent, and never while yours is. Ping your jungler for that window: he has no dash, so any second body on him turns the pull into a kill rather than a trade.","late":"At two or more items Cho'Gath's raw HP wall starts to outpace your W sustain, and Rupture plus the silence make approaching him alone a coinflip. You remain relevant by stealing Feast for priority targets in teamfights — true damage means no resistance on their carry matters — but you cannot 1v1 a fully-stacked Cho'Gath without jungle help or a hard lead. Buy health rather than MR against him, and flank the backline with the stolen R instead of re-engaging the thing that has been eating all game.","whys":["Neither of you has a reliable engage at level 1 — Cho'Gath pokes with Vorpal Spikes but Rupture is the only real threat, so farm safely and read his casting arm before stepping up.","E online: E1 closes the gap, the E2 recast pulls you onto him, and W heals back the HP cost — Cho'Gath has no mobility at all to escape the sequence once it starts.","Full kit rotations with passive Petricite Burst autos let you outpace his pre-tank HP in extended trades, and Rupture on cooldown is the signal to step forward and chain.","Rupture's 950 range and flat 6s cooldown mean the safe gap is seconds, not a lane phase — step in the moment it lands and misses, or you get knocked up mid-E and left exposed.","Hijacking Feast removes his win condition: you gain a true-damage AP-scaling execute and Cho'Gath cannot stack more HP while his R is on cooldown — force the all-in the second Hijack is ready.","Riftmaker's Void passive stacks through your chain rotation and W, keeping your brawl advantage alive, but Cho'Gath's first tank Mythic means extended trades start to even out.","Stacked HP, Rupture lockdown and a 9-11s silence outlast your W sustain in a straight 1v1, and Feast's true damage ignores whatever resistances you bought — use Hijack on their carries, not on him."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"darius","win":["Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Decimate's outer edge stacks Hemorrhage for free and heals him — he shreds your HP before E is online, so give up the first wave entirely."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Darius's Hemorrhage reaches full 5 stacks and his empowered Q outer-ring heal sustains him through your W — this is his clearest kill window before 6."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals Noxian Guillotine: base true damage only, since you cannot apply Hemorrhage — but it still refreshes on a kill, so use it as the finisher."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Omnivamp stacks through extended trades and stacks your W healing on top — sustained brawls start to tilt in your favour past this point."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2 only after baiting or burning his E Apprehend so he cannot pull you back into bleed","Trade in short bursts using Q slow and one passive auto, then disengage before Hemorrhage reaches 5 stacks","Steal Noxian Guillotine at 6 and look for the all-in the moment he dips below half health"],"foe":["Land Apprehend (E) to yank you into his Q outer ring and stack Hemorrhage without you being able to walk out","Reach 5 Hemorrhage stacks and execute with Noxian Guillotine before your W can heal you back up","Zone you off CS with Decimate's outer ring so you never get healthy enough to hook in"]},"early":"Darius owns level 1 completely — Decimate's outer ring stacks Hemorrhage and heals him, and you have no dash to punish it yet. Farm from the tip of your Q and never step inside that ring for CS. At level 2 your E comes online but Apprehend is live too; get pulled and you eat the empowered Q to the face, so hook only after he whiffs it and owes 26 seconds. Keep the wave even or slightly slow-pushed so you are never trapped at turret where the bleed finishes you.","mid":"Levels 3-5 you have your full kit but Darius is at his strongest — five Hemorrhage stacks and the outer-ring heal crush extended trades even through W, so buy Grievous Wounds before you contest them. Look for single short rotations: E1 forward to bait Apprehend, let him whiff and owe 16-26s, recast to pull onto him, Q slow into one Petricite auto, W immediately, then leave before the bleed stacks. His Crippling Strike wind-up is the cue to step out, not the cast. At 6 he is still favoured: his Guillotine carries five stacks and your stolen one carries none, so bait his out before you commit yours.","late":"Post-first-item the lane levels out into a genuine skill contest. Your Riftmaker Omnivamp stacks through the same extended fights Darius wants, and by two items you are the better teamfighter. In teamfights a stolen Guillotine is base true damage rather than the stacked version he fires, but it refreshes on a kill and ignores resistances — steal it before every dragon or baron and use it to clean up whoever he was supposed to finish. One-versus-one late you still have to respect the bleed: lead with W damage and keep the fight short so Hemorrhage never reaches five stacks.","whys":["Darius can Q the outer ring from range and stack Hemorrhage before your E is online — you have no safe trade and no dash to close, so cede the wave and farm with Q tip.","Your E is live but so is Apprehend — your hook is a trade, his pull is a death sentence, so move up only once he has spent it and eaten its 26s cooldown.","Full kit with Q slow, passive autos, and W sustain means you can skirmish, but Darius's bleed punishes every second you stay in range — keep trades short and disengage after one rotation.","Darius hits his pre-6 peak: Decimate's outer ring heals him through your W unless you have Grievous Wounds, and five Hemorrhage stacks make his execute near-guaranteed if you overstay — watch the bleed counter.","His Guillotine is the bigger threat at 6: yours carries no Hemorrhage stacks, so it lands base true damage. Bait his out, then use your copy as the finisher — it still refreshes on a kill.","Riftmaker Omnivamp and your sustained W healing now match his bleed sustain trade-for-trade; neither side has a clean kill window without blunder, making this a genuine skill-check in both directions.","Both champions scale into late teamfights with your stolen Guillotine as the swing factor — use Hijack before every objective so Darius enters the fight without his execute while you carry it."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"drmundo","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — hook him into W for the first real all-in; he has no mobility to escape and his sustain isn't online yet."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Maximum Dosage denies his lane-saving heal and hands you a speed-buffed chase tool — all-in immediately after he uses his R first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Void Corruption stacks through extended trades, letting your W heal compound against his health pool while Grievous Wounds cuts his regeneration."},{"when":"Late","text":"Maximum Dosage heals a percent of his MISSING health, so armour and MR do nothing — without Grievous Wounds live when he casts it he outheals you."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 and chain W into passive autos before his Heart Zapper burst can answer","Force trades on Heart Zapper's cooldown so his sustain window is dark when you commit","Steal Maximum Dosage after he burns it defensively, then run him down with his own speed boost"],"foe":["Land Heart Zapper to burst you mid-combo and offset your W heal","Eat your first immobilise on his passive, grab the cannister back, and farm to items","Reach full build where Maximum Dosage outheals every damage source you can output"]},"early":"At level 1 you only have Q — use it to clip him at max range as he last-hits and deny his passive regeneration stacks from building freely, but don't walk up into cleaver range without your E. His Q cleaver is percent-current-HP, so a full-health Sylas takes meaningful chip; respect the width and angle yourself to dodge its center mass. The lane is genuinely neutral here: he can't all-in you, but you can't safely hook in either.","mid":"Level 2 is your first real window — E in, W immediately for the heal, and weave autos between casts to spend Petricite Burst. The key read is Heart Zapper: 15-17s, never lower, and it both damages and stores what you deal to heal him back, so hooking in while it is live hands him the trade. Bait it, let it pop, then commit. His Blunt Force Trauma opens with an attack reset — that wind-up, not the cast, is your cue to step out. Levels 4-5 he itemises health and the cleaver gets oppressive: respect his Q angles, freeze or slow-push to deny farm, and never spend both E charges to initiate.","late":"At 6 the lane is yours: if he spends Maximum Dosage to survive a trade, Hijack it — you deny the save and gain the speed to run him down while his own copy sits on a flat 120s against your 80s. Force the all-in on his R cooldown, never into it. From first item the race tightens: Riftmaker carries you through long trades, but Grievous Wounds must be live before his R fires or the missing-health heal undoes the whole fight, and no amount of armour or MR touches it. By 2+ items a full Dr. Mundo is nearly unkillable without coordinated burst; the lead has to be cashed into objectives before then. Play the side lane for pressure rather than the kill, take the fight only when your team can collapse with you, and keep stealing whichever enemy ultimate wins the teamfight outright.","whys":["Q poke only — clip him at max range; his passive eats the first immobilise and drops a cannister he heals off; walking up costs current-HP chip.","E into W is online and his sustain isn't yet itemized — this is your safest all-in window before his health stack compounds.","Full kit access but Heart Zapper timing dictates every trade; bait it before hooking or he outheals your burst and wins the exchange.","He starts stacking HP items and his cleaver range gets punishing — freeze the wave, respect Q angles, and avoid wasting E2 on engage.","Stealing Maximum Dosage off his first cast denies his lane-save and gives you his speed buff — the clearest kill window of the entire game.","Riftmaker stacking through extended trades keeps you competitive, but Grievous Wounds must be online to cap his regeneration during all-ins.","Fully itemised Maximum Dosage heals a percent of missing health, so resistances change nothing and only Grievous Wounds does — your mid-game lead must already be cashed into objectives before this."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"fiora","win":["Fiora","Fiora","Fiora","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Fiora's Q poke and early vital procs punish any misstep before your E comes online — respect the pressure completely."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you a copy of Grand Challenge: the all-in math flips and you can now proc vitals on her instead."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks with W's missing-health heal, making your sustained brawl genuinely winnable if Riposte is down."},{"when":"Lvls 3-5","text":"Fiora's Lunge applies on-hit and sets up the vital-auto pattern — she trades more efficiently here unless Riposte is clearly on cooldown."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Riposte (W) with a fake E2 commit, confirm it fired, then chain in with the real E2-Q-W rotation","Absorb her Q-vital poke with E1's shield to deny free HP leads before committing","Steal Grand Challenge at 6 and use your copy to proc vitals on her during all-ins"],"foe":["Catch your chain or W lunge in Riposte, erasing the burst and slowing you out of range","Proc vitals with Q spacing and deny extended trades where your W heal would flip the result","Survive to two items where her percent-max-HP true damage from vitals outpaces your healing entirely"]},"early":"At level 1 you only have Q or W — do not step up for trades. Farm with Q tip and accept chip damage; forcing anything before E is online loses you HP you cannot afford to give a Fiora. At level 2 E arrives but Riposte is already set to punish your first hook: she parries the chain or your W lunge outright, slows you as she steps out, and cashes a vital while you walk. Spend levels 1-3 probing with Q poke and Petricite autos on her walk-ups, not committing E while Riposte is healthy. Your real pre-6 window opens when Riposte burns on a minion or a dodge — then E2-Q-W inside its 16-24s recharge.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a toss-up of execution. Lunge lets her step into a vital, auto it with on-hit applied, and leave before your chain arrives; threaten E2 as a fake to bait that step back, then Q her retreat and weave Petricite autos. Do not brawl a full-HP Fiora with Riposte up, and buy Grievous Wounds before you try — every vital she hits heals her through the exchange, and her Bladework second hit crits. At 6 the matchup pivots: steal Grand Challenge immediately, even if you are not fighting yet, because her 110s copy is gone while yours is back in 80. Force the fight inside Riposte's 16-24s gap, close with E1, recast to pull, and spend W at low HP for the bigger heal.","late":"With Riftmaker and a stolen Grand Challenge you become the teamfight threat Fiora never expected — your copy reveals vitals on her in a side-lane duel, and the omnivamp keeps you alive through her true damage. Never 1v1 her at two items if she is ahead: vital damage scales with your max HP and no resistance touches it, so only Grievous Wounds and clean spacing help you there. Play river skirmishes and objectives instead, where another enemy ultimate is often the better steal anyway. Your win condition is turning the 6-spike into a kill or Herald control before her two-item duelling peak fully arrives and the side lane closes.","whys":["You have Q or W but no E hook — Fiora's Q poke and free vital windows make any step-up punishable before your full kit is available.","E is online but Riposte parries your chain or W lunge outright and slows you off — Fiora dictates every trade unless you bait the 24s parry first.","Full kit access helps but her Riposte timing still controls the exchange; Q poke into passive autos is your ceiling until you confirm Riposte is on cooldown.","Neither kit fully controls the window — W can match her output, but every vital she hits heals her back, so without Grievous Wounds her Q-spacing simply trades more efficiently than your chain.","Hijack takes Grand Challenge — her 110s ultimate on your 80s clock, and your copy reveals vitals on HER. Steal it the moment it is up.","Riftmaker's omnivamp on top of the W heal finally matches her vital sustain, and a stolen Grand Challenge means the all-in is winnable whenever Riposte is on cooldown.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacking with W's missing-health heal lets you sustain through extended brawls, and a stolen Grand Challenge makes you the stronger side-laner in a 1v1 where you dictate the engage timing."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"galio","win":["Skill","Skill","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your E comes online — the first E2 hook into W punish is available if Galio steps too close to CS."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Galio's W taunt is his only damage-reduction window; bait it, then chain in with E2 and dump the full Q-W-passive rotation before it resets."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Hero's Entrance — a global R you can use on demand while Galio sits top lane without his teamfight tool."},{"when":"Galio W","text":"Shield of Durand taunt + damage reduction is Galio's defining threat window; every all-in attempt into an active W is a death sentence — never trade into the golden glow."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Shield of Durand (W) into cooldown, then E2 hook in and dump Q-W-passive rotation before it comes back","Steal Hero's Entrance at 6 and use it on a sidelane skirmish while Galio is trapped top without his global","Extend fights with W heal to outlast his MR-stacking burst pattern and punish long trades"],"foe":["Taunt you into his W during a trade so your damage reduction window negates your full rotation","Poke with Q Winds of War and passive magic-damage autos to whittle you down before you can sustain back","Bank Hero's Entrance for a teamfight or dive and roam to make your stolen ult less impactful"]},"early":"At level 1 Galio's passive Colossal Smash empowers his next auto with magic damage and he can poke your health bar every time you step up to CS — you have no ability to answer back without burning a Q that reveals your position. Farm from Q range and absorb the passive-auto poke passively; you will heal it back the moment W comes online. At 2 your E unlocks the first kill window: if he steps inside your hook range while farming, dash in, W immediately (before he activates Shield of Durand), and chain the Q explosion into passive autos before he can taunt. At 3 you have your full basic kit but so does he — his Winds of War Q pushes lane efficiently, so do not let him freely shove and roam. Shove back and follow any roam ping; his Q cooldown is long early and he cannot poke you safely through a full minion wave.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 belong to Galio in extended trades because his MR stacking directly blunts your AP burst — the longer a fight goes, the more his armor and MR absorb. Your answer is shorter, sharper trades: E2 hook when W is on cooldown, drop Q between you, W the instant you land, passive-auto twice, then disengage before Shield of Durand refreshes. Do not brawl into a full spell rotation where he has W ready. At 6 the dynamic flips hard: Hero's Entrance is one of the best stolen ultimates in the game — you get a global dive tool with a damage shield that lets you impact any skirmish on the map, while Galio sits top lane stripped of his teamfight identity. Use it immediately on a live skirmish; sitting on a stolen Galio R wastes the entire level-6 spike.","late":"In the mid-to-late game Galio's MR stacking starts to feel the pinch of your Riftmaker or Shadowflame — percent penetration and Void Staff cut through his resistance growth and your W heal tops you off faster than his poke chips you down. Hero's Entrance keeps paying dividends: every time Galio's R is on cooldown is a window where your team can force a fight and he cannot respond globally. In 5v5 teamfights his R initiates a dangerous engage, so track it as a cooldown; if you see it used, that is a 130-second window to force objectives. His damage in late-game fights stays capped by your magic resist (his own Q, W, passive all deal magic damage), so Riftmaker's omnivamp sustain keeps you healthier through extended fights than he expects. The lane is genuinely even throughout but every objective where his R is stolen or on cooldown tilts the game your way.","whys":["Galio's passive Colossal Smash procs on his first auto and gives him a free poke trade — you have no answer without E, so sit at Q range and last-hit safely rather than gifting him a free empowered auto.","At 2 your E2 hook comes online and Galio has no disengage; a hook into W punish is real, but so is his Shield of Durand — neither side can commit cleanly, making it genuinely even.","At 3 your full kit lands a real chunk, but Shield of Durand's 18-second taunt eats your best window — trade in short rotations while it is down and the lane stays a coin-flip.","Winds of War Q clears waves efficiently and his resistance stats let him weather your poke; neither of you can kill the other cleanly in the 4-5 window, so it plays out as a farm contest.","Hijack takes Hero's Entrance and hands you a global engage while Galio waits out a 180-second cooldown on his own — spend it on a live skirmish. His W taunt is 18s, so bait it before you E2 in.","Riftmaker or Shadowflame comes online and your W heal outraces his sustained poke; more importantly you hold Hero's Entrance and can use it globally while he has no ult — the stolen R swings every skirmish you touch.","Your penetration items cut through his MR stacking and Riftmaker omnivamp sustains through his magic-damage kit; in late fights you outlast him on HP while he loses his teamfight identity every time you Hijack his global R."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"gangplank","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Skill","Skill","Gangplank","Gangplank"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — the moment it hits, you can dash forward and chain-pull him into a W, punishing any early step-up before he has a barrel established."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full kit with W sustain lets you erase his barrel poke between engages; hook onto him past a cleared barrel window and the trade is yours."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Cannon Barrage — useful for objective setups, but it doesn't turn the 1v1; look for all-ins with Ignite rather than relying on the stolen R."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Gangplank's Tri Force or Sheen crit spike comes online here — he can chunk you with a Parrrley-passive-barrel combo for more than you can W-heal back, and his W cleanses your chain stun mid-fight."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 after his barrel explodes or misses, catching him without his spacing tool ready","Trade in short W-heal bursts between barrel cooldowns so you erase the poke deficit and force him to use Remove Scurvy","Shove and roam once you've confirmed his barrels are on cooldown — he has no mobility to match you"],"foe":["Chain Powder Keg explosions from range to chunk you down before you can E2 engage","Use Remove Scurvy to cleanse your E2 stun mid-engage and reset back to kiting range","Parrrley-passive-crit you through minion lines while banking gold for his scaling spike"]},"early":"At level 1 you have nothing — no E, no reliable engage — so let him Parrrley for free CS and track where he drops his first barrel. Level 2 changes everything: the moment E unlocks, forward-dash close, fire the chain-pull, and W him the instant he lands — this punishes any early barrel placement he's committed to, and he cannot Remove Scurvy fast enough to negate the full combo. Keep pressure on until your mana forces you back, but never walk up unless at least one of his barrels is down or exploded; a chained barrel into Parrrley crit when you have no E cooldown is half your HP gone.","mid":"Levels 3-5 are the most honest window of the lane. You can Q-poke him between his barrels, your W sustain erases most of what he dishes out in a full rotation, and your passive chains in melee range out-DPS his Parrrley-poke at close range. The trap is Remove Scurvy: GP's W cleanses your E2 stun on a 22-second cooldown, so if you E2 into him and he cleanses, you're melee range with no chain and no stun against a man with a loaded pistol. Bait the orange before you commit the pull — fake the E1 dash and watch him burn W early, then E2 for the real engage. At 6 the stolen Cannon Barrage is worth sending at objectives or for setup, not for soloing him — the 1v1 stays even.","late":"Gangplank's crit scaling takes over once he has Sheen or Tri Force online and especially at two items. His Parrrley crits through barrels deal more than you can W-heal, his W has enough uptime to cleanse your hook on nearly every engage attempt, and his global R swings objectives on the other side of the map while you're occupied top. Your goal is to have converted the level 2-5 windows into a kill lead or meaningful CS deficit on him — if the lane is still even at 20 minutes, the scaling edge belongs to him. Play for roams on his barrel cooldown and let your team win fights with a stolen Cannon Barrage layered on top of their CC, not for straight 1v1s.","whys":["Your Q reaches 775 units against his 625 Parrrley — poke him off the wave from outside his range and track where he drops his first Powder Keg.","E comes online and a forward-dash into chain-pull plus W hits him for real damage before he can Parrrley back; this is your first genuine kill threat.","Both kits are functional but the Remove Scurvy cleanse means every E2 engage is a coin flip unless you bait the orange first — neither side holds a clean edge.","The lane stabilises around a bait-the-W-then-hook pattern: if you read his Remove Scurvy, you win the trade; if you miss the read, you're stuck in melee with no stun and he Parrrleys you out.","Hijack nets you Cannon Barrage, which is strong for objective play but leaves the 1v1 genuinely even — he can still cleanse your chain and kite you with barrel spacing if you try to all-in.","His Sheen or Tri Force Parrrley crit can chunk you through a barrel for more than your W heals, and Remove Scurvy uptime now covers nearly every engage attempt you make.","Full crit Gangplank barrels chain for half your HP before you can E2 into range, his W cleanses your stun on a short enough cooldown to deny your pattern, and his R controls the map globally — the late game belongs to him."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"gnar","win":["Gnar","Gnar","Skill","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Mini-Gnar's Boomerang Throw (Q) shreds you for free — he pokes you to half HP before your E is even available."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack comes online and the math flips: you steal GNAR! and use the AOE stun against his own team in the next fight."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker spike — your W heal outheals his boomerang poke and one full E2-hook chain with a stolen R ends the lane."},{"when":"GNAR! window","text":"Every time Mega-Gnar transforms, respect Wallop (W): its stun into GNAR! is a full chain that kills you before your W heal lands."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 during Mini-Gnar to close the gap before he can kite back with Hop (E)","Bait out GNAR! (Mega) in a dummy engage, then all-in Mini-Gnar on his long transform cooldown","Steal GNAR! at level 6 and use the AOE slam to win the first river skirmish"],"foe":["Stack rage with Boomerang Throw (Q) poke while you are stuck in ranged-poke range levels 1-2","Transform to Mega-Gnar and catch you mid-E-dash with Wallop's stun into GNAR! R","Kite Mini-form until GNAR! comes back and force you to choose between engaging into CC or farming into his poke"]},"early":"Levels 1 and 2 are pure survival — Mini-Gnar's Boomerang Throw (Q) bounces back through you twice and you have no reliable way to close that gap without E, so you are eating poke for free. Last-hit under turret range with your Q Chain Lash and do not burn E trying to hook him yet. At level 2 your E comes online and the dynamic shifts slightly, but he can Hop (E) away from any hook attempt — only commit the E2 pull when his Hop is on cooldown or when he steps into a minion wall that blocks his exit.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are a genuine skill matchup built entirely around GNAR! rage management. Watch his rage bar: Mini-Gnar is your target, Mega-Gnar ends you. Force a dummy Q-W trade to bait his Hop, then hook him with E2 and chain your W-Kingslayer for the burst-heal follow-up while his mobility is spent. Full-kit Sylas with W sustain wins the extended trade against a Mini-Gnar who has used his escapes — the same trade against a transforming Gnar is a death sentence. At level 6 you steal GNAR! and the lane decisively swings; your stolen R has the same AOE slam potential he was threatening you with, and your W healing in the Mega-form skirmish is now tilting the HP math in your favour.","late":"Two items in, Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks on top of W's missing-HP heal and you win every 1v1 as long as you hook into Mega-Gnar AFTER his GNAR! R is spent rather than into it. In teamfights your stolen GNAR! R is a game-deciding AOE stun — save the steal for the fight, not for lane. Gnar's late strength is siege and disengage for his team; yours is assassination and stolen ultimates, so keep flanking angles open and never chase Mini-Gnar through an open field where GNAR! can transform mid-chase and stun-lock you against his team.","whys":["Mini-Gnar's Boomerang Throw bounces through you twice at level 1 and you have no E to close distance — last-hit safely and accept the poke deficit.","He can Hop away from any E2 hook attempt and the boomerang poke accelerates his rage bar toward GNAR! — do not commit E before his Hop is spent.","Full kit lets you threaten the E2 pull, but GNAR! is also online by now — this is a genuine spacing and rage-bar read battle, neither side has a clean edge.","Watch the rage bar religiously: hook E2 into a low-rage Mini-Gnar after his Hop, chain W for the burst-heal, and disengage before the transform completes.","Hijack flips the lane — you now have GNAR! and your W heal in Mega-form means you win the sustained brawl he was winning before; look for the all-in immediately.","Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks on top of W's missing-HP scaling and your stolen GNAR! R finishes the lane — force the all-in on his post-transform cooldown.","Two items plus a banked stolen GNAR! R makes you a teamfight carry; use the AOE slam to engage for your team and outscale his siege-and-peel pattern."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"gragas","win":["Skill","Skill","Gragas","Gragas","Gragas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Gragas's Body Slam (E) plus W damage-reduction lets him dictate skirmishes before your kit fully comes online — respect the window."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Gragas's R: a point-and-click knockback-burst that you now wield against him, swinging every all-in in your favour."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's stacking true-damage converts your W heal trades into decisive wins; Gragas cannot out-sustain your full chain at this point."},{"when":"Late","text":"Two items plus a stolen Explosive Cask lets you peel, engage, or burst from range — you carry teamfights by threatening his own displacement."}],"wants":{"you":["Absorb his Body Slam with your E1 shield, then E2-chain him during the cooldown to land your W-Q combo","Reach level 6 and steal his Explosive Cask — his win condition becomes your weapon the moment Hijack comes up","Trade in short extended bursts: E1 shield eats his burst, W heals you back, and passive autos pressure him out of W range"],"foe":["Land Body Slam (E) first to gap-close or escape so your trade window never opens cleanly","Pop Drunken Rage (W) for the damage reduction and empowered auto reset, turning the HP exchange against your heal","Zone you off CS pre-6 with Q barrel poke and Body Slam threats so you arrive at level 6 behind in gold and HP"]},"early":"At level 1 you both have single-ability kits and neither wants an extended brawl — last-hit with Q at max range and deny him free barrels. At level 2 your W comes online but so does his Body Slam, and he is slightly better at short-range skirmishes right now: do not open with E2 into his full kit because a Body Slam into W reduction into his Q barrel will out-trade you. Poke with Q, shield poke with E1, and wait for Body Slam's cooldown before you commit.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are his window — his full kit of barrel poke, Body Slam engage, and W damage-reduction beats your pattern if you engage recklessly. The correct play is to use E1's shield reactively, not proactively: let him Body Slam into you, absorb it, then immediately E2-chain him for a Q-W combo while Body Slam is cooling down. You cannot win the initiated trade, but you absolutely win the counter-trade. At 6 everything flips: steal his Explosive Cask immediately and look for the all-in — a Gragas with no R and a Sylas holding one is an entirely different lane.","late":"Post-Riftmaker you outduel him cleanly because your W heal exceeds what his W damage-reduction can negate, and Hijack ensures his teamfight disruption tool belongs to you. In fights, save the stolen Explosive Cask to peel off assassins or to isolate him for your team — do not waste it as an opener. Two items in you are a flex threat: steal the strongest R on the map at any given fight, flank the backline with E2, and trust your chain of passive autos to finish what W starts.","whys":["His barrel (Q) poke hurts but neither of you can all-in at level 1 — match his Q range and deny the free stacks without burning E.","Body Slam is up at 14s but so is your W burst-heal — neither of you commits through the other's answer, so poke and hold the E charge.","His full kit of Q barrel into Body Slam into W reduction beats your engage pattern at this level — counter-trade only, never initiate.","He sustains his HP lead through Q poke and W-buffered trades; respect Body Slam's cooldown and punish the window, not the initiation.","At 6 his Explosive Cask (100s) plus the 5-second W reduction and Happy Hour heal still edge the 1v1 — steal it, but fight with your jungler.","Riftmaker online: your W heal out-sustains Happy Hour, and with Explosive Cask stolen he engages on a 14-second Body Slam and nothing else — all-in the moment it is spent.","Two items plus a stolen Explosive Cask makes you the superior teamfight threat; his kit has no answer to your sustain and his own displacement in your hands."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"garen","win":["Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Garen's Q silence interrupts your W mid-channel and cancels your E2 chain mid-cast — he punishes your every approach attempt for free."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals Demacian Justice and gives you an AP-scaled true-damage execute he cannot buy back, making the first post-6 all-in genuinely winnable."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker omnivamp on top of W heal lets you sustain through Garen's Q-spin combo and trade extended once Courage expires."},{"when":"Late","text":"Garen's Courage stacks scale, his passive regen recovers HP between fights, and his R true-damage punishes any HP deficit — he retakes the duel advantage if the game drags."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Courage (W) with a fake E approach, then commit E2 chain into Q and W only after it expires","Fight extended with W heal active so Garen's spin damage bleeds into your sustain rather than a kill window","Steal Demacian Justice at 6 and cast it on Garen while he has no execute to threaten you back"],"foe":["Land Q silence on your E approach to cancel the chain mid-cast and trade into zero cooldowns","Pop Courage (W) to eat your burst window, then spin free and regen back to full on passive","Stack Courage, sit on a shutdown bounty, and R-execute you whenever your HP dips from bad trades"]},"early":"Level 1 is a concession — Garen's Q silence will interrupt your W mid-channel if you try to force a trade, and without E you have no reliable engage. Farm at Q splash range and do not walk into auto range; he will Q-silence you and spin freely with no cooldowns spent. At level 2 your E comes online but remain cautious: if he Qs the moment you fire E2, the silence cancels the chain and you eat his empowered auto and spin for free. The only safe trade at levels 1-3 is to bait his Q onto a minion or wait for it to expire on cooldown, then E1 dash to absorb poke on the shield and E2 chain into W. Do not extend the trade beyond the W heal window — Garen's passive regen and Courage reset his durability faster than you expect.","mid":"The matchup turns at 6 when you steal Demacian Justice: he loses his kill threat, you gain an AP-scaling true-damage execute, and that swings the all-in math enough to force a fight. Use Hijack immediately when he is below 50% HP so the true-damage execute value is maximal. Outside that spike, levels 4-5 are still Garen-favoured — Courage's damage reduction eats your burst window, so trade only when it is provably on cooldown. Count the 24-second timer aloud if needed. Farm and look for jungle assistance rather than forcing solo all-ins on a healthy Garen with Courage available.","late":"Past two items Garen retakes the edge: Courage stacks accumulate additional armour and magic resist, his passive regen is significant between short skirmishes, and Demacian Justice's true damage punishes any HP lead you failed to convert earlier. If you reach late game without a kill lead, your role is teamfighter not duelist — flank with E, Q-slow the backline, and steal the most impactful enemy ultimate rather than walking back into a stacked Garen for a 1v1 you increasingly lose. The window to close this matchup is mid-game around the first Riftmaker spike; let it pass and you are playing around team rather than lane advantage.","whys":["Garen's Q silence is your hardest counter at level 1 — it stops W mid-channel and there is no kit answer; last-hit at Q chain range and concede the wave tempo.","His Q still cancels E2 on cast; only trade into a Garen who already burned Q on a minion or is sitting at the end of its cooldown.","Full kit access helps but Courage (W) eats the burst window — commit only after baiting the W activation, then E2-Q-W in the gap.","Levels 4-5 are Courage-gated: his damage reduction and passive regen outlast short trades; freeze or slow-push to force a gank rather than duel.","Hijack takes Demacian Justice and a real execute, but his own R is back on 120s and Courage's 22-second shield plus his passive regen still win the straight duel.","Riftmaker omnivamp plus W heal makes extended trades survivable once Courage expires, but you still lose short all-ins where he Qs first.","Stacked Courage and regen dominate the late duel; stop trying to 1v1 and use E-chain flanks to enable your team instead."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"graves","win":["Sylas","Skill","Graves","Skill","Graves","Graves","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Before E is online Graves has only his four-bullet shotgun cone — your 775-range Q out-ranges it and chips him for free."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Graves gets Smoke Screen (W) and Quickdraw (E) together — a 26s vision cut plus a 16s dash, and spacing becomes lethal."},{"when":"Lvl 6 + items","text":"Graves's Collateral Damage R is explosive burst you cannot steal usefully, and his first-item spike (Youmuu's or Collector) lets him one-rotate you before W can heal it back."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacked with Shadowflame's magic pen makes your W heal loop permanently competitive, and his shotgun burst starts falling short of your total HP pool."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 the instant Quickdraw is on cooldown so he cannot dash out of your W range","Use Q Chain Lash at max range in the early levels to trade poke without walking into his four-bullet spread","Stack W heal in extended trades — drag the fight long enough that his burst window closes and your sustain wins"],"foe":["Use Quickdraw (E) to maintain spacing and dodge your E2 hook before you can chain the W","Smoke Screen (W) over you to cut your sight range, then disengage while you cannot track him","Build Youmuu's or Collector early so his burst burst kills you before W can heal the deficit"]},"early":"At level 1 Graves only has his shotgun — four bullets in a short cone that punish you heavily if you walk into it, but your 775-range Q Chain Lash out-ranges it, letting you poke him off CS without eating the spread. Farm with the tip of Q and do not step up into his auto range. The moment you hit level 2 and he does not, respect that his 425-range Quickdraw is one dash away from a point-blank trade that hurts; keep the wave pushed so he is the one walking forward into your Q zones.","mid":"Level 3 is when this lane goes against you — Graves has his shotgun, Smoke Screen, and Quickdraw all live. The smoke cuts your sight range rather than your casting, and his 16-second dash holds the exact spacing where the four-bullet spread deals maximum damage. Do not use E2 hook to initiate unless Quickdraw is on cooldown; he will simply dash through the chain and burst you while you are mid-animation. At level 6 his Collateral Damage is a 100-second skillshot with no displacement on it at all — real damage, but it repositions neither of you — so this matchup lacks your defining level-6 power spike. Play through the jungle, track Quickdraw's 16 seconds, and take small Q-poke leads rather than all-ins.","late":"At two items your Riftmaker omnivamp and Shadowflame magic penetration finally make the extended trade loop work — his shotgun burst still hurts but your W heal closes the deficit faster than he can reset the pattern. Force fights in tight sideline corridors where his 425-range Quickdraw has nowhere to carry him outside your chain range; open-field skirmishes still favor him. Collateral Damage is pure cone damage with no displacement, so the stolen R adds burst rather than a repositioning tool — win this through sustained brawling and map pressure, not through the ultimate theft that defines your best matchups.","whys":["His shotgun autos fire four bullets in a short cone and that is all he has at level 1 — your 775-range Chain Lash out-ranges it, so set the poke trade before E is even online.","Both of you gain your second ability and neither kit is complete — you have E but no full chain yet, and he has Quickdraw to deny your hook; genuine skill expression decides small trades.","Quickdraw and Smoke Screen arrive together: he can blind your Q cast, dash out of E2 range, and burst with Buckshot in one fluid pattern before your W heal applies.","His combo cooldowns reset frequently at this bracket but you have mana pressure and W sustain to match his short trades — neither side can force a clean kill without jungle help.","Collateral Damage is useless in your hands as an escape tool, so the level-6 Hijack spike that defines Sylas in most lanes simply does not appear here; Graves keeps the kill pressure.","Youmuu's or Collector first item lets him burst you through W heal before the omnivamp loop stabilises — his AD spike outpaces your first-item power in this ranged-vs-melee dynamic.","Riftmaker's omnivamp stacked with Shadowflame pen makes your W heal loop answer his burst; he cannot rotate fast enough to kill you before the sustain closes the gap."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"gwen","win":["Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas","Gwen","Gwen","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — hook her out of a Q wind-up, land W for the burst-heal combo before she can activate Hallowed Mist."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Full-kit brawling with W healing through her true-damage Q; force extended trades here, her Mist cannot follow a chase initiated by your E hook."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Gwen's R Needlework is strong on her but mediocre stolen on Sylas — do not bank your all-in purely on the hijacked ultimate; her mist can still negate your chain damage."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Gwen's Riftmaker stacks convert into true-damage empowered Q; her W makes her immune to your chains inside the mist, and she outscales your healing with sheer AP burst."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook her with E before she activates Hallowed Mist W so your chain damage is not wasted on the untargetable window","Trade through her with W heal in extended brawls at levels 3-5 when her Mist has a long cooldown and she cannot dodge your Q cross","Steal her R to add pressure at 6 and convert any lead into tower plates or a kill before her item spike arrives"],"foe":["Pop Hallowed Mist W the instant your E chain fires to make your hook land inside a dead zone and waste your cooldowns","Stack Riftmaker and Nashor's Tooth so her empowered Q true damage cuts through your W healing regardless of sustain","Survive the levels 2-5 brawl window and reach her first item where her skirmish damage exceeds what your W can recover"]},"early":"At level 1 you both have weak openers — her Q snips from range, your Q crosses take time to detonate, so farm with Q tip and do not waste your W on chip poke. The real moment is level 2: the instant E unlocks, look for a hook when she steps up to Q. If you land the pull before she activates Hallowed Mist, your chains hit freely and W's burst-heal punishes her back immediately. She cannot escape the hook range without a dash, and pre-Mist the full combo will shave a third of her health. If she pre-activates the Mist on your approach, back off — you burn nothing into an untargetable window.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are your clearest window. With full kit and W healing, extended trades favour you — she needs Mist to survive your chain autos, but it is 22 seconds at rank 1 and she cannot sit in it forever while you reset with W. Aim to hook her after the Mist drops: bait it with a chain Q toward her, and if you must contest early, step inside the mist, because only enemies standing in it can target her. At 6 you steal Needlework, which adds poke pressure but is not the matchup-defining swing it is against Malphite or Sion — her R is solid, not broken on Sylas. Still, use the level-6 window to force another all-in while your lead is intact; she transitions toward her spike faster than you do.","late":"Once Gwen has Riftmaker stacking online, her Q's true damage per snip starts to outpace what W heals back, and her empowered Q shreds regardless of your magic resistance. Hallowed Mist now lasts long enough to cover her major combo windows, meaning your E hook leads into a zone where chains deal zero damage if she times it right. Play for objective converts and team fights where your Hijacked ultimate has broader value — do not attempt a 1v1 into a two-item Gwen expecting the same trade win you had at level 4.","whys":["Neither kit does meaningful damage at level 1 — Gwen's Q snips and your Q detonates slowly; farm safely and set up a level-2 read on her Mist timing.","E unlocks your hook: pull her before she pops Hallowed Mist and W into her for a burst-heal punish she cannot sustain through without trading her Mist cooldown.","Full kit access and W healing let you control extended trades; she needs Mist to negate your chains, but that cooldown gaps let you reset and re-engage.","Your W sustain edges out her pre-item damage in prolonged brawls; hook after Mist expires and she has no tool to negate your chain passive autos.","Gwen's R Needlework plus Riftmaker early stacks shift the damage curve; her W is back fast enough to eat one of your engagement tools on cooldown.","Riftmaker stacks mean her Q true damage overrides your W healing; Mist timing consistently wastes your chain damage and she wins the extended brawl she once lost.","Both kits remain relevant but neither dominates cleanly; your stolen R adds some fight pressure while her scaling keeps the 1v1 honest — play for picks not straight duels."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"heimerdinger","win":["Sylas","Heimerdinger","Heimerdinger","Heimerdinger","Heimerdinger","Heimerdinger","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your E comes online — the only level where a surprise hook-into-W can land before Heimerdinger has three turrets down, but it requires him to be off his W cooldown."},{"when":"Heimer Turrets","text":"Once Heimerdinger places all three H-28G turrets at the wave, your E approach angle is blocked — any dash path runs through turret fire and his E stun grenade covers the gap."},{"when":"Heimer Lvl 6","text":"His UPGRADE!!! empowers the next spell into an Apex turret, amplifying turret damage and locking you out of the melee range your W heal needs."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heimerdinger's first Luden's or Shadowflame makes his W rocket burst and empowered turret shots lethal — he can one-rotation you before your W heal can matter."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook (E second-cast) around a turret angle where only one gun fires to minimize damage on your approach","Land W on Heimerdinger the instant you reach him — its missing-health scaling and your heal are the only way to survive the turret punishment","Bait his E stun grenade before committing your full E dash so he cannot interrupt your chain pull"],"foe":["Drop all three H-28G turrets at the wave to wall you out of melee range and zone you off CS","Hit W rockets in the walk-up phase to whittle your HP so your W heal cannot save you in the dive","Use E grenade to interrupt your E Abduct chain pull and keep you stuck in turret range without a target to hook"]},"early":"At level 1 you have nothing to threaten Heimerdinger with — he starts placing turrets immediately and his W rockets punish any step into CS range. Take Q and farm as far back as the hitbox allows; every HP point you lose here matters because his turret damage compounds. At level 2 your E is online and this is the one fleeting window where a flash-into-E second-cast chain pull can catch him off his rocket cooldown, but he will bait this by pre-placing his stun grenade (E) at the obvious approach angle. If his E grenade is down and only one turret is positioned, a level-2 all-in is the lane's best realistic kill window — otherwise, do not dive three turrets at half HP hoping W saves you.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are when this lane cements itself as a loss. Heimerdinger has a full turret spread covering the wave, Hextech Micro-Rockets reach 1325 units on an 11-second cooldown at rank 1, and your E hook has to cross at least two turrets to reach him — they chip you the whole way in. His grenade stuns you mid-dash on a direct hit, leaving you standing inside turrets with no target to hook and no W heal in flight. Play for safe CS, count his grenade at a flat 11 seconds every rank, and save your own E for escaping ganks rather than trading. At level 6 you steal UPGRADE!!!, but it only empowers your next spell — on a turret that is a zoning tool, not a direct damage ultimate, and he simply walks away from the one charged turret it gives you; it does not swing the matchup the way a Malphite or Sion R would.","late":"In a standard late game both players have items and a team — Sylas's stolen R becomes much stronger because the enemy team has more impactful ultimates to take, and Heimerdinger's turret-zone game matters less in a 5v5 where your team can peel the zone. However, if Heimerdinger froze and starved you in lane, you will be two kills behind with fewer items and his Luden's-Shadowflame burst will still shred you in split-push scenarios. This lane is genuinely winnable at the team-fight stage by stealing a different enemy R, but you must accept the lane is lost and play to not fall further than one item behind.","whys":["You only have Q at level 1 and his turrets are already placing — there is no safe angle to trade without walking into H-28G fire, so Sylas concedes the wave.","Three turrets are down and his W rocket poke is live; your E approach runs through gun range and he can stun you mid-dash with his E grenade to stop the chain pull.","Full kit access does not help when every E path crosses two live turrets — his turret zone walls you out of the melee range your W healing requires.","He out-zones you at every CS attempt; his W rockets whittle your HP faster than you can recover without a completed item, and your E is countered by his grenade timing.","You steal his R but the H-28Q Apex Turret upgrade is a zoning ultimate, not a kill tool — it does not give Sylas a slam-dunk all-in the way a Malphite or Volibear R would.","His first Luden's or Shadowflame makes W rockets and empowered turret shots lethal before you close gap; your W heal cannot compensate for three turrets plus a burst rotation.","Team fights reduce the one-vs-one turret advantage — Sylas stealing a better R from a teammate's opponent makes him relevant, and Heimer's zoning is more manageable with allied presence."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"illaoi","win":["Sylas","Illaoi","Illaoi","Illaoi","Illaoi","Illaoi","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Illaoi's Test of Spirit (E) comes online at level 1 and immediately makes every forward step a coin flip — a pulled spirit takes 15% increased damage from tentacles and forces you to either die in place or flee and surrender the wave."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sylas steals Illaoi's Leap of Faith (R) — a genuine power spike, but Illaoi's passive tentacles and E are already on the field, so stealing her R does not neutralize the lane pressure she has already built."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Illaoi's Heartsteel or Iceborn Gauntlet spike massively increases her tentacle hit damage and slows you in place, turning any trade inside her tentacle field into a guaranteed loss even through Sylas's W heal."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items Illaoi's AOE healing from tentacles during Leap of Faith outheals Sylas's W sustain entirely in extended fights, and her superior teamfight AOE makes the stolen R weaker in her hands than in his."}],"wants":{"you":["Dodge Test of Spirit (E) with your first E dash and only commit to a trade after it is on cooldown","Force short burst trades — E2 chain into W and walk out before tentacles stack damage on you","Steal Leap of Faith (R) and use it in a fight where you have already whittled her HP with Q poke, not from full HP"],"foe":["Land Test of Spirit (E) to rip your spirit out and force you to fight inside a tentacle field or abandon the wave","Stack tentacles around the CS zone so every trade involves passive tentacle slams regardless of her own actions","Activate Leap of Faith (R) when multiple tentacles are already on the field to trigger a cascading slam that outheals and outdamages your W"]},"early":"Level 1 is a pure survival exercise — Illaoi's Test of Spirit (E) is active immediately and one successful pull turns the lane into her tentacle field while your spirit takes 15% bonus damage. Stay at max Q range, land Chain Lash for CS, and do not step into her E cast range to trade. At level 2 she gains Tentacle Smash (W) which means she can proc a tentacle slam on top of a pulled spirit — still do not commit. Your only real window before level 3 is the fifteen seconds after her E misses: if you see it sail past you, immediately dash in with E and burn your W for a burst trade, then back out before tentacles re-stack. Missing that window and standing still is what loses this lane in the first five minutes.","mid":"Levels 4-5 give you more sustained trade pressure via passive Petricite Burst autos and better W healing, but none of that matters inside an active tentacle field — every tentacle that damages you heals her, and that outraces your W sustain in any drawn-out exchange. Your goal is to spot when she has zero tentacles near the CS zone, then take short E2-chain-into-W burst trades and exit. At level 6 you steal Leap of Faith, a real power moment — but only in a controlled all-in when she has no tentacles pre-placed; copying her R into an empty lane is strong, copying it at 30% HP into three tentacles is a death sentence. Keep trades short, respect the field, and convert your E2 chain-pull into a W kill window once poke has given you the HP lead.","late":"Illaoi becomes nearly unkillable in extended teamfights as her tentacles heal her for a percentage of the damage they deal during Leap of Faith — Sylas's W heal is a single-target burst while her healing is AOE and continuous. Your strongest contribution is stealing her R and using it in a side skirmish where no tentacles have been pre-seeded, effectively giving you the AOE slam without her passive infrastructure. In a 5v5 you are better off targeting the enemy team's most impactful non-Illaoi ultimate and staying away from her entirely; if you walk into the tentacle field in a late-game fight, you will die faster than W can heal you back.","whys":["Illaoi's Test of Spirit (E) is live at level 1 — a successful pull attaches a spirit that takes bonus tentacle damage and forces you to either fight in her field or abandon the wave; there is no safe trade answer at this level.","Tentacle Smash (W) is now online alongside E — a pulled spirit under two tentacle hits removes more HP than your W can recover, and your E dash is your only escape tool, not an engagement tool yet.","Full kit access still does not beat her field: your E2 chain-pull is your best trade opener but she can proc a tentacle slam during your own combo animation, and Illaoi's passive sustain from tentacle hits shows even in short trades.","Her tentacles accumulate around the CS zone with every passing minute — Levels 4-5 Illaoi with two to three tentacles active wins almost every melee exchange because the passive procs pad every trade in her favor beyond what your W corrects.","Stealing Leap of Faith is your closest thing to an even moment — a copied R in a clean duel when she has no tentacles pre-placed can swing the all-in — but her passive tentacles and E field mean she retains structural lane dominance even without her R up.","Iceborn Gauntlet or Heartsteel gives Illaoi both extra bulk and a slow on her empowered auto, which pins you inside the tentacle field long enough for multiple passive slams to fire — your Riftmaker spike does not close this gap.","Two items in, Hijack is a 30-second cooldown against her 120-second Leap of Faith, and Riftmaker plus anti-heal finally answers the tentacle healing — fight away from a seeded field and you are the stronger piece."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"irelia","win":["Skill","Sylas","Irelia","Irelia","Skill","Irelia","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E coming online is your first real spike — chain-pull onto her mid-dash with E2, land W on impact, and the burst-heal ends the trade before Ionian Fervor stacks compound."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Irelia's Ionian Fervor stacks and Bladesurge resets peak here — one minion wave sets up a full reset chain; never let her open a trade at max stacks and full attack speed."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Vanguard's Edge; the blades Mark on impact and the wall they leave damages and slows her escape route — use it on the next all-in, not defensively."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Trinity Force or Blade of the Ruined King Irelia with Q resets online outscales your Riftmaker — close the game or roam; do not fight a fully-stacked Irelia with two items unless you are ahead."}],"wants":{"you":["Chain-pull onto her mid-Bladesurge with E2, landing before her stacks and resets come online","Activate W the instant the E2 pull lands to burst-heal through her attack-speed burst and turn short trades into HP leads","Force trades when Flawless Duet is on cooldown so you can brawl without the risk of her stun setup locking you in place"],"foe":["Build Ionian Fervor to max stacks off minions, then Bladesurge into you with the bonus on-hit damage already online","Land Flawless Duet to lock you in place, negate your E escape, and let her Q-reset chain delete your HP bar","Deny your E2 hook by baiting it out, letting the charge expire, then hard-engaging while your dash is on full cooldown"]},"early":"Level 1 is a wash — her Bladesurge pokes and heals off the wave and you cannot punish it yet, so last-hit at Q range and keep your E charges full. The moment you hit level 2, E is your whole gameplan: she will Bladesurge in to build Ionian Fervor, so pre-aim the E2 chain to catch her mid-dash, land on her, and immediately W for the burst-heal. That sequence ends the trade before her attack speed compounds. Never use E1 offensively without E2 ready — an E-less Sylas against Irelia at level 2 is just a melee mage with no escape.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are her window, not yours. A full-stack Irelia with Bladesurge resets live is a blender — if she opens on a minion into you, get E1 up before impact and walk out rather than trading back. Your W heal does not outpace her reset burst in a drawn-out exchange at max stacks. At level 6 the math shifts: Hijack gives you Vanguard's Edge, whose blades Mark on impact and then leave a wall that damages and slows anything crossing it — that wall is what cuts her escape angle out of your chain. Steal it and all-in on the next trade; her R being down means no blade wall of her own while yours is up.","late":"With two items Irelia's Q-reset chain and Trinity Force or Blade of the Ruined King burst eclipse your Riftmaker sustain in short bursts — stop soloing her and treat this as a roam-and-teamfight lane. You still win the teamfight if you steal her R off cooldown: Vanguard's Edge into a cluster is devastating. Split only if you are clearly ahead and have kill pressure; otherwise you are a side-lane stat-check she wins at full build.","whys":["Her Bladesurge pokes and heals off the wave and you have no second ability to answer with — farm at Q tip and bank both E charges for the level 2 window.","E2 hooks her mid-Q-dash: you stun her before the fifth stack triggers, W burst-heals through her shred, and she loses the trade she came to win.","Full attack-speed stacks plus Bladesurge resets give her the damage to chunk you in short exchanges — use E1 on every engage and never extend the fight.","Irelia at full Ionian Fervor with Bladesurge resets live is the most dangerous she gets — stay back, let the stacks decay off minions, and trade only when Flawless Duet is visibly down.","Hijack copies Vanguard's Edge: the blade-wall root disrupts her dash pattern and you can all-in with her own ultimate while hers sits on cooldown.","Her first-item Trinity Force or Blade of the Ruined King burst outpaces Riftmaker in the short trades she prefers — look to roam rather than keep grinding the 1v1.","At two-plus items you outscale her in teamfights by stealing her R off cooldown and using it into grouped enemies — she cannot replicate that swing with your kit."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"jax","win":["Skill","Jax","Jax","Sylas","Sylas","Jax","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Grandmaster-at-Arms on 80s against his own 110s — you get the resist boost and the burst more often than he can, so force the all-in here."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Full kit with W sustain online: hold your autos through Counter Strike, keep Q and W landing, and heal past the burst he already spent."},{"when":"Jax Lvl 2-3","text":"Jax's Counter Strike E turns every extended auto-trade into his favour — respect the stun and get out before it lands."},{"when":"Jax 1st item","text":"Trinity Force spikes his auto damage and Sheen procs to a level where one misjudged trade under his E stun can erase your HP lead."}],"wants":{"you":["Hold your autos through Counter Strike and keep casting Q and W — it dodges attacks, not spells","Steal Grandmaster-at-Arms at 6 and force the all-in while his own 110-second version is still on cooldown","Bank the lead you build at Levels 4-6 into an early tower or kill before his Trinity Force spike"],"foe":["Bait your basic attacks into Counter Strike's dodge window, then stun you inside its 300-unit radius","Rush Trinity Force and force extended autos once his Sheen proc and R armor outpace your healing","Survive Levels 4-6, reach his item spike, and turn the lane around with sustained split-push pressure"]},"early":"Level 1 is a wash — Jax auto-trades with E held, and you only have Q or W, not both; match his aggression at the wave and don't give him a free Level 2 with Counter Strike. At Level 2 and 3 he wins the extended trade: Counter Strike dodges your passive Petricite Burst autos and the stun comes out before your W can finish healing you back. Keep trades short — poke with Q, back off before E's dodge window completes, and never stay in melee range long enough to eat the full stun burst.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are where the tide turns. Counter Strike dodges basic attacks, not abilities — so when he pops it, hold your autos, keep Chain Lash and Kingslayer flowing, and walk out of the 300-unit radius before the stun resolves. He spends 17 seconds of cooldown and you lose almost nothing. At 6, Hijack copies Grandmaster-at-Arms on 80 seconds against his own 110: you get the same armour and magic resist boost, and the burst around it, more often than he does. Force the all-in the moment Hijack is up and deny him the first Trinity Force window by turning the lane into a kill rather than a farm race.","late":"Jax's Trinity Force spike is the inflection point — if he gets it without dying, his Sheen-empowered pattern and R armor make extended brawls dangerous again, and he out-sidelines you at two items. Your goal is to have converted the Level 4-6 lead into a tower, a kill advantage, or both before he completes it. In teamfights you remain relevant by stealing his R on cooldown and peeling carries, but a Jax who survived lane will outduell you in a straight 1v1 past two items, so don't gift him that scenario — play through your team and keep baiting his R for Hijack.","whys":["Jax holds Counter Strike and auto-trades safely at Level 1 — you have one spell, he has a dodge; farm with Q range and don't extend.","Counter Strike E dodges your Petricite Burst passive autos and stuns you before W can heal the burst back — Jax controls the Level 2 window.","Full kit for Jax means he can now engage on YOU: Q leap in, auto, E to dodge your W cast, stun, and burst before you can chain your full rotation.","Counter Strike dodges basic attacks, not spells — hold your autos, keep Q and W flowing, and step outside the 300-unit stun radius before it resolves.","Hijack copies Grandmaster-at-Arms on 80 seconds against his 110 — you hold his resist buff and burst more often than he does, well before Trinity Force lands.","Trinity Force Sheen procs and R armor flip the extended-trade math back to Jax — avoid prolonged melee brawls and respect his item timing.","You retain the stolen-R flex and W sustain in teamfights, but Jax's two-item side-lane is a genuine threat; deny the 1v1 duel and play through objectives."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"jayce","win":["Jayce","Jayce","Jayce","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Jayce's Acceleration Gate + Mercury Cannon poke (E+Q) chunks you before your E hook can reach him — you have no safe approach without burning E into his gate slow."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Jayce's Transformation — your cleanest all-in window; force it the moment Hijack is up, while he is in hammer form and cannot reset to cannon range."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's vamp lets you survive poke long enough to land E hook for a W all-in, but Jayce's Sheen spike simultanously amplifies his burst in both stances."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items Sylas's sustained brawling in melee wins extended brawls Jayce cannot safely commit to, but a clean EQ knockback from Jayce still deletes a chunked Sylas instantly."}],"wants":{"you":["Land E hook (Abduct) after Jayce wastes his E Thundering Blow knockback so you can close the gap without being launched away","Force a trade in hammer form when Jayce is mid-stance-swap and his Mercury Cannon poke is offline, then sustain through his burst with W Kingslayer","Steal Jayce's R Transformation with Hijack at level 6 and immediately all-in in hammer range before he can swap back to ranged stance"],"foe":["Sit in Mercury Cannon (ranged) stance and land Acceleration Gate + Q poke to shred your HP before you can ever reach him with E","Use Thundering Blow (E) to knock you back the instant your Abduct hook lands, spending your dash cooldown and re-establishing range","Dodge your E hook with his own E, then burst you in hammer form if you're low from poke — Sheen-empowered Q in hammer stance punishes a chunked Sylas hard"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q or W and zero gap-close — Jayce immediately goes Mercury Cannon stance and starts chunking you with Acceleration Gate into Q poke. Do not walk up; farm with Q Chain Lash from max range and accept the poke. Level 2 gives you E, but Jayce's E Thundering Blow knocks you back the moment you land your hook, instantly wasting your only gap-closer and resetting him to range. The core early lesson: never fire Abduct into Jayce when his E is up — watch for it to go on cooldown (short, roughly 13 seconds early), THEN hook. You are being outranged, poked, and kited; stay healthy enough to all-in at 6.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are genuinely contested because your W heal and Petricite Burst autos can match Jayce if you survive the poke, but he still owns the approach — every time you close he has hammer-E knockback or a gated Shock Blast to burst you. At level 6 the matchup opens for one window: Hijack copies his Transformation and gives you the swap burst yourself. His own swap is a 6-second cooldown, so this is never about denying him anything — it is about your damage. Abduct pulls you onto him, W lands the missing-health hit, and passive chains finish it; Jayce caught in hammer range loses that exchange. Force the all-in now, because you will not get a safer window.","late":"With Riftmaker or Shadowflame finished you brawl better than Jayce in sustained melee, and Hijack continues to supply a strong R burst in fights. But Jayce's late-game damage in cannon stance is enormous and he becomes a teamfight-range threat you cannot safely chase without your E — if he saves Thundering Blow, your entire approach is negated. Play the side lane, force Jayce to commit to melee range to stop your push, and punish his stance-swap windows. Never chase Jayce through an open lane at low HP; a single Sheen-empowered hammer Q through an Acceleration Gate ends you.","whys":["Jayce opens Mercury Cannon and his Acceleration Gate + Q poke hits you at range you cannot match with just Q or W — farm from distance and accept chip damage.","Your E Abduct comes online but Thundering Blow (20s at rank 1) knocks you back the instant you land, spending your whole gap-close cooldown for nothing.","Jayce can poke, swap to hammer, all-in, knockback, and reset to range — his full kit in a level-3 skirmish outruns your W heal and leaves you too low to trade back.","A well-played Sylas can brawl in his Passive chain windows if Jayce overextends in hammer form, but Jayce's threat of a gate-Q burst keeps this genuinely even.","Hijack copies his stance swap on an 80-second cooldown and hands you the transform burst — pull in, W, and chain passive autos while he is stuck in hammer range; your strongest window in the lane.","Riftmaker vamp and Passive chain damage let you survive poke and threaten the all-in, but Jayce's Sheen spike in hammer stance mirrors your threat — respect his burst if you are below half HP.","At 2+ items your sustained melee brawl wins extended fights Jayce cannot safely engage, and a stolen R in teamfights is devastating — but one clean knockback into cannon burst still deletes you, so never approach from bad angles."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"karma","win":["Karma","Karma","Skill","Karma","Sylas","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Karma's Mantra-empowered Q (Soulflare) and Focused Resolve (tether) come fully online — she roots you at range before your E can close the gap."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Karma's poke rhythm accelerates as her Mantra recharges faster; you cannot walk up safely without E, and she tethers you the moment you try."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Karma's R gives you Mantra charges on demand — the all-in math flips the moment you can force a fight on her cooldown."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Riftmaker spike lets the W heal absorb her poke while your full chain — E2 hook, Q slow, W lunge, passive whip — threatens a one-rotation kill."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook her with E2 when Focused Resolve (tether) is on cooldown and her Mantra charge is spent","Use E1 shield to eat incoming Q poke before closing, so the trade starts in your favour","All-in at 6 by Hijacking her R and forcing a fight where she has no Mantra empowerment"],"foe":["Root you in Focused Resolve (tether) every time you step up, then walk out of W range while the damage ticks","Bully levels 1-2 with Gathering Fire passive to keep Mantra recharging faster than you can trade","Poke you below W's missing-health heal threshold so one tether root into a Mantra Q kills you outright"]},"early":"Karma owns levels 1 and 2 cleanly. Her Q poke has more range than your Q cross-detonation, and at level 1 you have no gap-closer — every step-up gets poked for free while her Gathering Fire passive lets her recharge Mantra off every spell she lands on you. At level 2 she gains Focused Resolve before you can reliably hook with E, so she tethers you if you try a W-lunge trade and walks out while the root damage ticks. Play entirely at your Q's maximum range, last-hit from a distance, and do not spend E until she commits a Focused Resolve. Your only goal before level 3 is health preservation.","mid":"Level 3 gives you your full kit, but Karma has also reached her level 3 poke ceiling and her Mantra comes back fast enough to punish every trade attempt with a Soulflare root that cancels your follow-through. Levels 4-5 remain hers — she stacks small HP advantages with repeated Q and tether windows that you cannot interrupt without burning E2 into her disengage. The correct pattern is patience: use E1 to shield a Q, let the shield absorb it, then only E2-chain when her Mantra charge is clearly spent and Focused Resolve has at least 8 seconds of cooldown remaining. At 6 the lane inverts — Hijacking her R hands you Mantra charges of your own and turns every all-in into a burst sequence she can no longer kite through.","late":"Post-6 with Riftmaker you hold the lane edge, but Karma transitions smoothly into a teamfight enchanter peeling for carries, which means your Hijack stops being a duelling tool and becomes a teamfight weapon. Use the stolen R to Mantra-boost your own Q into a root during skirmishes, and look to flank onto her carry rather than trying to chase Karma herself through her E move-speed. Your W sustain and passive chain burst are enough to delete squishy targets in two rotations; the longer the game goes, the more your value shifts from lane to catching the backline before her shields can land.","whys":["Karma's Q outranges your entire kit at level 1 and her Gathering Fire passive means every poke refunds Mantra — you have no answer until E arrives.","Focused Resolve comes online before your E hook; she tethers any W-lunge attempt at level 2 and walks out while the root ticks, winning the trade cleanly.","With your full kit you can E2-chain and Q-W into her, but she can tether before your hook lands if she respects the angle — genuine Skill on who predicts whom.","Her Mantra recharges fast enough at levels 4-5 to Soulflare-root every engage attempt; you lose every trade you start unless her Mantra charge is provably spent.","Hijack hands you Mantra charges — you Mantra-boost your own Q into a root, lunge in with W for the missing-health damage, and the fight math is now yours.","Riftmaker spike lets your W heal absorb her residual poke while your full chain threatens a one-rotation kill, but her E shield and move speed keep the duel honest.","Two items of AP plus a stolen Mantra R makes you a teamfight flex; burst the carry through her shield before she can re-cast, and your sustain handles the counter-dive."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"kassadin","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Skill","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"W comes online — the burst-heal converts even trades into wins the moment he steps up to Q you."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit: E1 absorbs his Q poke, then E2 chains you onto him for Q-W-AA — one rotation erases a poke deficit."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you a copy of Riftwalk — the all-in math inverts and every kill attempt snowballs your burst window."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Kassadin's Riftwalk at full stacks lets him blink through your E2 hook and kite your melee range permanently."}],"wants":{"you":["E1 absorb his Q poke, then chain E2 onto him the instant Riftwalk is down","Run W into every trade — the missing-HP heal erases his poke lead before he can reset","Steal his R at 6 and all-in with Ignite before Riftwalk reaches full stack efficiency"],"foe":["Charge Force Pulse off your casts, then cone-slow you out of the trade","Stack Riftwalk charges past 6 so the blink cooldown outpaces your chain hook","Survive to 2 items where his blink frequency and AP burst outpace your W sustain"]},"early":"Level 1 you have Q or W but not both — do not walk into his Q range and eat free magic damage trying to trade. Farm with the tip of your Q and let him burn mana on poke you will heal back anyway. At level 2 W arrives and the calculus changes: his Q poke is missing-HP damage for you now, meaning a half-health Sylas heals more off W than a fresh Kassadin loses. Take any short trade where he wastes the Q into your shield.","mid":"Level 3 is your first real window — E1 absorbs the incoming Q, then E2 hooks him before Riftwalk answers, and the Q-W-AA chain with passive autos is a genuine chunk on any squishy. The key discipline: never spend both E charges before he moves, or you leave yourself a stationary melee caster with no stun. At level 6 Hijack changes the equation completely — you now own a copy of Riftwalk, turning his escape tool into your engage. Use it on cooldown in any skirmish where the math favours you.","late":"Two items and a full Riftwalk stack is where Kassadin slips the leash — his blink cooldown drops low enough that your E2 hook rarely catches him, and his AP burst through Everfrost or Shadowflame lands faster than your W can answer. Play teamfight flex: steal the best ultimate on the enemy roster each fight, not necessarily Kassadin's, and use your superior early lead to have drake or a tower advantage in the bank before he scales into untouchable range.","whys":["Kassadin's Q shield and passive absorb your first magic hit — neither of you has a real trade pattern yet; farm safely and let levels do the work.","W online means his Q poke is now healing resource — step up once he wastes the Q and punish with the burst-heal before he can retaliate.","Full kit: E1 through his Q, E2 chain before Riftwalk fires, Q-W-AA with passive autos — one rotation wins most trades against a pre-6 squishy.","Riftwalk is coming online but only has one charge stacked — respect the blink but keep banking small HP leads with Q and passive autos between waves.","Hijack gives you his Riftwalk — the blink is now on your bar and the all-in threat inverts; look for Ignite all-ins at 6 before his stack count climbs.","Riftmaker spike with a stolen Riftwalk means you can chase any engage angle; force skirmishes in river where his one-shot pattern is harder to set up.","Full Riftwalk at multiple stacks blinks through your E2 hook range and his AP burst outraces your W heal — play teamfight flex and steal the scariest R available rather than chasing Kassadin."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"kayle","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Skill","Skill","Skill","Kayle"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online and your hook-into-W all-in can catch a pre-ranged Kayle completely off guard — she has no escape, no form yet, and your W heal eats her auto damage."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Divine Judgment and gives you a personal invulnerability that lets you walk through her Q-E poke window and re-engage with full health."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Void Corruption converts your W sustain and chain-passive autos into a ticking % health drain that Kayle, who builds zero MR early, cannot trade through."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-ranged Kayle at 2+ items kites your E hook with her passive movement speed and deletes you through Riftmaker's HP before W can save you — the lane debt must be paid by now."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook her with E2 before she hits level 6 and reaches full ranged form, then W-heal through her auto-attack response","Bait Divine Judgment on a fake all-in, then immediately re-engage with your real rotation while the invulnerability is on cooldown","Steal Hijack on cooldown and use your copy of Divine Judgment to absorb her strongest trade window, then run her down"],"foe":["Poke with Q and passive ranged autos to drain your HP below the W heal threshold so your sustain can't rescue a committed all-in","Stall every engagement past level 6 where ranged form, movement-speed passive, and full waveclear make your E hook easy to sidestep","Reach Nashor's Tooth and Rabadon's so her auto-attack DPS exceeds your Riftmaker drain and your W can no longer keep pace"]},"early":"Kayle is one of the softest level-1s in the game — she has no mobility and her Q slow only matters if she can already auto you down. Chain Q on every step-up and punish her CS attempts, but don't blow your E at level 1 when she can just walk away. At level 2 the match flips hard: hook her with E2, land the full Q explosion on her, and W immediately — she has no form shift yet, no invulnerability, and your heal will outsustain whatever autos she lands in response. This is the single cleanest kill window before she builds any safety into the lane.","mid":"Levels 4 through 5 are a chess match around Divine Judgment: she will hold it as a bail-out panic button the moment your E animation starts, so fake the hook, wait for the cast, then go for real. At level 6 you get your own copy — Hijacking Divine Judgment means you can walk through her entire Q-slow-poke rotation untouched and re-engage with a full-health W. That said, she also hits ranged form at 6 and her passive move speed starts making E2 genuinely missable if you telegraph it. Practice the E1-dash-pause-E2-fire micro so the hook fires at max range and she can't juke it with her move speed buff.","late":"With two items Kayle's auto-attack DPS through Nashor's Tooth and on-hit stacking simply races past what your W heal can compensate — your Riftmaker drain is meaningful but she builds no MR and still out-damages you at range before you can complete a full chain. Play for flank angles in teamfights, save Hijack for Divine Judgment to absorb her team's burst, and accept that the 1v1 is off the table. If you snowballed the early windows into objective leads and kept her from stacking, you win the game before this point becomes relevant.","whys":["Kayle is immobile and has no CC or escape at level 1 — Q her on every step-up and deny CS, but hold E since she can simply walk out of hook range with her passive move speed.","E comes online and hook-into-W is a genuine kill threat: she has no ranged form, no Divine Judgment ready, and your W heal absorbs her melee counterattack completely.","Full kit online but she begins kiting your hook with her passive movement speed buff — chain Q into a committed E2 only when she steps up for CS and cannot sidestep cleanly.","Both sides trade around Divine Judgment: she baits your E with the threat of invulnerability, you bait her R cast with a fake engage — the lane is defined by who blinks first.","Hijack copies Divine Judgment, giving you a personal invulnerability that lets you absorb her Q-poke window and re-engage; she also reaches full ranged form here, so hook timing becomes critical.","Riftmaker's Void Corruption + chain-passive autos drain her HP faster than her poke can bleed yours, but she has her own first-item spike in Nashor's Tooth — the power curves cancel and neither side takes a free fight.","Full ranged Kayle with two AP items kites your E hook indefinitely with passive move speed and kills you through Riftmaker's HP before W can save you — the game must be closed before this window opens."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"kennen","win":["Skill","Kennen","Kennen","Kennen","Kennen","Kennen","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals Lightning Rush — use it to close the gap and immediately chain E2 into W before Kennen can re-engage his own R."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's passive healing stacks during the W-into-passive-auto chain, making Kennen's poke deficit impossible to sustain across a full trade."},{"when":"Lvl 2-4","text":"Kennen's W (Electrical Surge) passively marks every third auto — he stacks marks safely at range and can stun you without closing in."},{"when":"Late","text":"With 2+ items Sylas's W heal and stolen R make him the superior teamfighter, but Kennen's AOE stun still punishes any over-extension."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Lightning Rush (E) before committing your own E2 hook — without it he cannot escape or re-engage","Land E2, immediately W for the burst-heal, and weave passive autos to dump the full chain before he can back off","Reach level 6 with a manageable CS deficit, then steal Lightning Rush and use it to close on him for the kill trade"],"foe":["Use Lightning Rush to dodge your E2 hook and reset to safe range before your chain completes","Stack W marks passively with autos and proc the stun mid-trade to interrupt your W cast or passive autos","Poke you below 60% HP so your W heal is maximised for him, not you, and force poor-angle E2 attempts"]},"early":"Level 1 is a wash — you have Q or W but no mobility, and Kennen sits at max auto range stacking Electrical Surge marks without ever needing to step up. Farm with Q and eat the poke without retaliating; every wasted E into auto range is a free stun opportunity for him. The moment he uses Lightning Rush aggressively or to last-hit, note the cooldown — that is your only safe engagement window pre-6.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 Kennen controls this lane cleanly: his W passive marks charge off autos he throws for free at range, and a single E2 attempt that he sees coming ends with you stunned and chunked. Respect the mark count — if you see the lightning bolt icon cycling toward three, back off before he procs the stun. Use Q to chip at him in the narrow windows when Lightning Rush is visibly on cooldown and play for CS rather than kill pressure; your power window has not arrived yet.","late":"Level 6 flips the script: Hijack steals Lightning Rush and hands you the mobility tool that defined his safety net. Use his own E to close, land E2 to stun him, immediately W for the chunk-plus-heal, and run passive autos through the chain — the full rotation kills or forces Flash. First Riftmaker makes the W heal generous enough to eat two Kennen combo rotations and keep fighting; by 2+ items your teamfight value as an AP bruiser with a stealable AOE stun R eclipses his, and you convert the lane lead into objective control.","whys":["Neither of you has meaningful kill threat at level 1 — Kennen autos safely at range to stack Electrical Surge while you farm with Q tip; no engagement windows exist yet.","Lightning Rush online: Kennen can now dodge your E2 at will and proc a three-stack stun, giving him full control of trade timing and distance.","His full kit poke loop — Q into passive autos building Electrical Surge marks — runs at zero risk to him; your only approach tool is E2 and he just dashes through it.","Kennen denies any extended trade by proccing the W stun mid-chain; you cannot sustain a poke deficit safely and your kill threat is too conditional to force.","Lightning Rush is still his — without Hijack you have no answer to his mobility and the stun threat makes every E2 a coin-flip at best.","His poke lead still shapes the lane; your first item spike has not fully arrived, and one well-timed Electrical Surge stun erases a healing advantage.","Hijack gives you Lightning Rush on demand — use his own dash to close the gap, land E2, and W for the win; your teamfight dominance seals the game."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"kled","win":["Sylas","Kled","Kled","Skill","Skill","Kled","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Sylas has Q poke before Kled gets his mounted Q grab online — use this window to chip health and set up the early wave."},{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Kled's mounted Q (Bear Trap on a Rope) comes up and he immediately looks to grab-and-chain you into a full combo; respect it or you eat a dismount sequence."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Kled's R (Chaaaaaaaarge!!!) is a weak steal — a 140s charge that buffs allies but gives no kill threat, so your level-6 spike is blunted compared to most matchups."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With Riftmaker plus a second AP item your W heal becomes a sustained-trade engine that outlasts Kled's remount window and turns drawn-out brawls your way."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E2 AFTER Kled blows his mounted Q grab so you land on a champion who can't immediately chain CC back","Trade in extended bursts using W when his health is low to maximise the missing-HP healing","Survive the mid-game first-item power spike and scale into a two-item brawl Kled cannot outlast"],"foe":["Land Bear Trap on a Rope (Q) to grab and chain-root you before you can E2 out of his combo","Force you to burn E1 on escape so your hook is on cooldown and he can run you down without fear of a stun","Convert a remount proc into a second all-in the moment your W or E2 is on cooldown"]},"early":"At level 1 you have the poke edge — Kled needs his mounted Q (Bear Trap on a Rope) to threaten a real trade, and he does not have it the instant the wave meets. Chip him with Q and passive autos from range to set a small HP lead before he gets the grab. The moment he hits level 2 and has Q, respect the range on that chain; if it snags you, his E follow-up chains a root and he free-autos while you're locked down. Level 3 is genuinely dangerous: his full mounted kit (Q grab into Violent Tendencies W burst plus Jousting E poke) deals more burst than your W can heal back before he re-engages. Play around the Q cooldown — when it is down you can E2 into him and force the trade on your terms.","mid":"The Levels 4-5 window is a genuine skill fight: your W heal and passive-auto weaving can match his burst if you avoid the grab, but one clean Q lands and you are eating his full combo dismounted. Level 6 does not flip this lane the way it does against Malphite or Sion — stealing Kled's R gives you a charge that helps your team but does not threaten Kled's life directly, so you are still playing the ground game. Focus on wave control, deny his all-in by staying just outside Beartrap range, and look for the E2 hook only when you are HP-even or better and his Q is clearly on cooldown. A first-item Riftmaker from Kled means his burst and remount health thresholds both go up; that is the patch of the lane where he outright wins straight brawls.","late":"Two items changes the math in your favour. Your W at this point heals enormous chunks off a low-HP Kled, and your chain of E2 stun into Q slow into W burst can delete him before he procs a second remount if you are ahead. The catch: Kled's R engages fights on his terms, not yours — never get caught in the open mid-lane without your E2 available, because a full-team Chaaaaarge!!! initiated over you ends the fight before you can steal anything. Play splitpush angles that force him to duel rather than engage, and when he does duel, burn his remount in the first all-in and finish him before Skaarl comes back.","whys":["Kled has no mounted Q at level 1 — use Q chain poke freely and build the HP lead you will need when his grab comes online.","Bear Trap on a Rope is live and Kled immediately looks for the grab-chain-root combo; eating it at level 2 burns your E1 or costs you 30% health.","His full kit (Q grab, E poke, W burst) outputs more damage than your W can heal in one rotation; play around his Q cooldown, not into it.","Neither kit has a decisive edge here — Kled's remount mechanic resets the duel if you do not finish him, but your W sustain keeps you even through drawn-out exchanges.","Stealing Kled's R gives you a charge engage, not a kill tool — the spike you counted on versus Malphite or Volibear simply is not here, so this is still a dogfight.","First-item Riftmaker on Kled inflates his burst and pushes his remount threshold higher; he wins the straight 1v1 here if you let him dictate the fight.","Two AP items and your W become an outlast engine — force a prolonged duel, burn his remount, and finish Skaarl-less Kled before he resets."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"ksante","win":["Skill","Skill","K'Sante","K'Sante","Skill","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"K'Sante's Q stacks into a knockup punish you hard if you stand still — bait the third Q hit before you commit your E hook."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking K'Sante's R neutralises his all-in window; use stolen R to isolate HIM while your W heals through his percent-HP damage."},{"when":"1st item","text":"K'Sante's Heartsteel and first defensive item spike his bulk faster than Riftmaker patches your damage — trade only on favourable cooldowns."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items Sylas's W heal cannot keep pace with K'Sante's percent-HP passive and fighter stats; avoid extended fights without a lead."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook K'Sante mid-Q animation with E Abduct so his knockup whiffs and you land W into the stunned window","Steal R at level 6 and immediately use it to drag K'Sante out of his own W dash path, flipping his all-in","Keep trades short pre-6 — chip with Q slow, disengage before his W block absorbs your burst"],"foe":["Stack Q charges at range to land the third-hit knockup before you can hook him","Use W to block your W lunge and dash through you, resetting a favourable trade angle","Survive to 2+ items where his percent-HP passive and transformed fighter stats outpace your healing"]},"early":"At level 1 neither of you wants a straight fight — K'Sante's Q poke is safe and his W already blocks a chunk of your damage. Stand at Q range, land the slow, and back off; do not spend E here because a missed hook puts you in his face with no escape. The moment E comes online at level 2, you have your first real threat: hook him mid-Q animation to cancel the knockup, then immediately W for the missing-health burst and chain Passive autos. He cannot W-block what he did not see coming. Still, avoid extended trades — his tankiness eats prolonged all-ins before you have AP.","mid":"Levels 3-5 are where K'Sante consolidates his advantage. He can stack Q charges from range, land the knockup, and then W-dash through you to reset position — if you get knocked up you eat the full combo with no counter-play. Respect the third Q hit: the moment you see him at two stacks, either E away or bait him to fire it into a minion. When HIS Q is down, short trades with Q-slow into W are fine; just disengage before W comes back up. At level 6 the lane resets: stolen R lets you use K'Sante's own isolation mechanic against him, pulling him into your threat range while your W heals through his burst. This is the window to look for a kill — all-in immediately after he wastes W on a minion.","late":"Past first items K'Sante's Heartsteel bulk makes your W healing increasingly irrelevant — his percent-HP passive shreds your HP bar faster than you recover it in long fights. Play around cooldowns: if you land E and immediately W you still delete a big chunk of his HP, but disengage the moment he pops W-dash. In teamfights, steal his R to prevent the isolation all-in on your carry and use it yourself; that is your highest-value play at this stage, not a side-lane duel you will likely lose. If you built a kill lead in the level-6 window, spend it on objectives before his item scaling negates it.","whys":["Neither kit shines at level 1 — K'Sante's Q needs stacks and your E isn't up; farm with Q and deny him a clean Q trade.","E comes online but K'Sante's W block and early bulk absorb your burst; short trades only until you confirm his W is on cooldown.","K'Sante's Q three-stack knockup locks you in place and his W-dash repositions out of your follow-up — he dictates the trade pattern.","He stacks Q freely at range and you have no answer to the knockup if you step up; stay back and Q-farm until level 6.","Hijacking K'Sante's R flips his all-in: you isolate him with his own mechanic and W heals through the burst he expected to delete you with.","Riftmaker's healing synergizes with your W but K'Sante's first-item bulk still absorbs it; trade only on his W cooldown and stay even.","The side-lane duel stays his, but two items plus a stolen All Out makes you the better teamfighter — flank, isolate his carry, and never trade him 1v1."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"lillia","win":["Skill","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"E comes online and your hook-into-W combo can catch Lillia mid-kite before her passive speed builds — one successful E2 at level 2 is your first real window to go even or ahead."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Lilting Lullaby flips the gank equation entirely — your jungler now has a sleep setup and Lillia loses the tool that made her dangerous."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks on top of W's heal, letting you tank the Dream Dust burn and win extended brawls Lillia cannot end quickly."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5 Lillia spike","text":"Lillia's passive speed and Q outer-ring poke let her kite your E approach freely before you stabilize — respect her movement in the open lane and bait her to stop moving before you commit."}],"wants":{"you":["Land E2 hook while Lillia is stationary or funnelled into a wall so her passive kite speed cannot save her","Burn W on a hooked Lillia who has already used her E to slow you, locking in the trade before she escapes","Hijack Lilting Lullaby at level 6 and use it with your jungler to instantly erase the main reason she wins ganks"],"foe":["Q outer-ring on every step-up to stack Dream Dust burn before you can use E to close","Kite circles to keep passive speed active and deny your hook angle without ever stopping to fight","Use R Lilting Lullaby to call ganks or sleep you mid-trade so her jungler can clean up what her kit started"]},"early":"At level 1 you have no hook, so don't walk into Q outer-ring range just to last-hit — Lillia's Dream Dust passive burn is free damage she is always banking. Play at the absolute edge of Q range, throw the chain on her step-ups, and accept that she will move faster than you can chase. Level 2 is your first moment of real agency: the instant E unlocks, look for a wall or a wave corner that limits her lateral drift, then commit E1-dash, E2-hook, W into her missing-health pool. Her passive speed only works if she is already moving — catch her mid-animation and the trade is yours. By level 3 your full kit means the sustained rotation of E2-Q explosion-W heal-passive autos beats her if she stays in range, but she will not stay unless you force her to.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are about respect and positioning: Lillia can loop Q rings indefinitely while her Dream Dust ticks you toward a bad all-in, so keep W for the reactive burst-heal rather than burning it as a poke. Every time she stops her passive speed to last-hit or cast E slow on you, that is your window — hook in, dump the chain, W for the heal. At level 6 the lane flips decisively: steal Lilting Lullaby the instant it is available, not just to deny her sleep but to give your own jungler a free dive tool. She loses her primary gank-magnet threat and you now dictate whether the next gank goes in your favour or hers.","late":"With Riftmaker active and Hijack on cooldown you are the stronger solo threat: your W heal absorbs the Dream Dust burn that lets her trade from range and your chain passive autos let you run down a Lillia who cannot permanently disengage in a side lane. She becomes a teamfight kiter in the late game, but you match that role with a stolen sleep and AP bruiser bulk — play through the frontline, E2 onto her if she over-extends, and her R is always on your bar to win any engage her team wants to start.","whys":["Lillia's Q outer-ring poke is free at level 1 and you have no hook to answer it — stay at Q chain range and build no HP debt before E arrives.","E2 hook online — the first window to catch her mid-passive-drift; a wall or wave corner removes her escape angle and W seals the trade in your HP advantage.","Full kit means E2-hook into the Q explosion slow into W burst-heal beats her burst if you land the hook before her speed is already rolling.","She can loop Q rings all day at 4-5, but every time she pauses to W sweetspot or E slow you she stops moving — that frozen moment is your hook angle.","Hijacking Lilting Lullaby removes her gank-invite threat and puts a sleep tool on your bar — the lane is now yours to all-in at will.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacks with W heal and lets you win the sustained brawl the Dream Dust burn was supposed to end in her favour.","Two items and a stolen sleep make you a teamfight flex she cannot simply kite away from — your omnivamp absorbs her burn and your chain reaches her before passive speed saves her."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"lucian","win":["Lucian","Lucian","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Lucian's passive double-shot and E dash let him punish every time you step up before your E is online — eat the poke and don't give free autos."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once your full kit is live, bait his E dash with a faked Q approach, then hook with E Abduct and land W Kingslayer before he can reposition."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Lucian's R Culling gives you a sustained ranged damage window he cannot answer in close range — use it immediately to threaten the all-in."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's stacking damage combined with W heal means extended trades go firmly your way; force fights after the first back."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E Abduct after baiting his E dash so he has no escape from your W all-in","Force extended trades where W Kingslayer heal outpaces his double-shot burst","Steal his R Culling at 6 and use it to threaten a kill before he can safely kite"],"foe":["Burn your E Abscond dash with his Q poke so you have no engage tool when he all-ins","Kite backward and use his E dash to stay outside your W lunge range all lane","Land his passive double-shot proc after every ability to chunk your HP before you can W heal"]},"early":"You have no safe way to get on Lucian before level 2 — his Q poke and passive double-shot punish any step inside his auto range, and you have no gap-closer yet. Take Q at level 1, farm from max range, and track his E dash cooldown rather than walking into his range. At level 2 your E Abscond comes online and you have a theoretical engage, but Lucian's E lets him dodge your hook easily if he respects it — only use it if he overextends past a minion wave that cuts off his sidestep angle.","mid":"Levels 3-5 are your first real window: full kit, W heal, and chain Petricite autos make extended trades go your way if you land the hook. The key read is his E dash — Lucian wants to dash sideways or back when you commit your E Abscond forward dash, then double-shot you while your engage is on cooldown. Fake the approach, let him dash, then immediately fire E Abduct into his landing spot and follow with W Kingslayer before he repositions. At level 6 steal his R Culling; you can now threaten him with sustained ranged damage at close range, a range bracket where he can't safely proc his passive double-shot without walking into your W.","late":"Riftmaker's stacking magic damage combined with W Kingslayer sustain means you outlast him in any fight that goes longer than his initial burst rotation, and you keep his R off the map for your team. The genuine risk is that Lucian remains relevant well into the late game — he is not a scaler who falls off, and his E dash lets him dodge your hooks in open-map teamfights far more reliably than in lane. Prioritise using E Abduct to initiate rather than escape so his E is the reactive tool, not yours, and always confirm his dash is on cooldown before committing your hook in a sidelane skirmish.","whys":["Lucian's Q poke and passive double-shot chip you down before you have a gap-closer — last-hit at Q Chain Lash range and never step into auto range without a plan.","His E dash is up and he can sidestep your E Abduct hook cleanly; he double-shots twice before you recover, making this still his window.","Full kit access and W Kingslayer heal start to offset his poke, but he can still kite your hook if his E is up — this window is genuinely contested.","You can bait his E dash and punish the landing spot with E Abduct into W; with his mobility spent, extended trades now favour your chain-passive sustain.","Stealing Culling hands you a sustained-damage tool at melee range where he cannot safely proc his own passive — look to all-in immediately after the steal.","Riftmaker's stacking bonus and W heal outpace his short-rotation damage; force the extended fight and he runs out of burst before you run out of HP.","You keep the edge through items: Lucian never truly falls off, but Riftmaker sustain and a stolen R mean he cannot freely out-trade you the way he did at levels 1-2."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"malphite","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Malphite","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — hook him into a W-Q burst and his armor passive does nothing against your magic damage."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full kit with W sustain: chain Q poke into E2 hook trades drain him faster than his base regen or W armor buff can answer."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Malphite's Unstoppable Force is one of the strongest teamfight ultimates — he banks it here and can deny your forward steps with the threat alone."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Riftmaker stacks accelerate on every W cast; two items plus a banked stolen Unstoppable Force makes you a teamfight weapon he can never take back."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 and follow immediately with W-Q-passive autos so your magic damage bypasses his stacked armor","Bait Unstoppable Force before committing your E charges — once it is on cooldown the lane is yours to run down","Steal his R at level 6 and bank it for the next objective or river skirmish so his win condition becomes yours"],"foe":["Bank Unstoppable Force and threaten the engage so you cannot step forward through Q slow poke","Stack armor via W passive and let your Q whittle you down in the farm lane until he can afford Sunfire or Thornmail","Land Unstoppable Force on your team in a teamfight to flip an objective before you can steal it back"]},"early":"At level 1 Malphite only has Q — the slow poke hurts but you take zero magic damage and your base MR shrugs off the chip. Farm with your own Q at range and deny his step-ups without burning your E. The moment you hit level 2 and E is on your bar, the lane changes: hook him with E2, land W-Q, weave passive autos, and walk out healed. His armor stacks do nothing against your magic damage chain, and his W armor buff only helps against your auto-attacks — your spells ignore it entirely.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are your clearest window. Full-kit Sylas runs Malphite out of the lane: Q slow into E1 shield dash, E2 hook, W for the burst-heal and the missing-health amp, then passive autos to close. Keep track of his Unstoppable Force cooldown — it is his only real answer once he is behind on HP, and he will bank it rather than use it offensively in this matchup. Shove waves, deny CS, and keep him low so your W heals for maximum value. At 6 the dynamic shifts: Malphite holds his ultimate as a teamfight threat and the threat alone freezes your aggression. Respect the engage window and do not walk into his R range with your E charges already spent.","late":"At two items the lane math returns to your favor hard. Riftmaker stacks on every W cast and your AP scales the burst-heal — extended fights in the side lane are almost unwinnable for a pure-armor Malphite who cannot touch your HP through magic damage. More importantly, stealing Unstoppable Force turns every Baron or Dragon fight into your engage rather than his. Land the stolen R onto their backline, follow with your own E2 hook on whatever survives, and Malphite goes from the team's engagement win condition to a walking armor sponge with no ultimate.","whys":["His Q slow is annoying but deals magic damage you largely ignore at base MR — your own Q out-ranges his step-ups and you do not need to commit E to punish him.","E comes online and your kit deals pure magic damage: E2 hook into W-Q immediately wins the trade because his stacked armor does absolutely nothing to reduce your burst.","Full kit plus W sustain — you chunk him with Q, hook with E2, burst-heal with W, and walk out healthier than before while his W armor buff only reduces your auto-attack damage.","Q slow poke plus the latent threat of Unstoppable Force keeps you from stepping up recklessly, but your magic damage trades still favor you heavily and he cannot all-in without burning his R.","He banks Unstoppable Force here and the threat of a 3-man engage freezes your forward steps — respect the cooldown, do not walk in with both E charges spent, and let him hold it.","Riftmaker first is your brawling consolidation but Malphite's Sunfire or Thornmail spike also lands here — the one-versus-one is genuine enough that whoever catches the other overextended wins it.","Two items plus a stolen Unstoppable Force is a teamfight weapon he can never reclaim — your W heal and Riftmaker stacks win the extended side-lane duel, and his engage ultimate now belongs to you."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"maokai","win":["Skill","Maokai","Maokai","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Maokai's W root can intercept your E hook on landing, shutting down your only engage before you can W-heal — respect his root timing before committing."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Nature's Grasp gives you a global root wave that Maokai can never use against you — look for an all-in the moment you hit 6."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker online: your W heals for massive chunks off Maokai's missing HP, and his passive sustain can no longer keep pace with your burst-heal combo."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your E-hook into W burst and Passive chain autos out-damage his regeneration entirely — you win any extended 1v1 he tries to force."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E when Maokai's W root is on cooldown — his root is his only tool to stop your approach","Chain W-heal immediately after landing your hook to erase his sapling chip damage and sustain through his Q knockback","Steal Nature's Grasp at 6 and use the global root wave to set up kills or objectives, turning his best teamfight tool against him"],"foe":["Root you mid-E with his W as you hook in, stranding you in melee with his Q knockback ready","Chip you down with saplings in brush before you can get in range to hook, wearing your HP below W-heal threshold","Out-sustain your early trades with his passive healing so you cannot convert short poke windows into kill pressure"]},"early":"At level 1 you have no engage — Maokai is content to sapling-poke you from distance and recover every last-hit with his passive sustain while you sit on Q or W with no way to chase. Do not burn a W on a fleeting trade you cannot follow up; last-hit safely and watch your HP so his saplings do not put you below W-heal range before you have a reason to commit. The moment you hit level 2 and E comes online, resist the urge to hook immediately — Maokai's W root is fast and targeted, and if he presses it the instant your chain lands he roots you in front of his Q knockback, losing you the trade entirely. Fake the approach to bait his W cooldown first, then hook.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a genuine grind: Maokai has his full sapling-Q-W kit and his passive healing keeps him topped off between trades, while your mana limits how often you can W-heal back into fighting shape. Play for the W cooldown window — after he uses it to peel your last approach it sits at 11 seconds, and that is your all-in window: E-hook, chain Passive autos, W for the heal and damage, Q-poke on retreat. Do not extend past one rotation. Level 6 changes everything: Maokai's Nature's Grasp is one of the best Sylas steals in the top lane — a long-range global root wave. Activate it to zone him off the wave, then E-hook into W all-in while he has no R. This is the lane's defining moment; do not sit on the stolen ult.","late":"Once Riftmaker comes online the sustain mismatch flips hard in your favor: your W heals off Maokai's large HP pool for chunks that outpace his passive regen, and each Passive-empowered auto in an extended fight adds up faster than his saplings can chip. The only thing to respect is that Maokai becomes a setup tank in teamfights and will try to W-root you into his team — do not E-hook into a cluster where his W pins you for follow-up CC. Take the side lane 1v1 or follow his roam and use the stolen Nature's Grasp to stall or flip a fight before his root wave can do real work for his team.","whys":["Neither champion has an engage at level 1 — Maokai saplings chip your HP but your passive Petricite autos and last-hitting range keep it even until E comes online.","Maokai's W root goes live at level 2 just as your E hook does — he can press W the instant your chain connects, rooting you in front of his Q knockback and winning the exchange.","Maokai's full sapling-W-Q kit is online and his passive sustain erases the poke damage you deal between trades, leaving you net negative in every short exchange without a kill window.","The W root cooldown creates brief 11-second kill windows but Maokai's passive healing closes the HP gap quickly — neither can convert reliably and the lane stalemates until 6.","Stealing Nature's Grasp gives you a global root wave that is devastating from Sylas's scaling and leaves Maokai without his best teamfight tool, flipping every all-in in your favor.","Riftmaker's AP and Omnivamp mean your W now heals for massive chunks off Maokai's large HP pool — his passive regen cannot compete, and you win every sustained trade.","At full build your E-hook into W burst plus Passive chain autos out-damages Maokai's regeneration entirely, and the stolen Nature's Grasp remains the strongest ult in the side-lane standoff."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"mel","win":["Skill","Mel","Mel","Mel","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Mel's ranged Q poke and W zone pressure are at their most oppressive before you have E — she can freely chip you down while you lack the hook to punish."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Mel's R turns her reflect tool into your own weapon; schedule all-ins around this window before she builds Seraph's."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Mel's Shadowflame or Luden's spike dramatically increases her burst potential, letting her one-rotate you if you've been poked below half health."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With Riftmaker and Shadowflame yourself, your W heal and chain combo finally outpace her poke attrition — the extended 1v1 swings decisively to you."}],"wants":{"you":["Shield through her ranged Q poke with E1, then E2-chain onto her the instant Rebuttal is on cooldown","Steal her R with Hijack and flip the reflect threat onto her team in skirmishes","Stack W heals in extended trades to erase the poke deficit she built levels 1-5"],"foe":["Keep Rebuttal active to reflect your Q chains and punish any projectile you throw","Zone you off the wave with W execute threat and W zone so you bleed CS and HP simultaneously","Kill you before level 6 while you have no impactful ultimate to steal"]},"early":"Level 1 is a standoff — your Q is the only tool and Mel's ranged Q outranges it cleanly, so CS with Q tips and accept that she sets the pace. At level 2 her W zone and E come online and she will start actively winning the lane: Rebuttal reflects your Q chains back at you, so never throw a Q into its glow. By level 3 she has her full kit and owns the lane decisively — she can zone, poke, and threaten a W execute on any trade attempt. Your job is to take as little damage as possible: shield her poke with E1, hold E2 for escape, and farm with the Q tip from max range. Do not burn E to engage here; her range advantage is too wide and Rebuttal punishes any projectile approach.","mid":"Levels 4-5 she still has the edge — her burst grows with levels and Rebuttal stays on a manageable cooldown, so the lane remains hers. Wait for Rebuttal to come off cooldown and visually expire before you commit your E2 hook; one clean E2 stun into Q-W-passive chain is your entire trade pattern and it only works when she cannot immediately reflect your Q. At level 6 the lane becomes contested: you can steal her R, but Mel's ultimate reflects targeted projectiles and her R has a large radius, so its direct value for Sylas is situation-dependent — what matters is denying her the ability to reflect your burst in the all-in. Hook in with E2 on Rebuttal's downtime, burn your rotation into the W heal, and her lack of a hard escape makes the all-in stick.","late":"Your first-item timing is when Mel wins most decisively — Shadowflame or Luden's lets her one-rotate a poked Sylas, and she will look for that window religiously. Survive to your own Riftmaker and the math flips: your W scales off her missing health and your sustain outheals her sustained poke in the extended fights she cannot avoid once you're on her. In teamfights the stolen R becomes genuinely impactful — a Sylas holding Mel's reflect in a late-game skirmish forces the enemy team to rethink every projectile they throw, which is a teamfight-warping tool on its own terms. Stay healthy, avoid her zoning W in the side lane, and the 2+ item window is yours to dominate.","whys":["Neither champion has a clean engage at level 1 — Mel's Q outranges yours but Rebuttal is irrelevant when you aren't throwing chains yet; farm safely and take nothing for free.","Mel's W zone and ranged Q let her start dictating spacing; Rebuttal now reflects your Q on any step-up, so she owns the lane from this point.","Full Mel kit with Rebuttal, W execute, and E zone pressure gives her the clearest edge of the lane — she can zone, poke, and punish any E2 attempt you try.","Pre-6 Mel continues to win with range advantage and Rebuttal coverage; she pokes you through shields and your W heal cannot keep pace with sustained ranged harassment.","Hijack takes Golden Eclipse, which strikes every Overwhelm-marked enemy at any range — you get her own detonation on an 80-second cooldown and she has no dash to leave your E2 chain.","Riftmaker lands and the W heal starts outpacing her chip; her Shadowflame only one-rotates a Sylas already poked low, so approach on Rebuttal's downtime and fight at full health.","Your Riftmaker sustain and chain combo finally outlast her poke attrition in extended fights she cannot escape — E2 in on Rebuttal's downtime and the W heal closes it."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"mordekaiser","win":["Skill","Mordekaiser","Mordekaiser","Skill","Mordekaiser","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Mordekaiser's Q isolated bonus and W shield come fully online — he out-trades you in extended melee exchanges before you have mana or sustain to match him."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mordekaiser's Realm of Death pulls you into a 1v1 duel and steals a chunk of your stats — you lose this isolated fight at six unless you can burn his shield before he casts R."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Omnivamp stacks through your W Kingslayer heals and extended trades, letting you out-sustain Mordekaiser and flip the brawl in your favour."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Two AP items make your stolen Realm of Death stronger than his own — Hijack his R to exile him while your Riftmaker heals keep you healthy through the duel."}],"wants":{"you":["Abduct onto him only once Indestructible is spent or eaten, then Kingslayer immediately for the missing-HP damage before he rebuilds it","Build Riftmaker, then Grievous Wounds — Indestructible's heal is what wins him the extended trades","Dodge his E pull — if he cannot isolate you for his Q bonus damage, his whole trade pattern falls apart"],"foe":["Land Death's Grasp (E) to drag you in, then Obliterate (Q) for its solo bonus","Bank Indestructible (W) by trading repeatedly, then eat it for the heal that wins the extended brawl","Cast R Realm of Death when you are below half HP to steal your stats and finish you in the duel"]},"early":"At level one neither of you has a complete kit, but Mordekaiser's Q slam already punishes you if you walk into his melee range without your E available yet — respect his auto-Q poke and last-hit from max range. His W Iron Man shield ramps off his own ability usage, so even short trades favour him once that shield is up. At level three his E pull comes online and the matchup becomes genuinely dangerous: if he E-pulls you he immediately triggers his Q isolated bonus damage, and you are stuck eating both while his W absorbs your counterplay. Keep the wave slightly pushed so you have room to E-dash through or away from his E pull rather than getting pinned against your own tower wall.","mid":"Once he hits six, Mordekaiser's Realm of Death is a death sentence if you are below half health — he exiles you, steals a chunk of your stats, and the 1v1 happens entirely on his terms with no jungle intervention possible. Your E Abduct hook does still work inside the Realm, so if he R's you, immediately E-hook back onto him, burn W Kingslayer on his missing health, and look to burst him before the stolen stats widen the gap. The matchup tempo shifts in your favour at first item: Riftmaker Omnivamp stacking through repeated W heals means you out-sustain him in extended trades he used to win, and your Q slow sets up free E-hook angles he has no mobility to dodge.","late":"With two items your Hijacked Realm of Death hits harder than his own version because it scales off your AP and Ability Haste rather than his, and your Riftmaker healing means you survive the opening exchanges that used to kill you at six. Force the all-in with E Abduct to chain onto him and open with W Kingslayer immediately — the missing-health scaling on W burns through his Indestructible buffer before he can bank it back. In teamfights, stealing his R lets you exile their frontline carry while he sits useless without his ultimate, which is the real reason this matchup is net positive for you despite a rough laning phase.","whys":["Neither champion has their full kit and Mordekaiser cannot yet trigger his E-into-Q isolated-bonus combo — farm safely with Q Chain Lash at max range and match his level-one Q poke without committing.","Mordekaiser's W Iron Man shield is already building and his Q isolated bonus rewards any melee exchange — your E is online but his shield absorbs your burst before you can W Kingslayer for meaningful damage.","His full E-pull into Q isolated bonus into W-shielded sustain is now live and punishes every extended trade — dodge his E with your own E dash and do not contest him in melee without a health advantage.","Both kits are fully active but neither has the item power to decisively end the other — trade short, keep your W Kingslayer heal ready, and avoid getting E-pulled near your own tower where his Q isolated bonus hurts most.","Realm of Death exiles you and steals your stats at the exact moment you have no item sustain to outlast the duel — respect the all-in threat and use E Abduct immediately inside the Realm to burst him before the stat difference widens.","Riftmaker Omnivamp compounds through every W Kingslayer heal in extended trades, flipping the sustain math that favoured him in lane — force repeated brawls now and his W shield can no longer keep pace with your healing.","Two AP items make your Hijacked Realm of Death stronger than his original cast and your Riftmaker keeps you healthy through the opening burst — steal his R, exile him, and convert the lead he built in lane into a decisive teamfight win condition."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"nasus","win":["Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"W comes online and your burst-heal flips even trades — hook in and sustain through his Q farm poke."},{"when":"Lvls 4-5","text":"Passive chain-autos between Q and W rotations build a real HP lead before Wither gets long enough to hurt you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you a copy of Fury of the Sands — the health, resists and percent-health damage are yours, but his own R is untouched, so all-in only while both it and Wither are down."},{"when":"Late","text":"Three-stack Nasus with Wither cripples your approach speed and Q damage — respect the stat check and avoid 1v1 past 25 minutes."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2 when Wither is on cooldown, then chain Q-W-passive autos for the extended trade","Steal his R at 6 and all-in early — Fury of the Sands burns percent health, so armour will not save you","Keep Q poke pressure pre-6 to deny free Q stacks and force him off the wave"],"foe":["Land Wither before you can hook to freeze you in place and shred attack speed for the counter-trade","Free-farm Q stacks with zero trades and hit the three-item power spike that walls out your W healing","Play under tower and deny your all-in angle until his scaling completely outpaces your AP burst"]},"early":"Level 1 you have Q only — chain it on his feet when he steps up to stack his Q, but don't walk into melee range looking for a fight you can't finish yet. He has no mobility and his W is his only real combat tool, so use that first level to establish where he likes to stand and deny a few free stacks. At level 2 W arrives and the math shifts immediately: his Q stacks are worth nothing if he's dead, and your burst-heal can flip what looks like an even trade into a comfortable HP lead. Short extended trades where you land Q slow, auto-chain passive, then W heal are his nightmare because he has no burst to end them quickly.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are your window to convert poke into a real lead — weave passive chain-autos between every Q and W cast so your damage compounds without giving him a clean Wither angle. The key discipline is Wither management: it is a 700-range point-and-click on an 11-15s cooldown, so the moment you see the cast, use E1 to dash forward (closing distance kills the kite), then E2-hook back out before the attack-speed decay ruins your counter-trade. At level 6 Hijack copies Fury of the Sands rather than taking it — you gain the health, resists and percent-health burn, and so does he if he presses his own. Run the all-in while his 120s R is still down.","late":"Past two items Nasus's stacked Q, Wither duration, and raw HP begin to outpace your W healing — his drain-fight becomes a true-damage wall you can't sustain through. Avoid straight 1v1s unless you have a freshly-stolen R to burn. In teamfights your value is flanking the backline with Hijack, not face-tanking a 800-stack Nasus who can Wither you and Q you into the floor. Convert your mid-game lead into Dragon or Baron control so his late-game scaling never gets to matter.","whys":["Q is your only tool and it only slow-pokes — last-hit safely with Q tip and deny his free Q stacks without committing to a melee brawl you cannot finish.","W online turns short extended trades into net-positive HP swings; hook in only when Wither is down so his slow cannot stop your chain-auto pattern.","Full kit: E2 hook into Q slow, W burst-heal, passive autos is a real combo that halves him — trade in full rotations and E1 out before Wither punishes you.","Passive chain-autos between every spell multiply your damage without extending your exposure; bank HP leads now by respecting Wither's timing, not ignoring it.","Hijack copies Fury of the Sands — you get the health, resists and percent-health burn, but he keeps his own R, so commit only while his 120s ultimate is still down.","Riftmaker spike deepens your W healing and AP ratio enough that even a contested trade into a partial Wither is survivable — look for full rotations on his cooldowns.","Stacked Nasus with Wither and three items outscales your W healing; play for teamfight flanks with a stolen R rather than soloing a stat-check you will lose."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"nautilus","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Nautilus","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"E comes online at 2 — E2 chain into W punishes every step he takes to CS, and at 3 your full kit lets you sustain through his passive-root autos and trade back harder."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Depth Charge is a point-click, unmissable knockup — Nautilus R-flashes on you the moment his jungler arrives, and your Hijack doesn't prevent him firing it first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Mythic passive and stacking omnivamp lock in your extended-trade dominance — W heal on a tanky Nautilus returns massive HP and you simply out-sustain him."},{"when":"Late","text":"Depth Charge into a teamfight initiator role keeps Nautilus relevant at 2+ items — respect his engage and steal his R for your own backline picks."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2 after Dredge Line is on cooldown to guarantee a free W-heal trade","Bully him off CS with Q poke and passive autos so he falls behind before 6","Steal Depth Charge at 6 and use it to peel for your carries or open teamfight angles"],"foe":["Land Dredge Line before you can get in range to start E2, punishing your approach","Use passive anchor-root on autos to stack short CC and deny your W access","Force the all-in during Abscond's 9-13s downtime, when you have no way to leave"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q only — poke him on his CS steps but never walk into auto range where his passive root activates on the first hit. He can't meaningfully engage without Dredge Line landing, so just farm, poke, and accept the neutral. At 2 your E arrives and the matchup tilts: E1 forward to close distance, then immediately E2-chain onto him while Dredge Line is still on its 14-second cooldown — follow with W for the burst-heal and walk with passive autos. He can't root you without the hook and he has no dash to escape. Level 3 cements it: full kit means every trade cycle nets you HP through W while costing him more than he returns.","mid":"The levels 4-5 window is pure farm bullying — your Q slow keeps him in Abduct range, Conqueror stacks fast in extended trades, and W on a tanky Nautilus returns a genuinely alarming amount of HP. Keep Dredge Line's 14-10s timer in your head: the moment it fires and misses, that's your green light to chain in and go. At 6 caution is warranted — Depth Charge is point-click at 825 range, and Nautilus can fire it INTO your engage, knocking you up out of the rotation you already spent. He may also have his jungler waiting. Take the E2-W-Q trade, don't commit to an all-in at 6, and decide whether to Hijack his R for later teamfight use or for an immediate 2v2.","late":"With Riftmaker finished you are the stronger solo fighter — W's omnivamp against his health-stacking kit means every drawn-out duel goes your way, and a Depth Charge in your hands is a point-click single-target knockup that opens any teamfight you want. Respect his own R, however: if he has it up and your jungler is not nearby, do not over-commit to a solo kill. Shove side lanes with your E2 threat keeping him honest, and look to steal his R before every Dragon or Baron contest so the teamfight punch belongs to you, not him.","whys":["Only Q is available and his passive-root auto is dangerous at close range — farm with Q tip, deny him nothing, and wait for E.","E2 chain into W-heal is online and Dredge Line is your only real danger — trade every time it misses and walk away healthy.","Full kit: E1 closes the gap, E2 chains you onto him, the Q slow lands free and W heals the exchange back — with no dash he cannot leave it.","Your extended-trade engine is running and Dredge Line cooldown is your metronome — bank small leads every time it hits the floor.","Depth Charge is point-click and fires before your combo resolves — he can knockup into your E2 engage and invite his jungler; don't over-commit to the solo kill.","Riftmaker omnivamp on a tank with a massive HP pool means W heals for massive chunks — the 1v1 is yours to take on demand.","Two items on and your sustain still scales, but steal Depth Charge for teamfight value and keep pressing side lanes rather than fighting him straight under his tower."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"neeko","win":["Skill","Skill","Neeko","Skill","Neeko","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Neeko's Q-E-Q burst combo comes fully online and her root lets her land every hit — respect her all-in range and don't stand in Q overlap zones."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Pop Blossom is one of the best things Sylas can steal — a leap that knocks up and then stuns everything nearby, so Hijack it the moment she spends her own 90-120s copy."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Neeko's Shadowflame or Luden's spike makes her one-rotation burst lethal; if she lands E-root you take the full Q-Q-R sequence through it."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Sylas's Riftmaker sustain and stacked Petricite Burst autos let him brawl through her burst if he can get on her, and her R in his hands becomes his primary teamfight carry tool."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E Abduct only after her E root is on cooldown so she cannot interrupt your W lunge","Force short extended trades where W Kingslayer healing absorbs her Q poke without giving her space to land a clean E-root combo","Steal Pop Blossom and open fights with it — the knockup into stun is her best tool and Hijack recharges in 30-80s"],"foe":["Root you with E Tangle-Barbs mid-dash so your chain whiffs and you take the full burst with no W heal","Poke you below 60% HP with Q Blooming Burst before committing to an all-in so her W passive bonus damage and R one-shot threshold shrink","Send a Shapesplitter clone before your E lands so you chain the decoy and waste the engage"]},"early":"At level 1 she is ranged and you are not — she walks up and drops Q petals on your feet while you try to CS, and you have no answer yet. Just last-hit from outside the petal overlap zone and accept that she dictates spacing. Level 2 changes nothing fundamental: she will root you the instant your E chain appears in the air, so do not blindly dash at her. The only early trade that works is if she wastes E on a creep or mis-times it — then your chain lands, W Kingslayer fires, and you can chain Passive autos before she resets range.","mid":"Levels 4-5 she still has range advantage and a root that counters your only engage tool, so the matchup stays in her favor. At 6 the script flips: her R is a disguised AoE stun leap that is genuinely one of the better steals in the game for Sylas. The moment she uses it aggressively and you see the animation, trade back and grab Hijack — you now have a teamfight-winning nuke she does not. Force short trades with W when her E is down; never dive in when she is sitting on root and full HP because the E-root into Q-Q burst sequence will cut through your W heal window.","late":"Late game Sylas holding Pop Blossom is a genuine teamfight threat — Hijack copies the leap, the knockup and the landing stun, though not her disguise, so you enter on your own terms with Abscond instead. Your Riftmaker sustain means you survive one rotation if you go in at full HP, and Petricite Burst autos in extended brawls let you shred her once she has no flash. The lane is even overall; your job is to be alive at 6 with enough gold for a first item, steal her R every time she gives you the chance, and translate that into objective fights where the stun is worth more than anything she can do herself.","whys":["Neeko opens with ranged Q Blooming Burst petal drops you cannot punish without E, so farm at the outer edge of her Q arc and hold your spells.","Her E Tangle-Barbs travels in a line and widens on the second hit — she will root your E chain mid-air if you dash straight at her, so neither side can convert a clean trade.","Neeko's full Q-E-Q combo from range outdamages what you can do without a confirmed hook; her root lets her land both Q detonations while you stand still.","Both kits are online but she still has ranged poke and a root that punishes your one approach tool — farm, track her E cooldown, and only engage the moment it is burned.","Pop Blossom knocks up everything nearby and stuns it on landing, and her disguise hides the wind-up — at six she opens from a fake ally with no tell for you to read.","Her Shadowflame spike makes her burst one-rotation lethal through your W heal; play around her E cooldown and do not let her land root before you get your W off.","Sylas's Riftmaker sustain and stolen R give him a genuine teamfight carry role, while Neeko without her ultimate is a lesser threat — use Hijack every fight to press the advantage."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"olaf","win":["Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Skill","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Olaf's Undertow axe-slow chained into a Tough It Out shield is fully online, and Berserker Rage hands him more attack speed and lifesteal the lower you push him."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Ragnarok — but so does he still have Ragnarok. Both players are CC-immune in their all-in window; the fight goes to whoever lands axes first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Olaf's Sundered Sky true-damage spike makes extended trades lethal; your Riftmaker spike narrows the gap but doesn't flip it — only fight on his spent Undertow."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your W heal and stolen Ragnarok let you win the 1v1 cleanly — Olaf's true damage stops scaling as hard as your AP burst and sustain compound."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Undertow (axe throw) into cooldown, then E2 hook onto him and chain Q-W-passive autos while he has no slow","Save E1 dash specifically for his all-in engage — absorbing the run-down gap-close before your W heal fires","Reach level 6 with at least 50% HP so stolen Ragnarok gives you CC immunity in a fight where you are not already losing"],"foe":["Land Undertow to root-slow you and deny your E2 hook angle while his true-damage E chips through your HP","Press Ragnarok as he commits — while he keeps swinging he ignores disables, so your Chain Lash slow stops mattering","Force extended fights pre-6 where Berserker Rage lifesteal ramps as he drops and outheals your Kingslayer"]},"early":"At level 1 Olaf simply throws his axe (Undertow) and walks onto you — his slow lasts long enough that your only level-1 tool, Q, cannot re-establish range. Stand behind minions so the axe hits creeps first, and do not step up to contest his CS unless the axe is visibly on the ground waiting for his pickup. At level 2 his Tough It Out (W) gives him attack speed and a shield; if he picks up the axe he resets Undertow outright and the slow chain never drops. These two levels belong to him: last-hit safely, track where the axe lands, and hold your E charges for escape, not aggression. At level 3 your full kit is online but his is more dangerous than yours — his E deals true damage every 7-11 seconds regardless of your resistances, while your Q slow requires him to stand still long enough to be chained. Only trade when Undertow is down and you are near full HP.","mid":"Levels 4-5 the lane remains his if he plays the axe correctly. Your only real agency is the Abduct window: when Undertow is on cooldown (a flat 9 seconds, shrinking with haste) you can E1 forward, chain E2 onto him, Q for the slow, and W for the burst-heal. That is your entire pattern, and it requires him to have missed or thrown the axe before you commit. If he has the axe in hand or on the ground nearby, do not chain in — he slow-loops you, shields with W, and true-damages you through the extended trade faster than your heal can compensate. Respect the pickup spot: standing where the axe lands denies his reset and collapses his loop; it is worth losing a minion or two. At level 6 Ragnarok makes him immune to disables for as long as he keeps attacking, so your Q slow simply stops applying. The fight is a pure damage and sustain check; small HP and gold leads from surviving early are the entire margin.","late":"Two items in, the matchup finally tips. Your AP and W healing compound past his true-damage scaling; stolen Ragnarok gives you the same disable immunity he has; and your chain-passive auto burst in a prolonged brawl outlasts a Tough It Out shield that only lasts a few seconds per cast. The realistic path here is: survive lane without falling more than one kill behind, control vision so he cannot run off to side-lane freely, and take the 1v1 post-second-item when your W heal is outracing his true damage. If you bled two or more kills in laning phase, this late window narrows sharply — Olaf with a gold lead still runs you down even at full items. Win the resource game, not the ego game early.","whys":["Undertow axe-slow at level 1 lets Olaf walk onto you freely — he has a ranged slow and you have a delayed AoE chain; concede auto range and last-hit at Q distance only.","Tough It Out (W) gives him a shield and attack speed at level 2 while the axe loop is already online — he absorbs your counter-trade and you have nothing that holds him still.","His Reckless Swing (E) true damage ignores your armor and fires every few seconds; at level 3 a full trade goes: axe-slow, true-damage E, auto-W-auto, and your W heal cannot outpace that volume at low AP.","Levels 4-5 the axe cooldown is your only clock — he controls the engage window and you must react to it, not initiate it; his damage lead is real and not yet offset by Hijack.","Ragnarok is an answer to CC-locks, not to burst-trades — when both players press R simultaneously neither stuns the other, so the fight resolves on raw damage and sustain where the margin is slim; call it even.","Sundered Sky true damage spikes his 1v1 lethality, but your Riftmaker healing is ramping — the fight is coin-flip territory on spent cooldowns; avoid letting him dictate the trade timing with an axe in hand.","At 2+ items your W heal and AP burst outscale his true-damage ramp; stolen Ragnarok means his all-in no longer shuts off your E2 hook, and the extended brawl finally resolves in your favour."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"ornn","win":["Skill","Skill","Ornn","Ornn","Ornn","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Ornn's full kit is online — Volcanic Rupture into Bellows Breath brittle-procs you for percent-HP damage every time you step up without dodging the line."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Call of the Forge God — the ram is yours on Hijack's 30-80s while his own sits on 100-140s, so over a fight you get far more casts of it than he does."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ornn rushes Iceborn Gauntlet and the Frostfield slow makes it nearly impossible to close on him without eating a full Volcanic Rupture + brittle proc."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your Riftmaker plus a stolen R makes you a teamfight carry — Ornn's item forge falls behind your AP scaling curve and you become the primary win condition."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 Abduct when Volcanic Rupture is on cooldown — that is the only safe entry window","Steal Call of the Forge God at level 6 and use his own knockup to set up a kill or force a flash","Never fight beside a wall or a fresh Q pillar — Searing Charge only knocks up when it hits terrain"],"foe":["Land Volcanic Rupture to plant the pillar and set up a brittle-proc knockup on your approach angle","Land the final gout of Bellows Breath to make you Brittle, so his next hit knocks you up","Forge Iceborn Gauntlet first item to slow your hook approach and make every trade a punish window"]},"early":"Farm with Q Chain Lash at max range and accept that the first two levels belong to whoever avoids a mistake — Ornn's base stats are deceptively strong and Living Forge hands him extra armour and magic resist from every source he buys. Do not dash in recklessly: if his Volcanic Rupture is up, the magma pillar it leaves is terrain, and terrain is exactly what turns his Searing Charge into a knockup. Instead, bait the Q cast, then punish the seven-second window before it returns. Your W Kingslayer heal keeps you in HP parity as long as you respect Brittle — trade short when Bellows Breath is recent, trade longer when it is clearly on cooldown.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are Ornn's strongest pre-six window — his kit is fully online, Volcanic Rupture's damage is meaningful, and Bellows Breath's percent-HP component punishes your sustain-trade pattern if you stand in it. Track the Q before every hook attempt: the pillar it plants is the terrain his 450-range Searing Charge needs to knock you up, so if the line misses, his engage is gutted for seven seconds. At level 6 you get a copy of Call of the Forge God, not his only one — he still holds his own on a 100-140s timer, so use yours to open a fight he has already spent his in, and never trade ultimates head-on while Brittle is on you.","late":"Two items into a stolen Call of the Forge God and you are a genuine teamfight flex — Ornn's forge-item lead matters less when you are one-shotting the carry with a Riftmaker W and dropping his knockup on his own backline. Do not get caught in side lane 1v1s without your E up: an Iceborn Gauntlet Ornn with brittle can tank your full combo and CC you back. Play for the mid-game skirmishes and river objectives where stolen-R uptime is highest, and let your AP scaling do the work he cannot itemize against.","whys":["Level 1 is decided by base stats and first-move mistakes — neither kit has a clean opener, so last-hit at Q range and do not commit your E before seeing his intentions.","Ornn's W passive brittle triggers on his first auto after Bellows Breath, turning every trade into bonus percent-HP damage; his engage pattern is safer than yours at this level.","Full kit Ornn runs Volcanic Rupture into Bellows Breath into brittle auto — the pillar boxes your E exit and the proc punishes the fight you thought you were winning.","Iceborn Gauntlet is near complete and the Frostfield slow sticks to your movement after every E dash, making it trivial for him to line up a Volcanic Rupture knockup on your approach.","Ornn has his forge spike online and maximum brittle-proc pressure — avoid extended fights and look only for punishes when Volcanic Rupture is visibly cooling down.","You steal Call of the Forge God and Riftmaker is coming — the all-in math is genuinely contested; land E2 Abduct after his pillar is down and the skirmish is yours more often than not.","Two-item Riftmaker sustain plus a stolen double-knockup ult makes you the teamfight carry; Ornn's item-forge lead does not scale into damage the way your AP curve does."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"pantheon","win":["Pantheon","Pantheon","Pantheon","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Pantheon's W Shield Vault is point-and-click: he can stun you any time his passive procs and burst you with empowered Q before you can respond."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Grand Starfall is a genuine swing — a global repositioning ultimate puts you on equal teamfight footing and opens kill pressure the moment it comes back."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Void Corruption stacks through your sustained trades, letting your W heal compound and your passive autos out-damage Pantheon's lethality spike at this phase."},{"when":"Late","text":"Sylas reclaims the 2-item window — Pantheon's kit falls off hard and your W sustain plus stolen R make you the stronger late-game skirmisher."}],"wants":{"you":["Use E2 chain only after his W Shield Vault is already on cooldown, never into it","Trade extended with W healing active and passive Petricite Burst autos weaving in","Steal Grand Starfall at 6 and spend it aggressively — it is a far better ultimate in your hands than his"],"foe":["Land W Shield Vault into empowered Q to chunk you before your E1 shield is up","Stack passive Mortal Will to proc the full five-hit empowered Q burst on every trade","Snowball the early lead and translate it into roams before you can stabilize at 6"]},"early":"Level 1, Pantheon already has his passive Mortal Will and can proc an empowered auto at any moment — don't step up aggressively because his point-click W stun into the empowered Q cone deletes a third of your HP before your E1 shield does anything. Farm the safe edge of your Q Chain Lash and accept that you're not winning a trade until at least level 2. Even at 2 with E online, his W is still instant and his passive replenishes fast — only use E2 to dodge away from a W attempt, not to hook in and gift him the stun-chain combo. Let him think you're scared; you are, but only until level 6.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the most honest window — his kit is all deployed and so is yours, and extended fighting with W healing into passive Petricite autos actually starts to match his burst if you don't get caught cold by the stun. Watch his passive stacks: if he has five, a W-into-empowered-Q is coming and you need to either pre-E1 the burst or stay out of his range until he wastes the proc on a minion. Level 6 changes the lane completely: Grand Starfall is a massive team-fight ultimate, but for the 1v1 it also means he can roam with zero warn — take it immediately, use it, and threaten him in the side lane while it is on his cooldown. The moment the stolen R is available, look for a W all-in at low opponent HP; your healing at missing-health thresholds will out-trade his burst if you hook in on the W cooldown.","late":"With Riftmaker or Shadowflame stacked, your W Kingslayer executes wounded targets and your sustained chain-passive trading loop out-damages Pantheon's kit, which has no scaling after his lethality first item. He is a level 1-6 champion who needs to convert early leads into a snowball; if you survive lane at even gold, the 2-item skirmish is yours. In teamfights, Grand Starfall gives you global presence he no longer has — drop it before dragon or Baron, let your team follow, and play the AP bruiser angle that Pantheon simply cannot match late.","whys":["Pantheon has full Mortal Will passive at level 1 and can proc empowered auto into empowered Q instantly — you have no answer to the point-click stun without E online.","His W Shield Vault is a 600-range point-click stun on a 13s cooldown — at level 2 he chains it into Comet Spear faster than your new E charges let you respond.","Levels 3-5 are his peak melee bully window: empowered Q cone after a W stun at full Mortal Will stacks is over 40% of your HP in one button press.","Both kits are fully online and the lane stabilizes into extended trading — your W heal versus his burst output is close enough that neither side cleanly wins without a key ability advantage.","Hijack copies Grand Starfall at 6 — he keeps his own, but his costs 150-180s while yours returns in 80, so the 1v1 tips your way when you chain in on Shield Vault's cooldown.","Riftmaker's Void Corruption stacking through your sustained trading loop out-damages his lethality first-item; W heals for massive amounts into his missing-health threshold burst.","Pantheon's kit offers nothing beyond his early burst pattern at full build — your W Kingslayer scaling and stolen utility ultimates make you the stronger late-game AP bruiser in every skirmish."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"poppy","win":["Skill","Poppy","Poppy","Skill","Poppy","Poppy","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Poppy's E (Heroic Charge) pins you to a wall for a stun, which holds you inside Hammer Shock long enough for its delayed explosion — she can run you down before your W heals anything back."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Poppy's W (Steadfast Presence) activates the moment you fire E2, canceling the chain pull and leaving you stranded in melee range of a tank who just blocked your only engage tool."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Poppy's first armor item (Sunfire or Heartsteel) makes your magic damage rotation feel negligible and her W cooldown drops, meaning she can deny your E2 on nearly every trade attempt."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Sylas reclaims the late window — multiple AP items let W heal enormous chunks, stolen Poppy R lets you peel or disengage teamfights, and her scaling flatlines compared to your AP curve."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Poppy's W (Steadfast Presence) off cooldown before firing E2, so your chain pull actually connects","Use Q slow and Passive chain autos to trade at the edge of her melee range where W still threatens but her E wall-pin does not","Steal her R and use it as a disengage to survive ganks or peel in teamfights — her knockback is a safety tool, not a win condition for you"],"foe":["Activate W the instant you dash — it stops Abscond and Kingslayer alike, then slows and grounds you where you land","Use E (Heroic Charge) to pin you against a wall, holding you in Hammer Shock for its delayed detonation","Sustain through your W heal with tank stats and let her passive shield absorb burst, then run you down below half HP where your W heals least"]},"early":"Poppy's W is the matchup in a single button — it stops Abscond and Kingslayer both. At level 1 neither of you has a clean edge: trade Chain Lash slows against her Hammer Shock zone and stay off walls. The moment she hits level 2 she can E you into a wall for a stun and hold you there for the Hammer Shock detonation, and that pattern chunks hard before your W has any healing power. Respect the wall-pin range at all times: fight in the centre of the lane, never near the side walls where Heroic Charge stuns. Your Q slow is the only safe harass this early — poke and step back.","mid":"Levels 4-6 are Poppy's danger window. Her W goes up the instant she reads your E1 dash animation, which means your entire engage pattern is shut down unless you bait it first. To bait it, dash E1 forward but do NOT fire E2 — let her W pop on your E1 movement, then immediately E2 the chain into her once the zone drops. This is tight timing and she can simply not activate W if she reads the bait; that is why this lane has a 45% win rate. At 6 her R is worth stealing for teamfights, but it does not swing the 1v1 the way Malphite or Sion's ult does — Poppy's R knocks enemies away, which Sylas can use for peel but cannot use to run her down.","late":"Past two items Sylas genuinely outscales Poppy. Your W heals for enormous amounts at full AP, your Q slow keeps her from running, and her kit offers no percent-HP damage or true damage to cut through your sustain. The stolen R becomes a powerful teamfight tool for peeling your carries or displacing enemies off objectives. Play the side lane carefully — she will still trade evenly in short burst windows — but in extended skirmishes and teamfights you are the stronger champion. Convert your mid-game into a gold lead through roams and objectives so this late-game advantage actually closes the game.","whys":["Neither of you has a reliable kill threat at level 1 — Poppy lacks E and you lack E2, so it is Chain Lash poke against her Hammer Shock zone.","Poppy unlocks E (Heroic Charge) at level 2 and can wall-pin you for a stun, holding you for the full Hammer Shock detonation before your W heal matters — she takes the edge.","Full kit Poppy with W, E, and Q means every approach with your E2 is countered by Steadfast Presence; she wins the extended trade while you are stuck without your chain.","Both kits are fully online but the W counter-dash dynamic stays even — you can bait W to land E2, but she can also simply not activate it; the matchup is genuinely even on reads.","Poppy hits level 6 and her W cooldown is low enough to cycle it every real trade, blocking your E2 repeatedly while she zones you with Q and runs you down after E stuns.","First armor item makes your AP rotation feel hollow; Poppy's W is now almost always available and your E2 baits are harder to execute when she has stats to trade through your Q poke safely.","Two AP items unlock Sylas's sustain ceiling — W heals more than Poppy can deal in trades, your Q slow kites her immobile kit, and the stolen R is a genuine teamfight tool for peel or displacement."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"quinn","win":["Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Behind Enemy Lines gives you her map speed, not a re-engage — the channel breaks on damage, so spend it following her roams."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Omnivamp stacks through your chain-passive-W rotation, letting you heal back the poke deficit and sustain through her bird-strike harass."},{"when":"Lvl 1-5","text":"Quinn's Harrier-empowered autos proc Vulnerable on every step you take forward — her blinded trades and Vault knockback keep you off her all early."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"At two items your AP and Omnivamp make you a full brawler; Quinn's Vault and kite pattern lose potency in teamfights where you can angle in from a flank."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Vault (E) on the wave, then E2-hook the moment her knockback animation ends and she has no disengage","Stack W heal into her bird-strike poke to erase the early deficit and enter 6 at near-full health","Steal Behind Enemy Lines at 6 and match her roams with it — the channel ends the moment she damages you"],"foe":["Proc Harrier on every forward step and cash the mark with her bonus-damage first attack","Vault away the instant you chain in — it interrupts you and lands her back at attack range","Roam with Skystrike between waves to snowball side lanes while your poke pins you under tower"]},"early":"Level 1 is a tax you pay. Quinn's Harrier passive marks you for a bonus-damage first attack and her Q reaches 1025 against your 775 — stay back, last-hit at chain tip range, and do not walk up without a minion body between you. At level 2 your W comes online but she still has Vault as a get-out: it interrupts your approach and drops her back at attack range, and Blinding Assault turns off the autos you were closing to land. Your job levels 1-3 is to shield E1 through her poke, count Vault at 8-12s and Blinding Assault at 9-11s, and accept you are not winning a trade unless both are spent.","mid":"Level 6 belongs to her, and the reason is map, not lane. Behind Enemy Lines is a channelled flight that breaks on any damage, so the copy Hijack gives you is a travel tool rather than a re-engage — do not press it mid-duel expecting a gap-close. The in-lane pattern that does work is unchanged: she burns Vault on your chain attempt, you back off, and you commit on the next one while both Vault and Blinding Assault are down, chaining Q slow into the W heal-burst with passive autos cleaning up. She will roam with her ult between waves, so crash before she hits 6 and ping every disappearance rather than trying to out-duel her for it.","late":"Two items is where the lane's 51% WR plays out — you are a genuine brawler and Quinn is a skirmisher with no tank stats; your Omnivamp and AP itemization mean every extended fight tilts your way as long as you get on her. The problem is teamfight positioning: she will try to stay at backline range and bird-dive your carries while you look for the flank. Match her roam timing with Teleport, steal the most impactful ult in the fight, and use your E2-hook as a peel tool if she dives your ADC. The lane is even across the game — neither of you runs away with it, so the team that converts skirmishes into objectives first wins.","whys":["Quinn's Harrier passive marks you Vulnerable on her first step-up and her Q poke outranges your level-1 Q — you have no safe forward move and no answer to her empowered auto.","Vault interrupts your chain and lands her back at attack range exactly as you close, and Blinding Assault blanks the Petricite autos you meant to follow up with.","Full kit is online but Vault runs on 8-12s and Blinding Assault on 9-11s — a commit needs both spent, not one, so count them before you step up.","Harrier-empowered autos plus Vault disengage let her pick the length of every trade; she walks in, procs Vulnerable, and Vaults out before your chain-passive rotation lands.","Hijacking Skystrike flips the disengage math — she burns Vault to dodge your hook and you re-close with the stolen dash, landing E2 into a full Q-W-passive rotation she cannot escape.","Riftmaker Omnivamp stacks through your full chain rotation and W burst; the poke deficit she built all lane erases in one sustained trade, and your kill threat is real from any HP value.","Two AP items plus a stolen ult make you a teamfight flex that outscales her skirmisher kit in full five-on-five brawls — flank in, hook her backline carry, and force fights she cannot bird-dive her way out of."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"renekton","win":["Skill","Renekton","Renekton","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Renekton hits level 2 first off a short wave — his Ruthless Predator (W) stun into Cull the Meek (Q) combo shreds you before your E is even online."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Renekton's fury-empowered W keeps you from ever freely trading; one stun into his full combo removes 40-50% of your HP before your W heal can answer."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Dominus on an 80s timer against his 120s — you get more casts of his own ultimate than he does, so all-in on his downtime."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks into your W heal while Renekton's early-game lead evaporates without items; extended fights start to favor you."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E Abscond/Abduct AFTER his Ruthless Predator (W) stun is on cooldown so your W heal lands uninterrupted","Steal Dominus at level 6 and force the all-in immediately — the AoE HP buff is your first genuine kill window","Farm safely early, then build Riftmaker with Grievous Wounds — Cull the Meek heals him through every extended trade"],"foe":["Land Ruthless Predator (W) to stun you before your E hook connects so you cannot complete the trade rotation","Spam Cull the Meek (Q) in every trade — it heals him off each hit and keeps the fury bar full","Shove the wave and fight you under his terms levels 1-5 before your stolen R gives you a power advantage"]},"early":"Renekton is the aggressor here — he wants to short-trade you with Slice and Dice (E) in and out, and his Ruthless Predator (W) stun hits before you can respond with your own chain. At level 1 you both have limited tools, but his Q poke and auto-attack reset punish step-ups harder than yours do; just last-hit at max Q range and don't walk into his E range. At level 2, if he hits it off the minion wave before you, expect an immediate W stun — try to bait it by standing at the edge of his E range, then E1 dash away so the stun clips air. Your W heal is strong but it requires you to be ON him, and his stun prevents that.","mid":"Levels 3-5 are where Renekton is most oppressive — his fury-empowered W stuns for 1.5 seconds instead of 0.75 and destroys shields on the way through, so the buffer you planned to trade behind is simply deleted. The key is sequencing: chain onto him only when W is visibly down (16 seconds at rank 1, 8 at rank 5), then Q into Kingslayer into passive autos. If he burns W on a minion for fury, that is your window. At level 6 he gains health and Fury from Dominus, but Hijack returns in 80 seconds against his 120 — so you can spend his own ultimate more often than he can. This is the first realistic kill window; play for it.","late":"Once Renekton is past his level 1-6 window his damage tapers fast without items, and your Riftmaker sustain plus a stolen teamfight ultimate make you far more relevant. In extended trades at first item you win if you avoid his W stun opener — hook first, heal through his damage, and chain passive autos between spells. Late game Renekton is a split-push threat with Sheen/Sunderer; respect the side-lane but do not let him set the 1v1 terms early. Your carry window is mid-game objectives after surviving lane — convert the level 6 spike into a tower or herald rather than over-staying for a lane kill.","whys":["Neither kit has a decisive opener at level 1 — you both auto and Q poke, but Renekton's Q reset and range advantage mean you should last-hit safely and avoid extended trades.","Renekton hits level 2 first off a short wave and his Ruthless Predator (W) stun arrives before your E hook is online — he dictates every level-2 trade.","His full kit with fury-empowered W stun removes 40-50% of your HP before your W Kingslayer heal can close the gap; only engage after his W is on cooldown.","Both kits are online but Renekton's fury management keeps him ahead in short trades — mirror his pattern and wait for W cooldown windows before committing your E hook.","Hijack steals Dominus: the AoE HP surge and Tyrant form give you a decisive all-in window that his post-6 kit cannot match — fight immediately when R comes up.","Riftmaker omnivamp amplifies your W Kingslayer sustain in every extended trade while Renekton's damage-without-items starts to fade; force long fights here.","Two items and a stolen teamfight ultimate make you a flex threat; Renekton becomes a side-laner with no late scaling, so shift focus to teamfights and objective control."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"riven","win":["Skill","Riven","Riven","Riven","Skill","Riven","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Riven's full kit comes online — her Q-Q-Q dash chain plus W-stun and E-shield lets her run a full animation-cancelled combo that you cannot safely trade through without your own E hook landing first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Blade of the Exile is among the worst ultimates you can hold — it grants attack damage and range, which an AP build cannot spend, so at six your real tool is still the Kingslayer heal and her spent Ki Burst."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riven spikes hard with Sundered Sky or Black Cleaver into armor pen — she can run a full combo that kills you before your W healing catches up if she gets ahead in early levels."},{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"The moment Riven hits level 2 she can Q-dash in and W-stun immediately — if you do not have E available to hook her pre-emptively or dodge out, this two-second window will always trade in her favor."}],"wants":{"you":["Chain in before Ki Burst lands — Abduct pulls you to her and does not stun, so move first","Extend trades with W Kingslayer heal to survive her burst window and out-sustain her short rotation","Reach level 6 with enough HP to force all-ins, using stolen Blade of the Exile plus your W sustain to keep the fight close"],"foe":["W-stun you at the start of every trade so your E hook is wasted or unused and she gets a free combo rotation","Chain three Q dashes inside your hitbox to maximize her Runic Blade empowered auto damage between each Q cast","Use E Valor shield to absorb your Q chain explosion damage and dash out once her combo lands, denying your W healing follow-up"]},"early":"At level 1 neither of you has a meaningful engage pattern — Riven has one Q dash but no stun or shield yet, and you only have Chain Lash or Kingslayer without Abscond. Farm safely at the edge of Chain Lash range and do not step into her Broken Wings range. The real danger begins the moment she hits level 2: her Q-into-Ki-Burst sequence lands before you can react if you are standing close, and Abduct will not stop it because the recast only pulls you to her. Use Abscond to leave, not to meet her. Levels 1-2 belong to Riven because she completes her short-trade kit before you complete yours — respect that window and do not contest it without E available.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are still Riven's strongest window: her Q-Q-Q animation-cancelled combo with W-stun in the middle and E-shield absorbing your Q burst means every extended trade has her dealing physical damage while your W heal tries to compensate. The trade pattern you want is: bait her E shield first by poking with Q chain lash, then once Valor is on cooldown E-hook her to stun, immediately W Kingslayer for the missing-health damage and healing, and use your Passive chain autos between casts. Do not let her E out cleanly before your W lands — if she shields your Q and then dashes away, you healed nothing and she won. At level 6 the lane resets to a genuine skill matchup: stealing Blade of the Exile gives you her empowered Q and W, but you are using AP scaling on her AD ultimate, so the value is moderate rather than game-ending; your W healing is the real equalizer here.","late":"Once Riven completes Black Cleaver or Sundered Sky she has enough armor penetration and burst that a full combo kills you before your W healing completes if you entered the fight low. Play around your E hook as the engagement tool — never all-in without it available, because walking into her Q range without a stun ready means eating her full rotation. In teamfights Riven will dive carries and use R Wind Slash as an execute, so track her position rather than matching her 1v1 in a sidelane you do not need to win. The 48.74% win rate reflects the reality: this is a genuine mechanics duel where your W sustain keeps you alive but her combo execution and animation-cancel ceiling give her a consistent edge if she plays the level 2-to-first-item window well.","whys":["Level 1 you have Chain Lash or Kingslayer and no Abscond, so neither of you can force a real all-in — but Riven's first Broken Wings dash keeps you honest at farm range.","Riven reaches her full Q-Q-Q plus W-stun kit at level 2 before you have meaningful sustained trading tools — her stun opening a free combo rotation is the defining early threat.","Levels 3-5 her animation-cancelled Q chain with W-stun mid-combo and E-shield absorbing your Q burst means every extended trade trends her way unless you land E hook first.","Her Q-Q-Q dash chain generates Runic Blade autos between casts and the W-stun in the middle locks you out of your own W Kingslayer — the sustained trade still favors her physical damage over your healing.","At level 6 you steal Blade of the Exile, but it pays in attack damage and range that an AP Sylas cannot spend — the W heal is what keeps you level, so the window turns on who commits first.","A first-item Sundered Sky or Black Cleaver gives Riven armor penetration that bypasses your W healing's value — she can run a full combo that kills you before sustain catches up if she is ahead.","At two-plus items your Riftmaker or Shadowflame sustain and AP scaling let you contest the duel again — W Kingslayer at full AP with missing-health bonus damage puts the 1v1 back in parity, making this a genuine Skill matchup late."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"rumble","win":["Skill","Rumble","Rumble","Skill","Rumble","Skill","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Rumble's Flamespitter (Q) comes fully online here — sustained DPS in trades that out-damages your W heal before you can close to hook range."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Rumble's Equalizer (R) is a massive zone that shuts down your E approach angle — if he drops it on the path you need to hook in, the all-in dies before it starts."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Riftmaker first-item keeps pace with his Liandry's; sustained brawling with W heal lets you trade evenly so long as you land your E hook to stick."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your W missing-health burst and stolen Equalizer swing teamfights — use his own R zone as a zoning wall and clean up with chain Passive autos."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E (Abscond/Abduct) to bridge his kite distance before Flamespitter uptime burns you down","Trade W (Kingslayer) on him when he's low from your Q slow to maximize the missing-health damage and healing","Steal his Equalizer (R) and drop it across his escape route to reverse his zone control"],"foe":["Run Flamespitter (Q) into overheat to out-DPS your W heal and punish your approach window","Kite with Scrap Shield (W) speed and Electro-Harpoon (E) slows at 850 range, outside every tool you own","Drop Equalizer (R) between you and him to block your engagement path and deny the all-in"]},"early":"At level 1 you have no mobility and Rumble's Flamespitter is already ticking sustained damage if he walks up — last-hit with Q's slow field and concede any trade where he's running Flamespitter at the boundary of its range. When E comes online at level 2, you have ONE real window: hook him mid-Flamespitter before he can use his Electro-Harpoon slow to back off. If you land E and he's committed to the DPS, your W heals the burn and you come out even or ahead. Miss the hook and you eat the full Flamespitter duration at no benefit — respect that and go back to Q farming.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are Rumble's strongest pre-6 window because his Flamespitter cooldown is low enough to run two casts into overheat in a single trade, and the overheat empowered autos hurt. Don't stand at his DPS edge fishing for a hook — bait the first Flamespitter cast by stepping just inside range, then E hook as soon as he's committed and the channel is almost over, so the second cast never fires. At level 6 his Equalizer fundamentally changes the approach geometry: the second he hits 6 treat any path to him as potentially blocked by a fire-line and wait for your turn to have R too.","late":"The late game is yours in isolation — your W's missing-health scaling one-shots Rumble once he's been kited into the red, and his Equalizer becomes your zone when you steal it. In teamfights, drop the stolen Equalizer across the front of the enemy backline, then E hook onto their carry through the fire; your Passive chain-autos amplify the burst on a target already burning. The win condition is not trading with Rumble — it's getting past him to softer targets while he has no R to zone you back.","whys":["Neither of you has mobility at level 1 — Rumble farms with Flamespitter pressure and you farm with Q range; genuine standoff.","Rumble hits Flamespitter + Electro-Harpoon slow with full uptime at level 2 before your E hook can close the gap reliably — he wins most level-2 skirmishes.","His Flamespitter DPS races your W heal at levels 3-5, and a two-cast overheat sequence out-damages what you can sustain without landing the hook first.","With full kits both sides the lane stabilizes into a hook-vs-kite minigame — landing E tips the trade your way, missing it tips it his; genuinely even execution-dependent window.","The Equalizer (R) walls off your approach the instant he hits 6, and at 130s it outlasts your 80s Hijack — until you have stolen it, he owns the level 6 window.","Riftmaker and Liandry's hit at similar power levels; the sustained brawl is even but his Flamespitter still out-ranges your approach, so the first-item window is a skill-check on hook timing.","At 2+ items your W missing-health burst and stolen Equalizer outvalue his remaining DPS — you own the late 1v1 and the teamfight swing."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"ryze","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Ryze","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"E hook online — the moment Rune Prison is on cooldown, dash in and chain Q into W to erase his poke deficit in one rotation."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Realm Warp is the worst ult on the map to hijack — a 180s repositioning portal with no damage and no CC, so treat level 6 as Ryze's spike, not yours."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ryze's Tear rush means he has raw damage but no durability — your Riftmaker W-heal wins every extended trade he can't end quickly."},{"when":"Late","text":"Ryze's Seraph's + Shadowflame spike turns him into a raid boss at range — respect his burst and play around your team's follow-up rather than straight 1v1s."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2 the instant Rune Prison is off cooldown and chain Q into W before he can reroot","Use W heal to absorb his spam-Q poke and out-sustain his early trading pattern","Bait Spell Flux off him first — without Flux his Rune Prison only slows, and the all-in survives it"],"foe":["Root you with Rune Prison and chain E-Q resets into a burst combo before your W lands","Out-range you across the wave and whittle your HP below the threshold where your W sustain matters","Buy time with Realm Warp escapes and stall into his two-item Seraph's raid-boss window"]},"early":"Ryze's level 1 is almost entirely Q spam — it stings but it cannot kill you, so sit at max Q range and CS while watching his position. The moment you hit level 2 and E comes online you have a real threat: if he wastes Rune Prison on a minion or misses, that is your window to dash in, auto-chain passive into W, and get out before he can reset his combo. Do not walk into melee range while Rune Prison is up at full health — he roots you in place and layers E-Q-Q for a punishing burst. Levels 3-5 are your best pre-6 window: full kit access means you can E1 dash forward to bait the root, eat it with E1's forward position still intact, then E2-hook and dump the full rotation — W heal converts the trade firmly in your favor.","mid":"At level 6 nothing flips. Hijack copies Realm Warp, it does not take it — Ryze keeps his ultimate and you gain a 180-second portal with no damage and no CC, which wins you no duel. Use it as a map tool: reposition your team or cut off his walk home, not as a combat opener. The lane still turns on Spell Flux. Rune Prison is a 615-range slow until Flux is on you, and only then a root, so bait the Flux out and step in during the 2.5-3.5s it is down, then E2-hook into Q-W. Doran's Shield blunts his Overload spam. Force the all-in before his Tear finishes stacking.","late":"Ryze with Seraph's Embrace and Shadowflame becomes a genuine problem in extended team fights — his Q resets are near-instant and he can burst you from outside your E range if you are even slightly behind. Do not look for a solo 1v1 when he has two items unless you have a clear root-down window and the HP lead to absorb his combo. Your job is to use the stolen ultimate earlier in the game to snowball a lead and deny him the stack time he needs; if you converted the lane correctly, his scaling window is irrelevant because you have already taken towers and created a numbers game his team cannot fix.","whys":["Ryze has Q poke only at level 1 — you match him on CS at range and deny him any hook angle since your E is not yet online.","E comes online and Rune Prison is on a long enough cooldown that one missed root gives you a free hook-W trade that wins the level 2 all-in.","Full kit access means you can E1 forward to absorb the root then E2-hook; the W heal turns the trade firmly in your favor through levels 3 to 5.","Both of you have your kits but neither has a decisive edge — trade around Rune Prison cooldowns and bank small HP leads for the level 6 spike.","Hijack copies, it does not steal: Ryze keeps Realm Warp and you gain a 180s portal that wins no duel, so his 6 is worth more than yours here.","Ryze's Tear-first build means he has mana and damage but no durability — your Riftmaker W-heal outlasts his burst in any fight that runs longer than his first combo.","Seraph's Embrace plus Shadowflame gives Ryze a true raid-boss window at range; avoid extended 1v1s without a root-down angle and let your team close around him."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"sejuani","win":["Sylas","Skill","Skill","Skill","Sejuani","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"You open with Chain Lash before she has Arctic Assault, but Frost Armor gives her slow immunity out of combat — the chip is free, the slow is not."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sejuani can now combo Q dash into E Permafrost stun then R Glacial Prison for a full CC chain that deletes you before Hijack matters — respect her engage range."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's healing starts to outpace her percent-HP W damage, shifting skirmishes back in your favour through sustained trade cycles."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Your full kit with W sustain lets you trade evenly through her Winter's Wrath ticks, but her Permafrost stun can interrupt your E2 follow-through — bait E before hooking."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E2 while Frost is short of max — Permafrost is a 1.5s cooldown but it needs full stacks first","Weave passive Petricite Burst autos between spell casts to stack magic damage through her armor","Steal Glacial Prison before objectives and use it on her teammates rather than burning it in the 1v1"],"foe":["Open with Arctic Assault dash knockup to interrupt your E approach before you can land the chain stun","Cap your Frost stacks with Winter's Wrath, then Permafrost — a landed stun stops Abscond outright","Chain Q into E stun into R Glacial Prison at level 6 before you can Hijack her ultimate away"]},"early":"At level 1 she has no mobility and her only tool is basic autos — open with Q Chain Lash to slow her and follow with passive Petricite Burst autos, denying her W stacks. Respect the fact that her Permafrost E activates off any frost application, so if Winter's Wrath ticks land in a trade she can cancel your W heal with the stun; keep trades short and reset with the Q slow until your full kit is up. She can't punish you from range so take every free Q you can get.","mid":"At level 6 Sejuani's CC chain becomes lethal: Arctic Assault into Permafrost stun into Glacial Prison locks you down long enough that you cannot W-heal or complete an E2 chain — this is her real kill window and it flips the lane dynamic before you can use Hijack offensively. Play around her Q cooldown religiously; the moment Arctic Assault is down, E2 hook and dump your full rotation plus the stolen R before she resets. If she lands Q first, disengage with E1 and reset rather than committing into full CC.","late":"At two items the matchup flattens back to Skill: Riftmaker's healing combined with Ability Haste lets you cycle Q-W-passive faster than her Permafrost cooldown, and Glacial Prison becomes an objective weapon rather than a kill tool in your hands. She scales into a teamfight engager while you scale into a skirmish carry — avoid the extended 1v1 near her team and look to roam or 1v2 with her stolen R on cooldown.","whys":["She has no gap-close yet and Arctic Assault is 18s at rank 1 — poke with Chain Lash, but Frost Armor makes her slow-immune out of combat, so the chip is the point, not the slow.","Permafrost only fires at MAXIMUM Frost stacks, so your window is the Winter's Wrath arcs before Frost caps — with E online you both fish in the same two seconds and it evens out.","Full kit plus W sustain lets you out-heal her Winter's Wrath percent-HP ticks in extended trades, but her E stun interrupts your W mid-cast if she has frost stacks built.","Her Permafrost stun keeps short all-ins roughly even — neither side can cleanly burst the other before CC resets the trade and both back off.","Sejuani can combo Arctic Assault knockup into Permafrost stun into Glacial Prison AOE stun, a full three-ability CC chain that kills you before Hijack fires — this is her strongest window and she owns it.","Riftmaker healing closes the gap versus her percent-HP W damage, and you can now cycle Q-passive faster than her Permafrost cooldown — skirmishes trend slightly in your favour but neither side dominates.","She becomes a teamfight engager with her R while you become a skirmish carry with your stolen R — the lane impact at two items is roughly mirrored, keeping it Skill."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"sett","win":["Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Sett's double-punch passive makes every auto exchange punishing — his passive hits twice with the second auto dealing bonus true damage, so concede the wave early rather than trading on even HP."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack The Show Stopper: it carries and slams one champion, but its range is 400, so it is a melee-range pick, not a ranged engage — walk into it, and it grounds the target's dashes."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Sett's E Facebreaker can grab Sylas mid-dash and stun him into a max-grit W true-damage smash — respect his grab range whenever the grit bar is orange or full."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through extended brawls that Sett wants; hit the item and keep fights in the 3-4 second brawl window where your W heal and Passive chain autos sustain through his grit smash."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E Abscond/Abduct after Sett burns his Facebreaker stun, then immediately chain W into passive autos to outsustain his grit bar","Steal Show Stopper at level 6 and use it aggressively — this turns a lane Sett was bullying into one you now threaten with his own suppression slam","Keep fights in the 3-4 second brawl range where W heal and chain-Passive autos pay off, rather than letting Sett dictate a single burst trade with a full grit bar"],"foe":["Bank grit through repeated short auto trades so his W panel carries true damage into every all-in","Land Facebreaker E grab before Sylas can hook away, pinning him in place for the max-grit W smash","Force extended stand-still brawls where his passive double-punch out-DPS Sylas before W heal can even register"]},"early":"At level 1 Sett wins every auto trade — his passive double-punch deals bonus true damage on the second hit and there is nothing you can do about it yet. Farm with Q Chain Lash at max range, do not auto-trade back, and accept you are behind in HP until level 2. When E comes online at level 2, the hook-into-W all-in becomes available, but Sett at this point still has healthy HP and a full grit bar building, so only take the all-in if he has stepped forward and taken Q damage first. At level 3 the real discipline is watching his grit bar: an orange or full bar means his W smash carries hundreds of true damage and can delete your HP bar even through W heal. Bait his Facebreaker E grab by feinting forward with your first E dash, then chain the hook onto him once the grab is on cooldown.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are a genuine skill check — Sett's Q gives move speed into you, making it harder to create the gap your first E dash needs to dodge Facebreaker. Trade this way: Q slow him, bait the E grab, then hook in immediately and chain W into Passive autos. If he E-grabs you before the hook lands, you eat a full grit W and lose the trade badly. Level 6 flips the dynamic entirely: steal Show Stopper, and you now have a targeted suppression slam that Sett cannot dodge. Use it the moment he has low HP from a prior W trade — suppress him into your wave or his own turret, and your W heal sustains through his answer.","late":"Late game this matchup is genuinely even around teamfight utility — stolen Show Stopper lets you engage or peel on anyone you choose, and your AP scaling keeps W healing relevant through extended fights. Sett at 2+ items with Sterak's Gage becomes almost unkillable in a prolonged brawl, so never commit your E hook onto a healthy Sett; wait for him to be engaged elsewhere or below half HP from a prior skirmish. Your goal is to have converted the level-6 Show Stopper windows into kills or objectives so the lane is not reset into a fair item-for-item comparison — if the game goes long and even, Sett's true-damage W and suppression slam are still threatening enough that you want teammates around before all-inning him.","whys":["Sett's passive double-punch deals bonus true damage and wins every level-1 auto exchange — last-hit with Q from range and do not trade autos.","E Abscond/Abduct comes online; bait his Facebreaker grab with a feint dash, then hook onto him once the grab is on cooldown for a W all-in.","Full kit available but Sett's grit bar is a constant threat — only hook in when his grit bar is empty or when you have landed Q slow first.","Sett's Q move speed closes your escape window; use the double-dash to dodge Facebreaker rather than trying to out-run him with a single E press.","Hijacking The Show Stopper gives you a 400-range grab, not a pick tool, and he still has his own — at 6 he has Grit, a 120s R and yours, so the level belongs to Sett for now.","Riftmaker's omnivamp feeds your W heal, but his first item feeds Grit, and Haymaker's centre is true damage your resistances never touch — the first-item brawl belongs to Sett.","Two items and a hijacked R make you the flex: Sett still needs to reach someone at 400 range, while you pick which ultimate on their team decides the fight. Do not duel him healthy."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"shen","win":["Skill","Shen","Shen","Shen","Shen","Shen","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Shen's Spirit's Refuge (W) comes online and immediately blocks Sylas's Petricite Burst chain-auto, gutting the trade pattern Sylas relies on at level 2."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sylas steals Stand United — a global shield and teleport — turning Shen's best macro tool into Sylas's own cross-map playmaking ability."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shen's first tank item (usually Heartsteel or Sunfire) consolidates his sustained trade advantage: W keeps blocking Petricite autos, Q empowers every trade, and Sylas's Riftmaker spike cannot match that durability through a live Spirit's Refuge."},{"when":"Late","text":"Both scale into teamfighters, the lane advantage dissipates, and Sylas's ability to steal Stand United on demand keeps him relevant in every skirmish."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E (Abscond/Abduct) only after Spirit's Refuge is burned — entering the blade zone before W is on cooldown hands the trade to Shen","Land Kingslayer (W) on a low-health Shen for the amplified heal, then disengage before his Q-empowered follow-up autos come through","Steal Stand United at 6 and use it immediately in a cross-map situation — a global shield on your team is the lane's whole payoff"],"foe":["Preemptively deploy Spirit's Refuge to nullify Petricite Burst chain-autos and win the extended trade every time","Use Twilight Assault (Q) to empower autos through trades, out-sustaining Sylas's Kingslayer heal with raw poke damage","Roam with Stand United to make the lane lead irrelevant, trading a stalemate top for winning fights bot or mid"]},"early":"Level 1 is genuinely neutral — Shen has no W yet and you both prod each other with Q poke. The moment Shen hits level 2 and activates Spirit's Refuge, the trade math flips: his spirit blade blocks your Petricite Burst chain-auto, which is the core of your damage in a melee trade, so never E in while that blue zone is active. Use Chain Lash (Q) to poke from outside the blade zone, keep your HP above half so Kingslayer's heal is modest rather than large, and accept that you are not winning a straight trade at these levels.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are still Shen's window — W has a short enough cooldown that he can cycle it in back-to-back trades, and his Q-empowered autos accumulate chip damage faster than your Kingslayer sustain pays it back. Commit only when you have watched Spirit's Refuge fully expire: E in, land Kingslayer while he is low on auto-charge, and retreat before the next W rotation. Level 6 changes the calculus entirely — steal Stand United with Hijack and immediately look to use it on a losing lane or teammate taking tower dive. Denying Shen his global R while holding it yourself is the entire point of playing this matchup.","late":"Once both of you have items the lane ceases to matter and you become a different champion problem — a mid-range AP bruiser with a pocket global R. Keep refreshing Stand United off Shen every time Hijack resets; every steal denies his team a shield-teleport and hands your team one. In teamfights, your E hook-stun on a priority target plus stolen Stand United shield on a fed carry is a legitimate win condition. The 49-50% matchup resolves here: neither side dominated lane, but Sylas's kit is broader in five-on-five skirmishes as long as he keeps stealing the R.","whys":["Neither Sylas nor Shen has a dominant level-1 tool — Shen lacks his W Spirit's Refuge and Sylas cannot safely hook in, so both last-hit cautiously and the first wave is a wash.","Spirit's Refuge (W) activates at level 2 and blocks Sylas's Petricite Burst chain-autos, turning every melee trade in Shen's favour from this point forward.","With full kit, Shen cycles Q-empowered autos through Spirit's Refuge repeatedly and out-trades Sylas's Kingslayer heal — Sylas can only poke with Chain Lash (Q) from range.","Shen holds the trade advantage through 4-5, but Spirit's Refuge is 16s at rank 1 — it is not back-to-back, so count it out and take the trade in the gap while Shadow Dash is also down.","Level 6 does not flip the trade pattern — Spirit's Refuge still blocks Petricite Burst chain-autos, and stolen Stand United, while valuable, does not help Sylas win a straight duel against a Shen who is blocking his passive every trade.","Shen's first tank item cements his durability; Sylas's Riftmaker spike cannot punch through sustained W-block trading since the spirit blade keeps negating his primary auto-damage pattern.","At two-plus items both champions transition to teamfighting roles, Shen's lane edge dissipates, and Sylas's ability to steal Stand United on every Hijack cooldown keeps the matchup genuinely even across the late game."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"singed","win":["Skill","Singed","Singed","Singed","Singed","Singed","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Singed gets his W Mega Adhesive and Fling combo online — the moment he plants the slow pool and Flings you into it, you eat the full poison trail and cannot escape with either E charge."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you a copy of Insanity Potion — use it to match his move-speed steroid in all-ins, because without it he simply outruns your chain."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Singed's Rylai's Crystal Scepter spikes here: every poison tick slows you, making your E2 hook approach trivially dodgeable and his Fling cooldown almost irrelevant."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Singed's proxy pressure peaks — if he starts farming behind your tower, your E2 range cannot punish it and you bleed CS while he burns tower plates."}],"wants":{"you":["Trade when Fling is down — it is 10s at rank 1 and only 125 range, so staying a step off it costs you nothing","Spend Kingslayer before Insanity Potion is up — once his trail carries Grievous Wounds the missing-health heal is worth half.","Steal Insanity Potion at 6 and match his move-speed steroid so your chain can actually land on a running target"],"foe":["Fling you into Mega Adhesive — landing in the goo roots you, and its grounding kills your dashes","Proxy-farm behind your tower at levels 4-5 where your E2 threat cannot reach him safely","Pop Insanity Potion so Poison Trail carries Grievous Wounds and halves Kingslayer heal"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q and nothing else — farm cleanly at max chain range and do not step into Fling range for any reason. He has no kill pressure either, so the level is a genuine standoff. At level 2 he gains Fling and immediately becomes dangerous: his pattern is to juke you into Mega Adhesive then Fling you backward into the full poison trail, and your E2 hook is too slow to land on a moving Singed before it happens. Respect the threat hard — use Q Chain Lash only from outside Fling range and accept that you cannot punish a Singed who plays the distance correctly in these first three levels. The lane is not yours to win early; your job is to survive it intact.","mid":"Levels 4 through 5 are his peak proxy window — if he vanishes behind your tower, do not chase. Your E2 hook cannot reach him before he Flings you under turret, and the poison trail ticking under your feet will bleed you out before you get a W cast off. Push to tower and rotate rather than following his proxy. At level 6 you steal Insanity Potion: this is the only window that swings back toward you, because matching his move-speed makes your E2 chain actually landable. The all-in at 6 is real — E1 into him to bait the Fling, side-step it, then E2 chain for the stun into Q slow and W burst-heal. If Fling hits you anyway, you will likely lose the trade regardless of the W heal, so the Fling dodge is non-negotiable.","late":"At two items Singed's Rylai's makes every poison tick a slow, which reduces your E2 hook to a coin-flip approach against a champion who already outruns most of the roster. Your role shifts to teamfight: flank wide, steal the scariest R on the enemy team, and chain into their backline — do not try to 1v1 a two-item Singed in open terrain because he will simply jog away from every hook while the poison does its work. Your W heal keeps you alive through the counter-engage, but the 1v1 is effectively over; play the map, not the lane.","whys":["You have Q only and he has only his trail and Fling threat — neither side has kill pressure, and the range gap keeps it honest.","Fling plus Mega Adhesive comes online and he immediately owns the extended trade — your E2 cannot close on him safely while Fling is available.","Full kit is up but so is his complete combo: Fling into slow pool into poison trail reads your approach and punishes the hook before it can connect.","Proxy pressure locks you under tower — your E2 range is insufficient to punish farm behind your own turret, and chasing earns you a Fling under tower.","Singed's Insanity Potion spikes his move speed; without Hijack your chain physically cannot catch him, and Fling remains on a short cooldown all fight.","Rylai's Crystal Scepter spikes here — every poison tick now slows you, and your E2 hook window against a slowed-but-moving Singed closes to nearly nothing.","Two items each and the poison-slow loop strips your approach entirely — play teamfights and steal the best enemy R rather than running the 1v1 that no longer favors you."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"sion","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Sion's fully charged Q knockup punishes every E hook attempt — he charges it while you're on the approach and you eat the stun before your W can land."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack Unstoppable Onslaught at 80s against his 140s — you get the charge back nearly twice as often, so spend it to start fights he then has no ultimate to answer."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sion's first Heartsteel or Sunfire stack makes him a wall your Riftmaker spike cannot crack — he out-bulks your W heal window and your E hook gives him a free W-shield detonation."},{"when":"Late","text":"Both scale into relevant teamfight tools — your stolen R becomes a second engage, but Sion's zombie passive and HP scaling make him equally impactful; neither side has a clean win condition."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E when Decimating Smash is down or uncharged — it is 10s at rank 1, and an uncharged swing only damages and slows","Trade with W the instant you land on him so the heal kicks in before his W shield detonates and knocks you back","Hijack Unstoppable Onslaught and charge it INTO him — the knockup scales with distance travelled, so start the charge from far out, not from on top of him"],"foe":["Charge his Q while you're walking in on E so the knockup catches you mid-dash and wastes your only gap-closer","Eat your burst on Soul Furnace, wait the 3 seconds, then detonate it for magic damage while your Kingslayer heal is still on cooldown","Stack HP and W-shield your trades until his Heartsteel snowball makes every short trade favourable for him"]},"early":"At level 1 neither of you has teeth — last-hit safely and do not throw your E into him blind. The danger window opens at level 2: Sion can begin charging his Q the moment he sees your E first dash, and if you complete the hook pull onto him he knocks you up before your W even fires. Respect the charge animation at all times; you will hear and see him wind up. Your job levels 1 through 3 is to Q-slow him from a distance, bait the charged Q by walking in and retreating, then punish the cooldown with a clean E hook into W. If you eat the knockup you lose the trade badly — his E poke adds chip damage on top of a W-shield he can detonate to push you away.","mid":"Your full kit is live but so is his — Sion's Q-W-E combo is legitimately painful for a melee champion with no hard escape, and your W heal does not outpace a good shield detonation. Play the approach angle: E hook only when his charged Q is either on cooldown or clearly uncharged (he's still at the cannon minion, not crouching in auto range). At 6 the matchup shifts — you steal Unstoppable Force and it is a strong theft; use his R on him immediately to force a flash or all-in, then layer your own E stun. The problem is he will look to return-trade with his zombie passive if you kill him, so burn your spells before the passive triggers to finish the fight quickly.","late":"Both of you become teamfight engines at 2+ items and neither side has a clean edge in the side lane either — Sion's HP wall eventually outheals your W output, but your stolen R every 110 seconds keeps you relevant in every fight. Play the objective game: your stolen Unstoppable Force plus your E stun is a genuine two-hit engage chain; Sion's zombie state makes him a trading resource even in death. Do not get caught charging into a full-health Sion without vision of his Q charge state — one knockup into his E poke at 200+ stacks ends your participation in the fight before it starts.","whys":["Both of you have single-ability access at level 1 — Sylas Q-slows from distance, Sion begins charging Q, but neither can force a decisive trade without taking equal poke back.","Sion can charge his Q the moment your E first dash animates, so the knockup lands before your hook pull completes — he wins the level-2 trade cleanly every time you approach carelessly.","Decimating Smash knocks you up if he holds the charge, Soul Furnace eats your burst and detonates three seconds later, and Roar of the Slayer shreds your armour from 800 — your sustain cannot cover all three.","Both champions have full kits now and the trade is decided by approach angle — you punish the charged Q cooldown with E into W, he punishes your aggression with knockup into shield detonation; neither pulls ahead cleanly.","Hijacking Unstoppable Onslaught gives you a steerable charge that knocks up harder the further it runs, but Glory in Death still gives him a second life after you kill him — real window, not a decisive one.","Sion's Heartsteel stacks and growing HP pool outpace what your Riftmaker can convert via W heal — he out-bulks your sustain spike and your E hook simply gifts him a free W-shield detonation at this point.","Both champions scale into relevant 5v5 tools — your stolen R plus E stun is a two-hit engage, his zombie passive is a second life; neither side has a winning edge and the lane result carries over, not the scaling."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"swain","win":["Skill","Swain","Swain","Skill","Skill","Swain","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Swain's E pull roots you during your approach — he can chain W bomb into Q poke and drain your HP before you ever land a W."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"His Demonic Ascension is not yet online at full value — E2 hook into Q-W on his step-up starts converting small HP leads."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Demonic Ascension is the turning point: you now drain him while he tries to drain you, and the all-in math flips."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Liandry's Torment hits before your Riftmaker — his poke-into-drain pattern reaches peak damage while your sustain is still catching up."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 the instant his E pull is on cooldown so he has no setup tool to punish your approach","Steal Demonic Ascension at 6 and force the all-in where both of you drain simultaneously — your W heal tips that fight","Buy Grievous Wounds early — Ravenous Flock heals him off every soul fragment he collects"],"foe":["Catch your Abscond with the Nevermove return, then pull you in for the full Death's Hand rotation","Keep Demonic Ascension active in extended trades so your drain outlasts your W heal","Poke you below 60% HP before engaging so his W bomb hits max missing-health damage on you"]},"early":"At level 1 you both have only one button — farm with Q chain at max range and do not step into his AA harass. His range advantage is modest and your Q slow sets up future all-ins. The danger window opens at level 2: Swain's E pull roots you mid-dash if you try to hook in, and his W bomb plus Q poke shreds you before W is even online. Walk up for CS only when his E is on cooldown; if he baits your E1 dash and his E is still available, do not throw the second part — you will land in his face with no stun and take a full punish rotation. Respect his pattern and take the small early losses without feeding.","mid":"Level 3 does not fix your problem — he still has the range and the root to punish your approach, so the rule holds: E2 only when his E is spent. Levels 4-5 are your first genuine window: track his E cooldown, wait for the visual tell that it is down, then E2 hook into Q-W with passive autos. Your W heal starts converting short trades into small HP leads. The real pivot is level 6 — steal Demonic Ascension the moment it is available. Now every extended fight is a mirror drain race and your W burst-heal plus the stolen drain tilts the math your way. Force the all-in on the next E-down window and do not let him reset to base and buy Liandry's for free.","late":"Liandry's Torment on a completed Swain is your hardest wall — his burn damage stacks through your W heal and Demonic Ascension outlasts your burst. Respect that spike, farm under tower if needed, and wait for him to misuse his E or overextend for a CS. At 2+ items your Riftmaker sustain and the stolen R let you win drawn-out skirmishes, but the lane is no longer a free kill — play for teamfight angles, steal the best R available in fights, and convert your pre-Liandry's kill lead into objective control before the game goes to a pure drain-tank slugfest.","whys":["Neither kit has a level-1 all-in — farm with Q chain and track how Swain positions his E before you commit a single step-up.","Nevermove roots on the return, then he pulls you in for Vision of Empire and Death's Hand — step up for CS only once you have seen the wave come home.","Full kit online does not change the fundamental rule: E2 hook is only safe after his E pull has been burned; extended trades here still favor his range and sustain.","His E cooldown is your trade window — hook in on its downtime, land Q-W with passive autos, and walk out before Demonic Ascension can drag out the fight.","Hijack reaches 950 and Nevermove only 850 — take his ultimate from outside his root, then run the mutual drain race your Kingslayer heal wins.","Liandry's Torment completes before your Riftmaker and his burn damage stacks through your W heal — respect this spike and avoid extended trades until your item catches up.","Riftmaker and a stolen Demonic Ascension give you sustained brawling power, but the lane is split-even by items — play for teamfight steals rather than trying to re-open a solo kill window."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"tahmkench","win":["Skill","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit online — E2 hook into Q slow into W heal lets you win extended trades that Tahm Kench's grey-health passive cannot recover from in time."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Devour — a few seconds with him eaten and silent, but only after three of your autos have stacked An Acquired Taste."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks during sustained trades; paired with W's missing-health heal, you out-sustain Tahm Kench's grey-health recovery in every long fight."},{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Abyssal Dive lets him vanish under you and re-emerge with a knockup — don't all-in when it is up or you eat the full CC chain into a grey-health regen window."}],"wants":{"you":["Force extended trades with E2 hook into Q slow into W so your healing outraces his grey-health passive regeneration","Land three autos to stack An Acquired Taste before you spend a hijacked Devour — the stacks are yours to build, not his","Track his An Acquired Taste stacks, not a cooldown — Devour (R) has none, it simply needs three stacks and 100 mana on you"],"foe":["Bank grey health on Thick Skin (E) and heal off every Tongue Lash that lands on you, then convert it to a shield","Save Abyssal Dive (W) to leave the moment you Abduct in, wasting the 400-range chain you spent","Devour your knock-up or chain CC so your W lands after you are already dead, not before"]},"early":"Level 1 you only have Q — use it to poke him off CS and build chip damage, but do not walk into tongue-lash (Q) range trying to auto him; he wins the straight level-1 slugfest on grey-health alone. At level 2 your W comes up and even trades start tilting your way slightly, but he can absorb a short rotation and walk away with greyed HP that fills back passively. Level 3 is where you take control: E forward to close distance, fire E2 chain to hook and stun him, land Q's crossing explosion during the stun, then W while he is slowed — the W's missing-health bonus and your chain autos strip more HP than his grey-health can recover before the next wave. Respect only one thing early: his Q tongue-lash. It stuns for a full second, and if he lands it on you mid-combo your W heal is interrupted and he turns the trade around.","mid":"At level 6 the lane changes completely in your favour — Hijack gives you Abyssal Voyage, and suddenly the champion who was supposed to roam the map is you, not him. Use the stolen R to contest Dragon or a mid-lane skirmish while he farms helplessly without his roam tool. In straight all-ins, keep baiting his Abyssal Dive before you commit your E2 hook: if he burns the dive to dodge your chain stun, the fight is already won — you heal through his follow-up damage and he has no escape. Never engage when Abyssal Dive is up and he has Devour available; he can eat you during the CC window to peel your damage entirely, then spit you out stunned with a refreshed fight.","late":"Late game you are the teamfight carry and he is a peeling support tank — fundamentally different roles. Your stolen Abyssal Voyage lets you reposition across fights or deliver a threat from an unexpected angle; his R without the ability just makes him a slow drain-tank waiting to Devour someone. You out-sustain him in long fights with Riftmaker omnivamp layered on W's heal, so do not try to burst him — let the fight go long and let him bleed. The danger is he protects a hypercarry ADC by Devouring them away from your burst; target his backline before he can react, or force him to Devour a tank-irrelevant target and then clean up the ADC while the cooldown runs.","whys":["Level 1 is Chain Lash against Tongue Lash — neither side can all-in, and Thick Skin banks the chip you deal as grey health he heals back out of combat; farm safe for level 3.","W comes online and short trades now favour you marginally, but his grey-health regenerates between rotations — commit only to full kit combos, not partial skirmishes.","Full kit at level 3 means E2 hook into Q slow into W heal; the combo strips more HP than grey-health can restore before the next rotation, giving you a real lead.","He has no hard all-in button at this range — trade with full rotations, track his Devour cooldown, and deny his Q tongue-lash by keeping E1's dash shield ready.","Hijack copies Devour, but you do not inherit An Acquired Taste — you must land three of your own autos on him first, so it is a finisher, not an opener.","Riftmaker omnivamp plus W's missing-health heal means sustained fights are almost unwinnable for him — force every 1v1 to go long and let your sustain do the work.","At full build both of you have strong teamfight presence, but your ability to steal any enemy R each fight and his inability to stop you makes you the higher-agency carry."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"teemo","win":["Teemo","Teemo","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Teemo's Blinding Dart (Q) shuts off your passive-empowered autos and your Q before you have E — stand behind minions and do not walk up."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your E comes online: once Blinding Dart is on cooldown, E2-hook onto Teemo and dump the full Q-W-passive rotation before he can kite back."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack copies Teemo's Noxious Trap — use the stolen R to zone chokepoints, then E2 straight to him and W for the kill; he has no dash to escape."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker or Shadowflame means your W heals for hundreds of HP mid-trade; Teemo cannot sustain through your burst and has no escape once the chain lands."}],"wants":{"you":["Strike him once to break Move Quick's passive speed, then Abduct in while Blinding Dart's flat 7s is running","Get into melee range and force a W-into-passive trade where his E poison cannot out-damage your healing","Shove the wave and force him to path into your E range without jungle cover"],"foe":["Land Blinding Dart on every trade to negate your passive auto burst and Q chain damage","Sprint Move Quick (W) as you Abscond — the active speed holds even after you strike him","Stack shrooms on every chokepoint and jungle entrance so you cannot safely roam or chase"]},"early":"Teemo owns the first two levels outright — his Blinding Dart lands before you have E, and his Move Quick passive means chasing on foot is suicide. Walk up only to last-hit with Q tip, absorb the poke behind minions, and do not burn E1 to close distance yet; he wants you to waste it. The moment you hit level 2 and E loads, count his Blinding Dart cooldown — it is roughly 7 seconds — and the first time it misses or is used on a minion, that is your window to E2 hook and chain into a W, but only if he cannot simply run out of range immediately.","mid":"Level 3 flips the lane. Full kit means E2-hook lands a stun, your W heals off his missing health, and your Q slow prevents the kite. Track his Blinding Dart like a second health bar: the blind lasts 1.5 seconds and has a 7-second cooldown, so there is always a five-second gap where your passive autos deal full damage. Force the trade in that window — E2 in, Q to slow, weave passive autos, W the moment you are low enough for the missing-health bonus. At level 6 you steal Noxious Trap; use the shrooms immediately on jungle chokepoints to deny his escape paths, then chain in freely because Teemo has no dash whatsoever once cornered.","late":"You win the 1v1 all game once your first item is complete — Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through every passive auto and W heal, and Teemo simply cannot match your sustain in melee range. The danger shifts to the map: his shrooms cover objective paths, river entrances, and your own jungle, so never facecheck brush near Dragon or Baron without sweeping first. Roam aggressively after kills because he has no way to follow and punish your absence except by proxy through a split-push his kit is not designed for — shove the wave, use Teleport or MS to collapse mid, and let his shroom vision advantage become irrelevant at the objectives you are already taking.","whys":["Teemo's Blinding Dart is available at level 1 and you cannot yet chain him; standing in poke range without E to punish the cooldown just loses HP for free.","He still has Blinding Dart and you have only one E charge with no follow-up combo ready — the hook is killable but the kill is not clean enough to commit.","Abduct pulls you onto him, Chain Lash slows the kite and Kingslayer heals — Blinding Dart is a flat 7s at every rank, so the gap between casts is always the same and known.","Your sustained trading through passive autos and W heal is too much for his E poison to match across multiple engages — force trades every time his Blinding Dart is down.","Stolen Noxious Trap zones his escape routes and your Hijack burst on top of W means he dies in one chain from 70% HP; he has no dash to deny the hook.","Riftmaker omnivamp through Q, passive autos, and W out-heals his poison damage; the sustain advantage is now decisive and he cannot disengage once you hook.","You still win the straight duel comfortably, but his Noxious Trap coverage on objectives is the real threat — sweep chokepoints and close the game before his map control compounds."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"trundle","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your E2 hook into W comes online — chain onto him at the wave, W for the heal, and force him to burn Pillar just to escape."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Subjugate turns Trundle's drain into your drain — the armor and MR he loses come straight onto you, and he cannot all-in the bruiser he just fed."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Trundle's Q bites stack stolen AD and his W move speed lets him chase — respect the extended fight and short-trade until you hit 6."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through every Q-Passive-W rotation and pushes your sustain edge past anything Trundle buys first."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2 when Pillar is on cooldown so he cannot wall you off mid-fight","Bring Grievous Wounds — King's Tribute heals him off every minion that dies near him, all lane long","Steal Subjugate at 6 and fight him while his resistances are drained into you"],"foe":["Bite with Q every trade to stack stolen AD and make your passive autos weaker against him","Drop Pillar between you to split the fight and let his Q AD advantage win the short exchange","Fight on Frozen Domain, where every King's Tribute minion heal and his own sustain are amplified"]},"early":"Trundle's Q bite steals a flat chunk of your attack damage every time it lands, which directly weakens your Petricite Burst passive autos — the longer the level 1 trade goes, the worse your autos hit. Farm with Q at safe range and do not auto-trade freely into his bite. At level 2 your E comes online and the dynamic shifts: hook him mid-wave, W to heal any bite damage, and get out. He has no engage of his own to answer a full chain rotation, and his Pillar is his only out — remember its location and stay on the same side so he can't use it to escape cleanly.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are a genuine tug-of-war. Your full kit with W sustain means you win the short burst trade every time, but Trundle's Q stacks snowball his AA damage, and his W move-speed zone makes disengaging painful once he starts chasing. Short-trade and reset: land E2-Q-W-passive autos, then back off before his bite count gets dangerous. Save E2 for disengages when his W zone is active — burning it to engage into his move-speed bubble means you're tanking bites with no escape. At level 6 the entire matchup flips: hit Hijack immediately, cast Subjugate on him, and then fight. You gain his armor and MR as you drain it from him — he becomes the squishy, you become the tank, and your W heals for enormous amounts off his suddenly-lower HP.","late":"Post-6 this matchup is yours. Every time Subjugate is available you should be looking to pick a fight — the resist drain is so punishing that even a Trundle ahead on kills cannot sustain through a Hijacked Subjugate plus Riftmaker healing. In teamfights, cast the stolen Subjugate on your own frontline tank to give them Trundle's drain, then brawl into the enemy with your stacks. Trundle without his R in a 5v5 is a glorified auto-attacker; Sylas with his R in a 5v5 has a point-and-click tank-shredder. Keep splitting if he splits, but contest every objective — his R cooldown is your timer, and you win every fight it is down.","whys":["Chomp is a 3.5s auto-reset at 300 range that saps your attack damage — the wind-up is the tell, so step back on it and last-hit from Chain Lash range.","Your E2 hook comes online and he has no point-and-click CC to interrupt it — one chain into W rotation outpaces his Q bite at this health pool.","Full kit access plus W healing lets you sustain through bite stacks in short trades; rotate spells and disengage before his Q AD gets too high.","His Q has now stolen meaningful AD and his W chase speed makes extended fights risky — short-trade, reset mana, and wait for level 6 rather than brawling.","Stealing Subjugate is the matchup-defining moment: you drain his armor and MR onto yourself, and his own kit cannot answer the bruiser he just buffed.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacking through Q-passive-W rotations outpaces his first-item sustain and keeps you healthy enough to keep forcing fights he cannot win.","Trundle has no scaling win condition that outpaces a Sylas with Subjugate available every 80 seconds — you own the late side of every objective fight."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"tryndamere","win":["Tryndamere","Tryndamere","Skill","Tryndamere","Sylas","Skill","Tryndamere"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Tryndamere's Q passive fury stacks and early crit autos outpace your single-spell poke before E comes online."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals Undying Rage — you now hold his panic button and can commit to the all-in knowing he cannot simply refuse to die."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Tryndamere's crit chance ramps with fury stacks and his E gap-close punishes any over-extended Q attempt before you have Hijack."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Phantom Dancer or Kraken Slayer Tryndamere's sustained crit DPS and free lifesteal outlast your W healing in extended side-lane duels."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook in with E2, burst with Q-W-passive chain while his Undying Rage is confirmed down or already stolen","Steal Undying Rage with Hijack and force the all-in immediately — holding the ult denies him his entire win condition","Buy Grievous Wounds — Bloodlust is a 12s heal and it is the only thing keeping him in the lane"],"foe":["Bank Fury for crit chance, then Spinning Slash in at 650 and force the extended trade while it is full","Burn your E1 dash with a fakeout spin, then E again to close and crit while you have no escape","Activate Undying Rage the instant your W connects so your missing-HP bonus heals into thin air and you cannot finish him"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q or W and no hook — Tryndamere's fury-stacked crits already beat you in a straight auto trade, so stay at Q-chain range and poke without stepping into E-spin distance. Level 2 is marginally better once W comes online, but his passive fury charges off your very poke, meaning a full-fury Tryndamere is more dangerous every time you go in. Respect that his E has a shorter cooldown than your full E1-E2 cycle; if he burns it to engage, that is your window to W-back and reset, not to commit.","mid":"Level 3 onward you can threaten the hook-into-Q-W pattern, but Tryndamere's crit DPS in an extended trade still edges you out — land E2 only when his E is on cooldown and never let the fight drag past one full ability rotation before using your own E1 to create distance. The real turning point is level 6: the moment Hijack is available, check his fury bar and bait the spin before locking R. Once you hold Undying Rage, you can all-in freely — commit with E2-Q-W-passive autos and activate the stolen R the instant he drops you low, flipping the kill condition entirely onto him.","late":"Post-level 6 Sylas holds a meaningful window as long as Hijack is available, but Tryndamere's item scaling — Phantom Dancer, Kraken Slayer, lifesteal — means dueling him without his R stolen is increasingly punishing. Force side-lane skirmishes only when Undying Rage is confirmed on cooldown; if it is up, disengage with E1 and wait rather than trading into a 5-second death timer. In teamfights, the stolen Undying Rage is most valuable as a last-second survival tool for yourself, not a damage cooldown — pop it to survive burst, then chain your W onto the nearest target to sustain back up.","whys":["Battle Fury (P) builds crit chance off every auto he throws, and you have no gap-close yet — poke from Chain Lash's 775 and refuse the auto trade entirely.","His E gap-close has a shorter cooldown than your full E1-E2 sequence; he can re-engage after baiting your dash, so W trades only are safer than committing your hook.","Full kit access lets you threaten E2-Q-W, but his crit DPS in a prolonged fight still edges your sustain — short burst rotations only, then disengage.","Bloodlust raises his attack damage the more wounded he is, so your chip makes him hit harder; poke for the lead but wait for him to spend Fury on the heal.","Hijack flips the lane — you hold Undying Rage and can commit the all-in. Force the fight the moment you steal it; every second you hold it is pressure.","Riftmaker's healing amplification stacks with W's missing-HP scaling, making this your best extended-trade window, but only if his R is confirmed stolen or down.","Kraken Slayer percent-HP shred and lifesteal outpace your W healing in side-lane duels — avoid 1v1s unless Undying Rage is on cooldown and you have Hijack ready."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"urgot","win":["Skill","Urgot","Urgot","Urgot","Sylas","Urgot","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Echoing Flames (P) blasts fire off his autos and off Purge, chunking you through your Q approach — his leg damage outpaces your poke before you have the W heal online."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Fear Beyond Death gives you a fear + execute threat; use it to force Urgot off the wave or convert a kill on a low-HP target — your only clean solo power spike."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's Void Corruption stacks during extended trades and partially offsets his W passive damage, giving you a slim window to contest the 1v1 if E lands cleanly."},{"when":"Late","text":"Echoing Flames leg blasts, a rank-5 Purge that is free and always up, and E into the R execute hard-counter your brawl at full build — avoid isolated 1v1s, steal his R instead."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 after he burns Disdain (E) so he has no disengage from your W brawl","Force short trades with Q slow into one W cast and reset — never let shotgun knees tick you to execute range","Steal Fear Beyond Death at 6 and use the execute threat to zone him off objectives rather than hard all-inning solo"],"foe":["Walk forward and let Echoing Flames (P) leg blasts grind your HP down before he throws you with Disdain (E)","Proc Purge (W) at close range and activate Disdain (E) the moment you try to W heal through the trade","Stack enough HP that your W heal is irrelevant and Fear Beyond Death (R) can one-shot you through the execute threshold"]},"early":"Level 1 is a straight loss — you only have Q and Urgot's Echoing Flames (P) adds a leg blast to his autos, so walking up to last-hit bleeds you faster than Q can poke him. Play behind the wave, last-hit with Q at its 775 range, and do not contest the cannon. At level 2 your E comes online, but Disdain shields him as he charges and hurls you into point-blank Purge — do not hook in unless his E is down, and that is a 16-second wait at rank 1. Stack waves and accept that he owns this early window.","mid":"The lane opens at 6 when you steal Fear Beyond Death, but a stolen execute is not a win button — it only finishes him under the threshold, and at that HP your W heal will not outlast his Purge damage long enough to impale him first. Use the stolen R as a deterrent: the threat of a Sylas holding the execute forces him to respect you and step off the wave. Look for the pattern where Disdain is down — 16 seconds at rank 1 — hook in with E2, W immediately for the heal, proc your chains, and disengage before Echoing Flames ramps. Never let it become a prolonged brawl.","late":"At two items Urgot's sustained damage trivialises your W sustain in an isolated 1v1 — Riftmaker stacks cannot keep pace with Echoing Flames blasts through a full Purge channel, and rank-5 Purge costs him nothing to re-cast. Your role shifts to teamfight flanker: steal Fear Beyond Death off its 70-second cooldown every fight, use E2 to hook carries, and let your team's burst do the work his HP stack can't tank. If he Disdains you in a 1v1, you lose.","whys":["Urgot's Echoing Flames (P) adds leg blasts to his level-1 autos — you have no W heal yet and every step toward the wave costs more HP than Q poke returns.","Disdain (E) shields him as he charges and hurls you into point-blank Purge; at 16s rank 1 he holds it for your step-up — he dictates every early trade.","Full kit Urgot still out-damages your sustained pattern; his W active plus passive autos into a flip combo beats your E2-W-Q rotation before you can reset.","He funnels HP leads through the shotgun-knee grind: each trade you survive at 60% HP is a setup for his level 6 execute — deny CS rather than trade.","Hijacking Fear Beyond Death flips the threat dynamic: now he must fear the execute, and you can force him off the wave or bait reckless plays into your team.","Riftmaker Void Corruption partially offsets his percent-HP damage in short trades — contest only with E2 landed cleanly and his E on cooldown, reset immediately after W.","At full build his HP stack and Echoing Flames leg blasts outpace your healing entirely; your value is stealing his 70-second R in teamfights, not winning the solo duel."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"vayne","win":["Vayne","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Vayne"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — one hook into W all-in can take half her HP before she lands a Condemn."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack turns Vayne's own Final Hour against her: the invis and AD steroid now belong to you while she has nothing."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's stacking damage plus W healing lets you absorb Silver Bolts stacks and still win the extended trade."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Vayne's percent-max-HP true damage from Silver Bolts overtakes your W sustain — she shreds you regardless of AP."}],"wants":{"you":["Land E2 hook from an angle she can't wall-Condemn, then chain W into passive autos before she Tumble-kites away","Steal Final Hour at 6 and use its invis to chase her through her own Tumble repositioning","Force all-ins before 2 items when your W heal outpaces Silver Bolts true damage stacking"],"foe":["Land Condemn into a wall while you are mid-E to interrupt the hook and deny the all-in entirely","Tumble sideways on your E hook approach to dodge the chain and reset distance","Stack Silver Bolts with three autos and let true damage grind you down through any amount of W healing"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q or W but no gap-close — Vayne will auto-harass you off the wave freely and you cannot punish her. Accept the poke, last-hit at Q range, and don't burn mana. The moment E unlocks at level 2 the dynamic flips: approach from an angle that has no wall behind you so Condemn just knocks you back rather than stunning, then hook into W to burst her before she can Tumble away. She has no sustain to match your W heal in a proper extended trade, so if you get on her you win the short skirmish cleanly.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are your bully window. Full kit means every E hook is a real kill threat: chain Q-slow into E2, proc passive autos in the landing animation, then W for the heal. Keep tracking her Condemn — 20 seconds at rank 1, the only thing that saves her — and commit your E only when you have seen it spent or she is away from terrain. Her burst opens on a Tumble auto-reset, so step off on the wind-up rather than the cast, and save E2 for the moment she stops moving. Shove the wave, deny plates, and all-in the moment she missteps.","late":"At 6 steal Final Hour immediately — the AD and the Tumble invisibility are devastating on an AP bruiser who already sticks to her, and hers is a 100-second cooldown she cannot re-buy. Force the kill here; this is your cleanest window of the game. Into first item the W sustain still outpaces Silver Bolts provided you keep fights short enough that she cannot land three hits on you repeatedly. At 2+ items respect her hard — Silver Bolts true damage ignores your healing entirely, and her attack-speed kite means you can no longer run her down safely. At that point play for teamfight angles, flank to hook her into your team, and let someone else absorb the Bolts stacks while you burst her from the side.","whys":["Vayne free-autos you at level 1 and you have no gap-close to answer — last-hit at Q range and bank your health for the level 2 spike.","E unlocks your all-in pattern: hook from a wall-safe angle, chain W into passive autos, and her no-sustain kit cannot keep up in the short trade.","Full kit with W sustain means every trade goes your way if you dodge Condemn — she has no healing to contest yours in an extended skirmish.","She cannot Tumble away faster than your Q slow into passive autos, and Condemn is 20s at rank 1 — bank small HP leads for the level 6 all-in.","Hijack Final Hour and use her own invis steroid to chase through every Tumble repositioning — this is your cleanest kill window of the lane.","Riftmaker stacking plus W heal lets you absorb Silver Bolts autos and come out ahead in sustained trades — force the all-in before she finishes second item.","Silver Bolts true damage at full build ignores every point of AP healing — she out-scales you hard, so play for teamfight flanks and deny her free stacks."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"vladimir","win":["Skill","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Vladimir"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit online — E2 chain into Q slow into W burst-heal is your first real kill window; if he hasn't pooled yet you can burn him in one rotation."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijacking Vladimir's Hemoplague hands you the exact teamfight nuke he planned to use — look for the first all-in the moment your R is up."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through sustained W trades, letting you claw back poke deficits and turn extended fights into near-even exchanges."},{"when":"Late","text":"Vladimir's Rabadon spike makes him unkillable in extended fights — his Q heals over your damage output and his pool resets every trade he loses."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Sanguine Pool with E1 dash, then E2 chain into Q-W-AA the moment he resurfaces","Track his pool cooldown and all-in exactly when it expires — that is the only window that reliably sticks","Steal Hemoplague at level 6 and force the fight before he builds a second AP item"],"foe":["Keep you at max-Q range with E poke so your E hook never connects cleanly","Sanguine Pool every E2 commit to eat your burst window and reset the trade","Stall until two items where his Q healing outpaces your W and you can no longer kill him alone"]},"early":"This lane is decided by one cooldown: Sanguine Pool, 28 seconds at rank 1. Level 1 you have Q only — chip his step-ups and avoid extended auto-trades, since Transfusion (Q) costs him nothing and heals back anything short of a full combo. Level 2 W comes online and turns your autos into a sustain engine, but he has no pool yet so don't over-extend. Level 3 is your first real moment — E1 eats his Tides of Blood on the shield, Q slow lands first, then E2 pulls you onto him for the W burst before he can react. If he pools it, back off and wait the full 28 out.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a spacing war. He keeps himself outside E2 range and pokes with Transfusion to force small HP leads, but Tides of Blood is blocked by units — hold a minion between you and his poke pattern disappears. Level 6 flips the equation: your R copies Hemoplague, the nuke he was saving for a teamfight, and a full Q-E2-W-R rotation kills him outright if pool is down. Force the pool with a fake step-up, then re-engage the instant he reappears — his own R is a flat 120 seconds, so every Hijack is a Hemoplague he cannot use in the next river fight.","late":"Two items is where Vlad reclaims the lane. His Q heals through your W output, his pool resets every all-in you attempt, and Rabadon amplification means one Hemoplague trades your entire health bar. Your job is to have converted early Hijack windows into actual kills or objective advantages before this point — if the lane is still even at 25 minutes with two items on both sides, respect his scaling and play for flank angles in teamfights where you can steal the R from a safe distance rather than 1v1ing him.","whys":["Only Q available — chip poke at max range and deny his Transfusion last-hits; no combo yet so never step into his E poke for free.","W online means extended trades now favour you if he has no pool — probe with E1 shield to absorb his counter, but don't blow E2 into an open pool cooldown.","Full kit: E2 pulls you onto him but does not stun, so land Q slow first, then W burst; your shield absorbs the Transfusion counter-hit and the trade is net positive.","Pool is your only meaningful cooldown to track; weave passive autos on his step-ups and bank small HP leads — they cash out the moment Hijack arrives.","Hemoplague stolen on the first all-in flips the damage math; the burst-heal from W at low HP health plus the nuke damage kills him before pool saves him.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacks through W chains and keeps you healthy enough to threaten a second engage even if the first trades even.","His Q-heal at two items outpaces your W unless you are carrying Grievous Wounds, and pool eats every E2 commit — play for stolen-R flanks in teamfights, not solo kills."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"volibear","win":["Volibear","Volibear","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Volibear's Q Thundering Smash comes online at 2 alongside W — his run-down stun into double-bite combo can burst you before your E hook is even available."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack Stormbringer and flip the tower-dive threat: now you can disable his tower and dive him, using his own win condition against him."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full kit on both sides — your E2 hook into Q slow and W heal makes extended trades roughly even; neither of you can cleanly disengage once the brawl starts."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through your repeated W casts and passive chain autos, making sustained all-ins tilt in your favor against Volibear's HP-stacking."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 Abduct when Thundering Smash is on cooldown so he cannot run you down before W lands","Steal Stormbringer at 6 — his own R is 160 seconds at rank 1, so your copy is a free extra dive","Hold Grievous Wounds before the extended brawl — Frenzied Maul heals him on every second cast into you"],"foe":["Land Thundering Smash Q stun before your E hook to lock you in place and open the double W bite pattern","Dive you under tower with Stormbringer R and deny your backing windows while the tower is disabled","Stack HP and build Sundered Sky so his W heal and crit-proc outlast your mana-gated brawling pattern"]},"early":"Level 1 is purely his — Volibear's passive lightning ramps through short trades and you have no answer yet, so last-hit with Q Chain Lash at max range and decline every auto. At 2 his Thundering Smash run-down stun pairs with Frenzied Maul, a 5-second cooldown that heals him on every second cast into the same target, and you cannot escape that burst before your E is live. His Q is an auto-reset, so back off on the wind-up rather than the cast. Reach level 2 fast — E Abscond online is the benchmark that makes this lane playable.","mid":"Once you have all three basic abilities the lane evens out sharply: E2 Abduct into Q slow and W heal matches his stun-bite pattern, and passive Petricite Burst chain autos chip him faster than he expects from an AP bruiser. The key read is Thundering Smash — 12 seconds, and while it is down he cannot hold you still for the double bite, so that is your only hook window. Expect Sky Splitter as his answer: it shields him if he stands in his own blast, so bait it before you spend E2. Level 6 is the turning point — Hijack Stormbringer, pocket the tower-disable, and every all-in either kills him or threatens a dive he cannot reciprocate while you hold his R.","late":"Both of you become teamfight playmakers, but Stormbringer in your hands is a bigger swing than Stormbringer in his — you can steal it every time it is off cooldown while Volibear contributes nothing special without it. In the side lane he eventually outscales into a tanky brawler you cannot one-shot, so avoid pure 1v1s once he finishes Sundered Sky plus a tank item; instead prioritize objective fights where your stolen R disables the enemy tower, your W heals off multiple targets, and his HP-stacking bulk becomes your W damage fodder rather than your problem.","whys":["Volibear's passive lightning ramps through every trade and you have no CC yet — Q at max range and do not step up until you hit level 2.","His Q Thundering Smash run-down stun into double W bite can burst you before your E Abduct is available — respect every walk-up and race to level 2.","Full kits on both sides: E2 hook into Q and W heal matches his stun-bite, and Petricite Burst chain autos keep the damage even across a long brawl.","Sky Splitter slows you from 1200 range and shields him inside its blast — trade only when his Q is down, since the stun lockout is why his combo beats yours.","Hijack Stormbringer and the lane flips: you hold the tower-disable and every Volibear all-in just hands you a better version of his own win condition.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacking through repeated W casts and chain autos makes sustained brawls tilt your way — the longer the fight, the more his HP pool feeds your heal.","He becomes a tank brawler you cannot burst cleanly once Sundered Sky and a second HP item are up — contest objectives with stolen Stormbringer rather than split-pushing against him."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"warwick","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online — punish the 8-second gap after his Q lunge and W into him before Blood Hunt (W) closes the distance."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack his R Infinite Duress: you now own the suppress that makes him terrifying, turning his all-in win condition against him."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks during extended trades — your W heal plus item sustain outraces his passive drain in every prolonged fight."},{"when":"Late","text":"Warwick's R becomes less decisive once you have it too, but his Blood Hunt speed lets him chase down your squishy allies — peel and track him on the map."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E2 on his 8-second Q cooldown, before he can lunge and heal off you again","Steal his R Infinite Duress and use the suppress on him or a priority carry before he can","Carry Grievous Wounds — Eternal Hunger triples his healing under 25% and undoes your burst"],"foe":["Run you down with Blood Hunt W move speed when you are below half HP and Q into a drain trade","Use Infinite Duress to suppress you before you can fire E and negate your combo entirely","Force a prolonged fight at level 1 where you have no E to escape his nose-dive Q"]},"early":"At level 1 Warwick's Q is his whole game — he lunges, bites for a share of your max health, heals off it, and resets the aggression. You have no E yet, so respect the range: last-hit with Q and never let him nose-dive you at low HP, since Eternal Hunger heals him harder the lower he drops. The moment you hit level 2 everything changes. E closes on his 8-second Q cooldown and pulls you onto him for the W burst before he can lunge again. Run that pattern every trade and he cannot sustain through it.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are your bully window. Blood Hunt (W) makes him faster onto anyone under half HP, so keep your own bar above that when you disengage — but in an all-in you want him low first, with chain Q procs and passive autos, then W. At 6 this swings hard your way: steal Infinite Duress and all-in, because his own is 110 seconds and yours is not. Expect Primal Howl — 2.5 seconds of damage reduction ending in a fear — so hold W until that howl is spent, then E2 in and cash the stolen R.","late":"You win the 1v1 convincingly all game as long as you keep his stolen R ready for the engagement. In teamfights the stolen Infinite Duress is a priority-target lock that can single-handedly decide a fight — save it for their hypercarry or fed carry, not Warwick. Respect his Blood Hunt in the open river; if a teammate is below half HP he will run them down. Track his position on the map and peel when needed, but do not give him free roams — your side-lane pressure in this matchup is real.","whys":["Warwick's level 1 Q is a nose-dive heal — without E you cannot punish the approach, so farm safely at Q range and deny him a free drain trade.","E online at level 2 lets you hook mid-Q animation, cancelling his heal and chaining straight into W burst — trade pattern set for the whole lane.","Full kit access and W sustain let you win every extended fight; chain Passive autos reset between spells and out-damage his drain at equal HP.","Blood Hunt speeds him onto you below 50% HP — bait his approach above that threshold, then hook and burst before the speed advantage matters.","Stolen Infinite Duress at 6 removes his entire win condition: he cannot suppress you and you can suppress him, making every all-in a clean Sylas win.","Riftmaker omnivamp on top of W healing means he cannot outtrade you in the sustained brawls his kit is built around.","He has no hard scaling win condition but Blood Hunt lets him punish overextended allies; you stay relevant as long as you hold stolen R for key teamfight moments."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"wukong","win":["Wukong","Skill","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Wukong's Q armor-shred and raw AD pressure wins the very first exchange — you have no healing yet and no hook to punish his all-in."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you Cyclone: spin into him with his own knockup and chain W for a burst-heal all-in that reverses any early deficit."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's AP and omnivamp let your W heal eclipse Wukong's Black Cleaver armor-stacking — the longer the fight, the more you win."},{"when":"Clone bait","text":"Wukong's W decoy can eat your E2 hook at any point in the lane — always confirm the real monkey before spending your only gap-closer."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook the real Wukong (not the W clone) with E2, then burst-heal through his Q armor-shred with W before he can disengage","Stack passive Petricite autos in every extended trade — your AP magic damage ignores all the armor Black Cleaver stacks","Steal Cyclone at 6 and use the knockup spin to pin him for a full E-Q-W-passive combo he cannot W-decoy out of mid-spin"],"foe":["Pop W clone to eat your E2 hook, then burst you while your gap-closer is on cooldown and your heal is not yet active","Open with a Crushing Blow auto-reset so the shred lands before you react, then compound autos on your lower effective HP","All-in pre-6 with Nimbus Strike into Cyclone while you lack a stolen ultimate to match his knockup threat"]},"early":"Level 1 is his — Wukong's Q shreds your armor and he can auto-trade into you before your W heal is relevant. Stand at the back of the wave, last-hit with Q tips, and do not walk into melee range or burn your E on instinct. His Level 1 pattern is a short Q-auto burst and back out; absorb it with minion aggro and keep your HP above the threshold where his Level 2 all-in becomes lethal. The moment you hit Level 2 and W comes online, short trades become your friend: E1 dash in, W for the burst-heal, passive auto, and E1 shield to eat his counter-swing before you back off. Do not throw E2 at him unless you are certain it is not hitting a clone — he will drop W the instant you cast and your only engagement tool goes on cooldown while he bursts you freely.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are a genuine skill check on clone identification. Wukong wants to drop W at the last possible moment to bait your hook, then Nimbus Strike in and auto-chain while your E is down. Counter-pattern: watch for the decoy (it runs in a straight line and never uses abilities) before committing E2, then hook the real one and chain Q slow into W for the heal burst. At 6 the lane flips in your favour — Hijack gives you Cyclone, which is a free knockup spin that also procs your passive autos. Spin into him, W mid-spin, and the combo deals burst-heal damage his armor stack does not mitigate because it is all magic. He cannot W-decoy out of Cyclone once the spin is active, so Level 6 all-ins are cleaner than anything pre-6.","late":"Post-first-item Riftmaker, your AP omnivamp and the healing from W during prolonged fights outpace whatever Black Cleaver gives him — the longer any 1v1 goes, the more the math favors you. In teamfights, Cyclone is a strong engage tool but only moderately powerful compared to what you can steal from other champions on the enemy roster; if Wukong is not the scariest R on the map, prioritize stealing the better ultimate and use your Q slow to peel or pick. The late-game split-push threat is real — Wukong with two tank items and Black Cleaver is tanky enough that he can 1v1 most side-lane opponents, so do not let him free-farm a side wave without checking his position on the minimap first.","whys":["Wukong's Q armor-shred makes Level 1 auto-trades punishing — stand back, farm Q tips, and concede the first exchange rather than feeding him a shutdown lead.","W online means your burst-heal now punishes his short trades; take controlled exchanges but respect his clone juke before committing E2.","Full kit with clone awareness is the key variable — confirm the real Wukong before hooking, then chain Q slow into W heal to win the extended fight.","Both of you have full kits and item rushes in sight; neither side has a decisive edge, so convert small HP leads into wave control rather than forcing all-ins.","Hijack gives you Cyclone — spin into him immediately, W mid-spin for the burst-heal, and your magic damage bypasses every armor item he built.","Riftmaker omnivamp and AP amplification let your W sustain outpace his Black Cleaver trades; force the long fights and deny his short-burst pattern.","Two items in, the lane is behind you — roam to steal better ultimates on the enemy roster and let your AP sustain handle any 1v1 Wukong tries in side lanes."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"yasuo","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Yasuo"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online and you can chain onto Yasuo mid-dash — his habit of stepping forward to stack Steel Tempest is a free E2 landing."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack grants you Last Breath — but it only lands on an Airborne target, so it is a follow-up to his tornado, never an opener."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through your full E2-Q-W-passive chain, making every extended trade unwinnable for a crit-reliant pre-IE Yasuo."},{"when":"Late","text":"At full build Yasuo's crit burst and Conqueror stacking outpace your W heal — avoid clean 1v1s and use your stolen Last Breath as a teamfight playmaker instead."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Wind Wall with Q poke before committing your E2 hook so it cannot swallow the chain","Dodge his two-stack Steel Tempest tornado with the E1 dash, then counter with E2 into W — the chain pulls, it never stuns","Steal Last Breath but hold it — it only casts onto an Airborne target and your own kit has no knockup"],"foe":["Stack Steel Tempest Q to tornado without walking into your E range, forcing you to E forward into his CC","Place Wind Wall to swallow your E2 chain in flight the moment you commit to a trade","Reach two Crit items so his burst can one-rotate through your W heal before it can top you off"]},"early":"At level 1 you have only Q and nothing to hook him with — farm with the tips of your chain and do not chase his level-1 Steel Tempest stacking. The moment you hit level 2 and E unlocks, the dynamic flips: Yasuo loves to step forward to stack Q, and that forward step is exactly the range for your E2. E1 forward to close distance, absorb whatever he throws, then E2 pulls you onto him and W lands in the same beat. Wind Wall cannot eat a chain that already connected, but it swallows one in flight — throw E2 from a side angle, never through the wall. By level 3 your passive autos weave between spells and Q slow into W heal sustains you through any short trade.","mid":"The key discipline from levels 4-5 is Wind Wall tracking — 25 seconds at rank 1, and Yasuo places it reactively the moment he sees you step up. Bait it, watch it go up, count the 25 out, then commit your full E2-Q-W rotation while he has nothing to swallow the chain. At 6 you gain his Last Breath, but read the condition: it blinks only to an Airborne champion, and nothing in your own kit knocks up. Hold it as a follow-up to his own tornado or your jungler's CC and it is a second fight-winner in every river skirmish; press it on an unairborne target and it simply will not cast.","late":"Two full crit items change the math — Yasuo's burst rotation can delete enough HP faster than W heals it back, so do not look for clean solo duels in the side lane at 25+ minutes. Your role shifts to teamfight flex: hold Hijack for the fight, steal Last Breath the instant he uses it, and redirect the knockup setup onto his own carries. You still beat him in a chaos brawl where your W can heal across multiple targets; you lose the clean 1v1 duel he wants in an open lane.","whys":["Yasuo stacks Steel Tempest at level 1 by stepping forward — farm at Q range and don't gift him a free tornado by standing in dash range without your E up.","E unlocks and his habit of walking up to stack Q becomes a free E2 target — the chain pulls you onto him, so land W in the same beat and win the first trade.","Full kit with Petricite Burst autos lets you sustain through his trade pattern; Q slow into W heal into passive auto chain beats his short Steel Tempest cycle at this level.","Wind Wall has a real cooldown — bait it with a Q, count the timer, then commit your E2 hook in the window where he cannot swallow the chain with a wall.","Hijack gives you Last Breath, though it only fires onto an Airborne champion — hold it as follow-up on his own tornado or your jungler's CC, and note he has nothing to steal back from you.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacking through your full rotation beats a pre-IE Yasuo who cannot yet burst through the heal; force extended trades and let the item do the work.","Full crit build lets Yasuo's burst rotation outpace W's healing in a clean duel — play the teamfight angle, use stolen Last Breath as an engage tool, and avoid the open 1v1 he wants."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"yone","win":["Yone","Skill","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Yone's Q dash lets him poke and reposition safely before you have E — he can harass your last-hits and back off before you can hook him."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack steals Fate Sealed — a blink behind the line that pulls everyone hit toward you — turning his kill setup into your engage."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through your W heal and passive autos, letting you sustain through his E-in/E-out poke pattern and win extended brawls."},{"when":"Late","text":"Yone's two-item spike (Kraken Slayer + Navori) lets him shred your W healing with percent-health damage and recharge his E to disengage any all-in you start."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E2 the moment Soul Unbound is down — he cannot rewind back to safety","Trade in extended fights where W heal offsets his Q damage and passive autos stack up","Steal Fate Sealed at 6 and force the all-in while his own copy sits on 120 seconds"],"foe":["Use Q dash to poke your HP down then E out before you can land the hook","Activate Soul Unbound into a trade then rewind out, keeping the damage but escaping your chain","Scale into two items where Kraken Slayer's percent-health damage bypasses your W sustain"]},"early":"You have no real answer to his level 1 Q dash poke — he dashes in, auto-Qs, and backs off faster than your Q slow can punish, and his Q is on 4 seconds. Stand near the minion wave to tighten his dash angles and last-hit with Q from distance. At level 2 your E comes online, but Soul Unbound is a 22-second escape at rank 1 and he can trade then rewind before you hook — commit the E2 chain only when his E is down or he has overextended past the minion line.","mid":"Levels 4 through 5 are the steadiest stretch: your W heal absorbs his Q trades and passive chain autos chunk him back, but Soul Unbound — 22 seconds at rank 1, down to 10 at max — remains his get-out card, and Spirit Cleave shields him every 14 seconds on top of it. Count both before you commit: bait the E by stepping up hard, then hook once he has spent it. At 6 the matchup swings, because Fate Sealed is an exceptional Hijack — a blink behind the line that drags everyone hit back to you — and every all-in becomes yours to dictate.","late":"In a split-push both of you are mobile enough to 1v1 at any point, but his Kraken Slayer percent-health damage starts eating through your W heal by two items, and Navori lets him recharge Soul Unbound faster so he can disengage the moment the brawl turns against him. Win the game through teamfight rotations where stolen Fate Sealed lines up their backline rather than chasing a kite-happy Yone in a side lane — your 1v1 edge narrows significantly once he has his core build.","whys":["Yone's Q dash poke is free at level 1 — you have no E to respond, so stand behind the wave and chip with Q to deny his angle rather than trading.","Soul Unbound lets him dash in for a trade then rewind out; your E is online but commit it only when his E is down or you will hook an empty space.","Spirit Cleave shields him every 14 seconds and Soul Unbound is his exit — bait the E with a step-up, then chain him before the rewind returns him.","Weave passive autos into every Q or W touch; the healing edge compounds over repeated skirmishes and builds a meaningful HP lead before level 6.","Hijack Fate Sealed immediately — it blinks behind the last champion in a line and drags everyone hit back to you, and his own copy is 120 seconds.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacks with W heal through his poke pattern; you win extended brawls if you absorb his burst and retaliate with the full chain.","Kraken Slayer percent-health damage begins to undercut your W sustain at two items — prioritize teamfights where stolen Fate Sealed can flip the whole engagement rather than extended side-lane 1v1s."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"yorick","win":["Skill","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Sylas","Yorick"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"E comes online and you can hook-into-W all-in before Yorick's ghouls are a factor — punish him before he builds a grave."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Eulogy of the Isles gives you the Maiden and a ghoul swarm that fights for you — this is your hardest kill window in the entire game."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through ghoul chip damage and keeps your HP topped, letting you trade through his E slow and W cage safely."},{"when":"Late","text":"A fully-stacked Yorick with Maiden and two items outpushes and outduels you — respect the splitpush and rotate rather than fighting 1v1."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook him with E Abduct before he lands Mourning Mist so the slow never sticks on you","Never trade inside Dark Procession — caged, the mist shreds your armour and the swarm gets free hits","Steal Eulogy of the Isles at 6 and immediately all-in while he has no Maiden to tank for him"],"foe":["Land Mourning Mist first — it shreds armour and marks you, so the swarm speeds up","Trap you inside Dark Procession W and let the Maiden and ghouls shred your HP","Survive until two items and let the Maiden push independently while he duels you"]},"early":"At level 1 you only have Q or W and Yorick's Shepherd of Souls passive lets him raise Mist Walkers off graves — don't step into swarm range or you take free chip damage with no reliable trade back. Farm from max Q range and accept that he shoves early. The moment you hit level 2 and E is available, look for a hook the instant Mourning Mist is on cooldown — 12 seconds at rank 1 — because if you Abduct before the mist marks you, W Kingslayer fires before any ghoul reaches you.","mid":"Levels 3 through 5 are your safest sustained-trade window. Kingslayer heals you off Yorick himself, and his damage comes through the swarm — extended fights favour you because every W into him refunds what the ghouls chip. The one thing that beats you is his cage: walled inside Dark Procession you eat a Mourning Mist that shreds your armour and marks you, and marked targets pull the swarm in faster. Watch the cast and dash clear with E1 before the wall closes — it is 20 seconds at rank 1. Last Rites is an auto-reset, so read the wind-up, not the swing. At 6, steal Eulogy of the Isles and all-in while he has no Maiden buffer.","late":"Yorick's two-item spike with Sundered Sky or Titanic and a full ghoul kit means the Maiden pushes T2 towers alone while he duels you — that 1v1 you won at level 6 becomes much harder when he has grievous wounds and percent-health Maiden damage stacking. Don't take a free duel past 25 minutes; instead, use your mid-game lead to drag him into team fights where the Maiden is across the map and his W cage is a smaller threat. Your stolen R is still valuable in those fights — a Sylas-summoned Maiden in a teamfight does real work.","whys":["Yorick spawns a ghoul off any minion kill at level 1 — stepping in to trade hands him free chip damage before you can Q or W back. Stay at Q cast range and last-hit cleanly.","E Abduct comes online and you can hook him the instant Mourning Mist E is on cooldown — one hook leads into W Kingslayer for a short trade that hurts him more than the ghoul chip hurts you.","Full kit with chain-Passive autos and W sustain lets you bully his melee pattern; his ghouls deal chip, your W heals it back in any trade that goes more than two seconds.","Kingslayer heals off Yorick himself and E1 dashes clear of the cage — force trades whenever Dark Procession is down, and that is 20 seconds at rank 1.","Stealing Eulogy of the Isles removes his primary dueling tool and gives you the Maiden and ghouls to fight for you — this is the biggest tempo swing of the entire game; all-in immediately.","Riftmaker omnivamp stacks through every ghoul auto and Maiden hit, meaning his sustained-damage style refills your HP faster than it depletes it — keep forcing fights.","Full-item Yorick with Maiden's independent push and percent-health damage outduels you in a straight 1v1 — rotate to teamfights where his cage is less threatening and you can still use his stolen R."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"zac","win":["Sylas","Sylas","Skill","Skill","Sylas","Zac","Zac"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"W comes online at 2 and your full kit at 3 — E2 chain into Q slow into W burst-heal beats every trade pattern Zac has at this point."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack gives you a copy of Let's Bounce — you now own the teamfight engage he planned to use, and the 1v1 all-in math swings hard in your favor."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Riftmaker's omnivamp stacks through the W heal and passive chain-autos, making every extended 1v1 a win so long as you don't start it while Elastic Slingshot is mid-charge."},{"when":"Zac Lvl 6","text":"Let's Bounce gives Zac a hard disengage bounce — the moment he hits 6 he can survive an all-in he would have died to at 5, so don't over-commit without Hijack online."}],"wants":{"you":["Hook with E2 chain while Elastic Slingshot is on cooldown, then Q-W-AA through his counter-trade with E1 shield absorbing the damage","Steal Let's Bounce at 6 and look for the flank or river skirmish where a Zac ult in your hands is a kill, not an escape","Bank small HP leads from each trade and convert them at 6 before Zac's team comp can leverage his engage in a 5v5"],"foe":["Land Elastic Slingshot to catch you in a clunky position before your E is up, eating your HP before the real fight starts","Reabsorb his blobs through your poke, then bank on the bloblet revive so the kill you counted as lethal simply does not stick","Disengage with Let's Bounce the moment his HP dips, resetting the trade and wasting your W heal timing"]},"early":"Level 1 is quiet — your Q is your only tool and Zac reabsorbs his blobs to heal the chip back, so poke his step-ups with Chain Lash but never chase. The real game starts at level 2: W online means even trades heal you back, and Zac has no escape from a committed E2-into-W burst. By level 3 your full rotation — E1 in, E2 to pull yourself onto him, Q slow so he can't walk, W lunge for the burst-heal — out-damages everything he can throw. Buy Grievous Wounds before you plan a kill: fatal damage splits him into four bloblets and he revives if any survive. Punish the Slingshot's 21-second cooldown rather than stepping into its charge.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a compounding lead: weave passive chain-autos between every spell cast, trade freely whenever his Elastic Slingshot is down, and bank the small HP margins because they stack into a kill at 6. The moment Hijack comes online you steal Let's Bounce — that copy transforms every 2v2 river fight into your engagement rather than his, and the 1v1 math is now lopsided enough to force a decisive play. Look for the side-lane all-in the instant Zac steps forward, and don't let him reset on a tower bounce.","late":"Two items of Riftmaker into Shadowflame and a stolen Let's Bounce makes you a teamfight flex threat, not just a duelist. Zac's value in the late game is his engage for his team — if you've stolen his R in a side fight he's just a blob with CC and no delivery mechanism. Keep using Hijack on cooldown in fights: even if his ult is weaker by late, denying his team that engage bounce is a win condition in itself. Don't get caught alone in the river without E up — his Q into W zone can still lock you down if your dash is burned.","whys":["Q Chain Lash is your only level-1 tool and Zac reabsorbs blobs to heal the chip back — poke his step-ups but never burn E chasing.","W Kingslayer online at 2 turns even trades into healing wins; Zac has no dash yet, so the hook-into-W burst already chunks him meaningfully.","Full kit: E2 chain into Q slow into W burst-heal is a true rotation; E1 shield eats his counter-swing and passive autos pad the damage.","Elastic Slingshot is 21 seconds at rank 1 and that downtime is your aggression cue — but Unstable Matter still pulses percent max HP every 5s up close.","Hijack at 6 hands you a copy of Let's Bounce — you own the engagement tool he built his whole kit around, and the all-in math becomes overwhelming.","Riftmaker spike lets the W heal compound through omnivamp and passive chain-autos; any extended 1v1 that starts even ends in your favor.","Two items plus a stolen Let's Bounce makes you a teamfight initiator; denying Zac his engage bounce is itself a win condition regardless of the duel."]},
  {"a":"sylas","b":"ziggs","win":["Ziggs","Ziggs","Ziggs","Skill","Sylas","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Ziggs's Q (Bouncing Bomb) out-ranges your entire kit — he shoves wave and pokes for free before your E hook is relevant."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stealing Mega Inferno Bomb flips the script: a 5000-range nuke on your own AP — Hijack copies his R, it does not disarm him."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Once you have E maxed and know his W cooldown, a patient hook past the minefield can land your full combo before he repositions."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ziggs with Luden's or Shadowflame dramatically increases his one-shot threat on your hook approach — respect the burst and hook from unexpected angles."}],"wants":{"you":["Land E hook PAST his E minefield to pull yourself in without triggering the slow","Bait Ziggs's W knockback BEFORE committing your E so it cannot interrupt your chain pull","Steal Mega Inferno Bomb at 6 and use it immediately — it forces him back under tower or to burn flash"],"foe":["Lay E (Hexplosive Minefield) between himself and you to slow your E hook approach and zone you off minions","Use W (Satchel Charge) reactively the moment your E first-tap dash appears to knock you away before your chain pull lands","Shove waves with Q poke under your tower repeatedly and deny CS from range where your W heal cannot kick in"]},"early":"At level 1 you have Q or W and no reliable way to close the 850 range of Ziggs's Bouncing Bomb — accept the poke, farm with Q at maximum range, and do not walk past the wave into his bombs. Level 2 changes nothing, because Satchel Charge knocks you away the instant your chain is in the air; hold your E until you see him place it or it goes on cooldown, and at rank 1 that is a 20-second wait. Level 3 is your first real window: if he spends it on the wave or misplaces it, dash in with E-first-tap, fire the chain past his minefield hitbox, and chain into W-Kingslayer before he can reposition. Even then he will likely burn flash — take the trade, it is a win.","mid":"Levels 4-5 shift the equation as your E cooldown shortens and you learn his repositioning habits. He will almost always pre-place Hexplosive Minefield in the expected hook path — train him by faking dashes at minion clusters, then fire the real hook from an angle the minefield does not cover. If you hit the pull you are on top of him with no CC of your own, so the follow-up has to be instant: passive chain autos plus W-Kingslayer burst him in one rotation. Level 6 is your defining moment: steal Mega Inferno Bomb and use it immediately on him or toward his tower. Hijack copies his R rather than taking it, but his own is 120 seconds at rank 1, so your copy is genuinely extra pressure.","late":"With both of you at first or second item the lane evens out — your W heal and stolen R give you a credible all-in threat, but Ziggs's burst and poke range mean a missed hook in a corridor or an E minefield you walk into can still cost you the fight. In teamfights you are fishing for the same hook pattern: E into the backline past his minefield, chain pull, W-burst, and your stolen bomb is now a global execution tool. If the lane went even, respect that his siege damage dismantles towers faster than you, so collapse on him in skirmishes rather than letting him split-push objectives for free.","whys":["Ziggs Q out-ranges your entire kit at level 1 — he pokes for free while you have no dash and cannot retaliate without walking into three bomb hitboxes.","Satchel Charge knocks you away the moment your chain is in the air, and he holds it reactively — at 20 seconds rank 1 he still wins every level-2 skirmish you start.","Full kit access means you can bait his W placement and then hook past his E minefield, but his range advantage still forces you to outplay him just to trade evenly.","His W is on a long cooldown after one use and your E is shorter now — wait for the W whiff, then hook in; the fight goes to skill on who reads the other's cooldowns first.","Stealing Mega Inferno Bomb gives you his siege and teamfight tool on your own AP — Hijack copies it rather than taking it, but at 120 seconds his is the slower clock.","Shadowflame or Luden's spike means he can nearly one-shot you mid-hook if you are poke-damaged; your Riftmaker sustain keeps you in the fight but you must approach with more HP than you think you need.","Both of you have meaningful teamfight presence in late skirmishes — you on the hook-dive pattern, him on global poke and minefield denial — and the 52% win rate reflects that neither side cleanly dominates."]}
